import{_ as o,P as s,d as r,e as c,f as n,H as l,q as i,y as a}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Part 12 Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Part 12 Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice","xkChars":0,"xkWords":6356,"xkLessons":{"intro-12-part":{"title":"Part XII: Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice","lead":"The previous part mapped possible paths; this part asks which future judgments will submit to tests at resolution, and under which values and protective conditions actions may be compared and recommended. These questions connect, but prediction confers no power to make others bear costs.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2602–2605; original anchor: part_12; short quotation: “keeps the two apart, each with its own conditions”. See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Conditional foresight undertakes fallible future judgments within targets, deadlines, and evidence boundaries. Fallibility requires explicit support, non-support, or unresolved conditions at resolution; not every path may count as success, and deadlines cannot extend indefinitely. Sections 12.2–12.7 specify registration, local predictability, baselines, expression, evaluation, and what forecast failure means.","Bounded choice compares what can be done, how, when to stop, and when not to act within explicit value, protection, and authorization boundaries. Section 12.8 onward connects foresight to options, distributional consequences, reversibility, and procedures for actual adoption. Inaction also requires comparison and cannot be presumed costless.","Together they form a working loop: predictions provide conditional consequences for choices; actions and responses may change future material; outcomes then help correct judgments. Keeping each with its own conditions prevents cross-level substitution: a likely consequence does not mean it ought to be produced, or that anyone may compel others to bear costs to avoid it.","A comparative recommendation may itself be a complete analytical deliverable. With explicit evidence, normative premises, costs, and limits, grounded preferences, rejection, or delay recommendations are possible; lack of execution authority need not reduce the output to questions alone. Actual adoption and execution separately require applicable protection, review, and valid authorization. “Only advice” also cannot conceal foreseeable high-impact uses.","This part promises neither universal predictability nor a model that unifies every value. It permits bounded answers, unknowns, incomparability, multiple options, and current nonaction, requiring explicit material that could change the judgment."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Conditional foresight","leftText":"Undertakes a fallible judgment about a future target.","rightTitle":"Bounded choice","rightText":"Compares options under explicit norms and limits."},{"leftTitle":"Recommendation for deliberation","leftText":"It can be an explicit analytical conclusion.","rightTitle":"Actual adoption","rightText":"Must independently pass the applicable protection, review, and authorization requirements."}],"boundaries":["A prediction may be accurate while a choice remains unjustified; a qualified choice procedure does not guarantee successful execution.","The introduction guides task boundaries; it provides no theorem of empirical gain."],"questions":["Without authority to execute, must we only list questions and refrain from recommendations?","Why are foresight and choice adjacent yet kept apart, each with its own conditions?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/figures/intro-12-part.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"intro-12-part","start_line":2650,"end_line":2653,"startLine":2650,"endLine":2653}],"outputId":"intro-12-part","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Turn a judgment about the future into a forecast registration with a target, deadline, and evaluation obligations.","Separately disclose the normative, protective, distributional, and permission conditions for comparing options.","Allow complete recommendations for deliberation, without turning predictions or recommendations into execution permission.","Append actual outcomes as feedback records while retaining the original registration and failure records."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: the same prediction is insufficient to determine an action","text":"A fictional task predicts that a queueing arrangement will reduce average waiting by 2 minutes, but it is unknown whether it makes one person unable to exit wait an extra hour. This average foresight can enter a comparison but cannot determine that the arrangement should be executed. Distribution, minimum protections, alternatives, and permissions must be explicit. Even if the completed analysis favors it, the real queue has not thereby been scheduled.","assumptions":"The numbers, objects, and consequences are teaching stipulations; no actual measurements or population trials were conducted.","boundary":"This does not provide operational advice to actual service organizations."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model predicts overall improvement, so it can directly order everyone to adopt the option.","effect":"Factual consequences do not automatically generate value premises, rights-based justification, or execution permission.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Without authority to execute, must we only list questions and refrain from recommendations?","answer":"No. A recommendation for deliberation can be completed within what the evidence and explicit norms permit; its execution status is recorded separately.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why are foresight and choice adjacent yet kept apart, each with its own conditions?","answer":"To connect outcomes to action comparisons and feedback records while preventing prediction from being substituted for authority.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/figures/intro-12-part.png","alt":"Conditional foresight: target/deadline/baseline, open to calibration and failure. Bounded-choice analysis: norms/options/distribution, preferred option and opposing case. Real-world adoption and execution: protection/review/authorization, stopping and remedies. Parallel obligations do not automatically upgrade status; work can connect, but qualifications must be checked separately.","caption":"Parallel obligations do not automatically upgrade status; work can connect, but qualifications must be checked separately."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. The introductory diagram guides three types of obligation; it does not mean that projection can automatically authorize action.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The part introduction specifies divisions of work and boundaries, rather than an independent empirical case claim. Concrete historical material appears in the relevant numbered units."},"openQuestions":[],"taskId":"S-12-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 2446–2462; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-12-part: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts","12.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"norm","title":"Historical research draft: the source text's division between normative choice and real-world adoption","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §1.4 lines 107–128; §1.7 lines 147–152; §4.14.1 lines 908–925; §14.6 lines 2940–2953","claim_support":["Distinctions among explanation, normative recommendation, and interference; the protection floor; the limited scope of C12 and atomic J authorization"],"limits":["These are procedural provisions of the user's theory; they do not substitute for external legal effect or actual authorization.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report; the page gives no separate publication date","locator":"§3.3 table, DPE(km), T+24=57; see §1 Introduction for the measurement convention","claim_support":["2024 North Atlantic mean direct positional error at T+24 hours: 57 km"],"limits":["Aggregated by the operational agency; individual cases were not independently recalculated. It does not represent a current forecast or grant authority for real-world action."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"第十二部分　条件前瞻与有限选择; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"80de49a0a0534739c58d14402e7190680d8ae29b5e6dc8e94fc9f04ea2088777","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.1":{"title":"12.1 Separating four types of output","lead":"“I think this will happen, so that should be done” may quietly cross several gates. This section requires identifying whether we are explaining the past, unfolding conditions, predicting the future, or proposing a normative choice: each answers to different questions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2606–2618; source anchor section-12-1; short quotation: “Explanation, projection, conditional foresight, and bounded choice are four kinds of output and must be labeled separately.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","Explanation answers what happened and why it may have happened, requiring objects, scales, evidence, mechanisms, and counterexamples. Its upper bound is a bounded mechanistic account. A coherent account of the past does not prove that the future will unfold the same way. An explanation may also retain several mechanisms; unknowns cannot be narrated as a unique cause.","Projection starts from a fixed snapshot, update rules, clocks, and branches to provide conditional paths and distinguishing signals. It can say, “if working-time and resource conditions remain, this branch may unfold.” Listing multiple paths does not give each a probability, and selecting the realized branch after the fact cannot establish predictive success for the entire projection.","Conditional foresight adds registration before the outcome, a simple baseline, target indicators, and feedback-record obligations. It asks which path is better supported as an expectation within a specified deadline. Its expression may be an evidence-based probability, ranking, or direction, or remain unknown when evidence is insufficient. Being open to calibration and failure is not the same as having demonstrated excellent calibration; the two cannot be merged into a single “reliable” label.","Bounded-choice analysis requires normative premises, evidence, genuinely comparable options, prices to be paid, and affected parties. Real-world adoption separately requires explicit normative premises (§14.3), the protection floor (§14.8), itemized authorization, the four-step operational procedure (§14.6), and bridging qualifications under the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” (§4.14.1). This follows the source text's division of work; it is not a model inventing public authority. Even when these premises remain unmet, explicit analysis for deliberation is possible, but it must not claim to be ready for execution.","Connecting successive gates does not automatically upgrade status: explanation generates projection candidates; projection generates foresight candidates; foresight supplies consequences for comparison; norms and procedures support bounded choice; actual execution still requires stopping, restoration, and feedback records. Any layer with sufficient material can provide a clear bounded answer. Answering an explanatory question does not require completing every action procedure.","A single deliverable can contain all four output types, but their status must be labeled by sentence or paragraph, with sources and support ceilings stated separately. For example, discovering a processing-capacity decline after the fact is explanatory material; assuming continued low speed tomorrow is a scenario; registering the probability that tomorrow's 09:00 backlog exceeds a threshold is foresight; recommending maintenance first is normative analysis. They can share material without sharing all qualifications.","Accuracy is performance relative to a target and evaluation arrangement, not a moral, legal, or representative qualification. A weather model's smaller positional error does not authorize its publisher to determine how anyone evacuates. Real-world protective action has its own actors, permissions, and procedures. This non-entailment does not deny weather prediction's usefulness; it prevents usefulness from expanding into unlimited power."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Explanation","leftText":"Provides a bounded mechanistic account of phenomena that have occurred.","rightTitle":"Projection","rightText":"Unfolds possible paths from conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Projection candidate","leftText":"Lists consequences if assumptions hold.","rightTitle":"Registered foresight","rightText":"Set down before the outcome and subject to evaluation at the deadline."},{"leftTitle":"Evaluable","leftText":"A target and scoring arrangement are in place.","rightTitle":"Good performance has been verified","rightText":"Requires actual outcomes and appropriate review."},{"leftTitle":"Foresight ranking","leftText":"An evidential judgment that a consequence is more reasonably expected.","rightTitle":"Recommendation ranking","rightText":"Also requires norms, distribution, costs, and protection."},{"leftTitle":"Bounded choice passes","leftText":"The relevant conditions are supported.","rightTitle":"Execution is certain to succeed","rightText":"Real-world deviations still require stopping and feedback records."}],"boundaries":["The four output types are not a value hierarchy that must be climbed sequentially; an explanatory task can stop at explanation.","Lacking permission does not preclude bounded analysis; calling something “only advice” does not exempt high-impact uses from responsibility.","The number of scenarios, graphical complexity, and the model's tone do not generate probabilities.","Current predictive performance does not substitute for normative justification or the validity of external procedures."],"questions":["Does registering foresight mean excellent predictive performance has been proved?","If A has probability 0.8 and is harmful, can we directly say “choose A”?","Can all four output types coexist in one report?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/figures/section-12-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"2024 North Atlantic cyclone forecasts: the boundary of an evaluation metric","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"2024 North Atlantic cyclone forecasts: the boundary of an evaluation metric","text":"The North Atlantic table in the UK Met Office's 2024 Northern Hemisphere cyclone verification report lists a T+24-hour mean direct positional error of 57 km. This specific evaluation metric is evidence about predictive performance. The lesson uses it to show that the metric itself contains no authorization to direct particular people's actions.","sourceLabel":"Met Office, 2024 Northern Hemisphere verification report","sourceUrl":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","sourceLocator":"§3.3 North Atlantic basin storms, Table of mean error statistics, DPE(km) row, T+24 column","boundary":"This historical operational statistic supports only the corresponding region, year, lead time, and sample convention. It is not a probability of personal harm, a current weather forecast, or an evacuation decision. The claim that a metric does not entail authorization is this lesson's explanation of a logical boundary.","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.1","start_line":2654,"end_line":2666,"startLine":2654,"endLine":2666}],"outputId":"section-12-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["State separately each output type's question, inputs, and supportable conclusions.","Check what additional evidential or normative conditions are needed to move from one layer to the next.","Evaluate foresight only against its target and deadline set down in advance; do not count all scenario branches as successes.","Analytical recommendations state norms and distribution; real-world adoption separately checks normative premises, the protection floor, itemized authorization, operational procedures, and the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging”.","Retain responsibility for failure, stopping, and remedies after execution; a qualified procedure does not guarantee success."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: assigning four statements to their proper places","text":"Fictional records show 2 arrivals yesterday, 1 completion, and a starting value of 6. Under the assumption of no other flows, the endpoint is 7: a conditional explanation of records of past events. If the same conditions hold tomorrow, the endpoint will increase by another 1: a projection. Conditional foresight requires setting down, before the outcome, the target “whether it exceeds 7 tomorrow at 09:00,” the information cutoff, baseline, scoring, and signals, and giving an evidence-based judgment. If the stated norms are safeguarding deadlines and respecting personnel's minimum protections, and a recommendation follows comparison of maintenance, delay, transfer, and inaction, this is bounded-choice analysis. It still does not mean maintenance has been authorized or completed.","assumptions":"The entire example is a teaching structure and supplies no real-world probability; yesterday's records are also constructed stipulations.","boundary":"Correct arithmetic does not prove an actual mechanism. Without actual registration and outcomes, no foresight gain can be claimed."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: likelihood and preference can rank in opposite directions","text":"Fictional events A and B have probabilities 0.8 and 0.2, stipulated by the example. The value premise considers A seriously harmful and B harmless. A's greater likelihood does not mean it should be promoted; it may instead be something against which protection is warranted. If a response harms third parties, rights and distribution require separate comparison; 0.8 alone cannot determine coercive measures.","assumptions":"The probabilities and values are purely constructed; no real-world event risk was estimated.","boundary":"No specific action follows, nor is expected benefit treated as the only norm."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Mechanism A holds in explaining the past, so A must occur next time.","effect":"Applicability conditions and future inputs may change; the responsibilities of forecast registration must be taken on separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"One of ten scenarios occurred, so all ten count as successful predictions.","effect":"Exploring multiple branches does not equal ten successes; evaluation must follow the target and expression specified beforehand.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model predicts accurately, so it is qualified to decide others' destinies.","effect":"Performance does not generate norms, representativeness, jurisdiction, or authorization.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does registering foresight mean excellent predictive performance has been proved?","answer":"No. Registration only states an arrangement open to failure; performance still requires actual outcomes, baselines, and review.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If A has probability 0.8 and is harmful, can we directly say “choose A”?","answer":"No. Probability describes likelihood; choice separately requires norms and comparison of feasible options.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can all four output types coexist in one report?","answer":"Yes, but their statuses, evidence, and ceilings must be labeled separately; no hidden upgrade within a sentence is allowed.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/figures/section-12-1.png","alt":"Explanation: what happened/why, mechanisms and counterexamples. Projection: what happens if conditions hold, paths and distinguishing signals. Conditional foresight: target/deadline/baseline, open to failure at the deadline. Bounded-choice analysis: explicit norms and options, prices to be paid and affected parties. Real-world adoption is checked separately: normative premises/protection floor/itemized authorization/operational procedures/normative bridging; prediction does not create authority. Execution can still deviate: stopping/restoration/remedies, with outcomes appended as feedback records. Each card specifies output qualifications, not an automatic upgrade ladder; actual action must have independently valid grounds.","caption":"Each card specifies output qualifications, not an automatic upgrade ladder; actual action must have independently valid grounds."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Separate cards avoid a visually direct route from “explanation” to “command”; a proposed interaction would display gaps by output type.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"A primary operational evaluation table provides a concrete performance metric, helping explain why performance evidence and normative permissions address different questions."},"openQuestions":["This lesson cites the publisher's aggregates without recalculating individual cyclone forecast errors. It issues no actual weather-protection instructions."],"taskId":"S-12-1","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 2446–2462; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["intro-12-part: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts","12.1: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"norm","title":"Historical research draft: the source text's division between normative choice and real-world adoption","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §1.4 lines 107–128; §1.7 lines 147–152; §4.14.1 lines 908–925; §14.6 lines 2940–2953","claim_support":["Distinctions among explanation, normative recommendation, and interference; the protection floor; the limited scope of C12 and atomic J authorization"],"limits":["These are procedural provisions of the user's theory; they do not substitute for external legal effect or actual authorization.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report; the page gives no separate publication date","locator":"§3.3 table, DPE(km), T+24=57; see §1 Introduction for the measurement convention","claim_support":["2024 North Atlantic mean direct positional error at T+24 hours: 57 km"],"limits":["Aggregated by the operational agency; individual cases were not independently recalculated. It does not represent a current forecast or grant authority for real-world action."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.1　四类输出的隔离; Chinese source lines 2606–2618; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ebc15f133e37f4e94bf2689f6af8a45e7192072981ac0942b00c3ccbf049f411","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.2":{"title":"12.2 Registering conditional foresight","lead":"First specify what counts as the prediction, when it ends, and how correctness is judged, then await the result. Otherwise, “the relationship will improve” can forever mean a slight improvement somewhere, and no model can genuinely fail.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2619–2634; source anchor section-12-2; short quotation: “sets the forecast down after the input cutoff and before the results appear.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","Registration time, input cutoff, and target deadline play three different roles. First close the entry point for material usable on this occasion, then set down the judgment, without having already seen the target outcome. The deadline may be a window; the input cutoff need not equal the target's start. Late evidence can inform a new version but cannot backfill the old projection. If the target outcome is already known, the work is retrospective explanation or backtesting and cannot be renamed foresight registration.","A registration that can be identified on its own and the model version ensure traceability. The target, deadline, object, and circle scope determine who and what will be evaluated at the deadline; the baseline time specifies the starting point for measuring change. If the original target covers the whole team, narrowing it after the outcome to high-performing members changes the original registration and cannot retain the original success status.","The target must be adjudicable, specifying at least the observable quantity, observer or source, threshold, and treatment of missingness. For example, if “relationship improvement” uses a particular scale, it evaluates only change under those measurement conditions, not whether the whole relationship or everyone's life improved. First check whether the target definition actually corresponds to the task; do not replace the outcome the user cares about merely to make scoring easier.","A simple baseline prevents complex stories from declaring their own victory. The candidate mechanisms and path set specify mutual exclusivity, parallelism, or incomparability. Probabilities, intervals, grades, or directions must match the strength of their grounds. Without grounds for ranking, state unknown; mandatory registration does not justify invented numbers. Fill in decision thresholds only when used for decisions; otherwise they may be marked not applicable with a reason.","Early and contrary signals provide material for distinguishing paths. Suspension conditions specify when to stop using a model or variable; retirement conditions specify when to withdraw it. Calibration and outcome-feedback plans specify the actual labels, timing of appended records, and evaluation rules. A single prediction's score is not long-term calibration; the plan must also specify consistent conventions and uncertainty handling across accumulated tasks.","The original table's seven minimum questions address vague success, endless waiting, complexity as self-proof, retrospective branch selection, false precision, seeking support alone, and disappearing failures. A filled table is not a certificate of immunity to failure. Honest unknowns in its items may also mean the entry is not yet qualified for release as foresight.","Subsection 12.2.1 bounds local capability claims by object, scale, resources, and expression; 12.2.2 requires independent evaluation of each recursive order. The parent section governs the registration entry. One overall prediction registration cannot combine results from different orders, populations, or cutoffs as a single item."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Input cutoff","leftText":"The latest acquisition boundary for material allowed in this projection.","rightTitle":"Outcome deadline","rightText":"The time point or window at which the target is adjudicated."},{"leftTitle":"Baseline time","leftText":"The starting time for calculating change.","rightTitle":"Simple baseline model","rightText":"A comparison judgment without complex structure."},{"leftTitle":"Decision threshold","leftText":"A trigger criterion for reviewing a specified action.","rightTitle":"Probability or classification expression","rightText":"Does not automatically become execution permission and need not involve a decision."},{"leftTitle":"Complete registration","leftText":"Traceability and conditions for failure are explicit.","rightTitle":"Predictive capability established","rightText":"Still requires outcomes, baselines, and appropriate evaluation."}],"boundaries":["A successful subgroup narrowed after the outcome must not count as a hit on the original target.","A window may be an interval, but it cannot be extended indefinitely after the fact.","Changes to target conventions require a new version; unresolved must not be entered as did not occur."],"questions":["Can a new signal obtained at 09:00 be backfilled into the 08:00 prediction?","Why is Ptoy01's target probability 0.6?","If the inventory count is missing, may its label be recorded as 0?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.2","start_line":2667,"end_line":2682,"startLine":2667,"endLine":2682}],"outputId":"section-12-2","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Before the outcome, set down the target, deadline, scope, input cutoff, registration time, and version.","Define adjudicable rules for occurred/did not occur/unresolved to prevent changing populations and thresholds after the outcome.","Fix the simple baseline, mechanistic paths, expression form, and applicable decision thresholds.","Specify positive and contrary signals, suspension, retirement, calibration, and append-only outcome feedback in advance.","When material is insufficient, retain the gaps preventing publication; do not substitute empty forms or fabricated parameters."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: one complete foresight registration","text":"Registration Ptoy01, model v1; object=fictional repair queue Q, circles=no circle grouping used for this local task; baseline 08:00 B0=6; input cutoff 08:00, registration 08:01; target is recorded B≥8 at 10:00, horizon ending at that day's 10:00 count, labels verified at 10:05, with missing counts unresolved. Stipulated generating mechanism: arrivals 2/hour for two hours, processing rate c constant throughout, c=1 with probability 0.6 and c=3 with probability 0.4; endpoints are thus 8 or 4, target probability 0.6. Simple baseline p0=0.5 is an independently predefined teaching comparator. Paths are mutually exclusive; calibration plan accumulates tasks under the same mechanism and records Brier scores and event frequencies in equal-probability groups, without certifying calibration from one case. Decision threshold is inapplicable because only prediction is evaluated. Early signal=09:00 backlog 7 or 5 under the same definition; contrary signal=inconsistent values or changed generating conditions; suspension=changed input definition or object; retirement=long-term failure to outperform the baseline in later tests under the same arrangement, after review. Write-back=append count source, label, loss, and deviations after 10:05; evidence reference=public stipulation Etoy in this example, not a real record.","assumptions":"Probabilities are a stipulated formal generating distribution, not estimates from actual repair data. All times, records, and calibration plans are constructed; repeated tasks were not actually conducted.","boundary":"Using the new 09:00 signal for updating requires a separate registration, newly set down; Ptoy01 remains evaluated on 08:00 information."},{"title":"Teaching construction: operationalizing a vague target must preserve unresolved status","text":"“Team relationships improve” may be operationalized for a particular measurement task as “the same voluntary measurement on a fixed member set at two predefined times, using predefined scoring and missingness rules, with an indicator change reaching a threshold.” If members exit or refuse disclosure, do not default to improvement or deterioration; record missing/unresolved under prior rules and check target identity.","assumptions":"Design principles only, without a real psychological test or personal-data collection. No threshold or scale is supplied, so no values are assigned.","boundary":"One indicator does not represent every relationship, dignity, or life outcome; real adoption requires construct and protection checks."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The original forecast concerned improvement across a department; looking only at an improved subgroup afterward also counts as success.","effect":"Object scope has been switched; create a new target while continuing the old record.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Even without real probability grounds, enter 0.5 to be neutral.","effect":"0.5 is a probability claim, not an automatic product of ignorance.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can a new signal obtained at 09:00 be backfilled into the 08:00 prediction?","answer":"No. Make a new projection with a 09:00 cutoff; evaluate the old prediction under its original cutoff.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is Ptoy01's target probability 0.6?","answer":"Under the explicit artificial generating mechanism, only c=1 yields 8 and meets B≥8, with stipulated probability 0.6; this is not a real estimate.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If the inventory count is missing, may its label be recorded as 0?","answer":"No. Record it as unresolved, as originally registered; missing is not nonoccurrence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2.png","alt":"08:00 input cutoff; 08:01 Ptoy01 registration with stipulated p=0.6; 10:00 target count, label 1 for B≥8 and 0 otherwise, missing label unresolved; after 10:05 append evaluation, preserve the original prediction, and version new signals separately. Entirely teaching registration; times and probabilities are not real operational records.","caption":"Entirely teaching registration; times and probabilities are not real operational records."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns describe conditions and evaluation responsibilities; the static figure connects to no real prediction or outcome system.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section requires complete prior registration. The historical retrospective evaluation report obtained lacks this lesson's full set of registration items set down in advance, so an explicit generating distribution and complete construction are used rather than presenting a retrospective report as prior registration."},"openQuestions":["No real forecast registration or experimental outcomes are provided here; a team-relationship task would additionally need suitable measurement, missingness rules, and protections."],"taskId":"S-12-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2463–2490; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report","locator":"§3.3 table, Possibly verified row: T+24=125, T+168=15","claim_support":["12.2.1: different lead times have different numbers of verifiable forecasts, illustrating temporal and observation boundaries."],"limits":["Not independent storm counts; no case-level material obtained, so no causal effect or current accuracy is estimated."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"metdefinitions","title":"Tropical cyclone forecast verification - measures of error","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/appendix","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page undated; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Glossary entries Possibly verified and Detection rate","claim_support":["Possibly verified counts forecasts with verification observations and is distinct from detection rate."],"limits":["Operational terminology is used only under this report's definitions, not equated with denominators for all classification tasks."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.2　条件前瞻登记; Chinese source lines 2619–2634; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"745ffe78139ca712456fa259c3d8938ea1f362e94421a96b8c0a0c32fd5df696","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.2.1":{"title":"12.2.1 Boundaries of local predictability","lead":"Do not ask only “can this model predict?” Ask which object, which state region, how far ahead, what error is allowed, and with what information and resources. Good performance on one task is not a license for every future.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2635–2640; source anchor section-12-2-1; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “not a ladder of precision every task must climb in sequence.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Local predictability binds at least object, state region, scale and circles, window, tolerance, available information, computation and observation resources, version, baseline, abstention, and failure conditions. A model effective one hour ahead under normal load need not work under extreme load or a year ahead; expanding boundaries needs new evidence.","Predictability of the world as a whole exceeds this framework's directly testable scope. What is permitted here is bounded increment within particular tasks: local success does not establish complete foreknowledge, and long-term unknowns do not negate all short-window questions. Bounded capability may genuinely exist under its corresponding constraints.","Classification, ranking, intervals, and probability have different meanings. Classification identifies categories; ranking gives grounded order; intervals preserve admissible values or explicit coverage conventions; probability concerns event generation or empirical estimation. They are not a universal ladder from low to high. A task may need only a category, and probability need not suit the use better than a correct interval.","Choose expression after checking target, units, generating method, identification conditions, and calibration status. If even direction is unidentified, state unknown rather than “at least judge the trend.” A formal model may have known probabilities, explicitly supplied by assumptions; empirical probabilities need real data and applicable scope, not AI converting sparse prose into parameters.","Borrowing mathematical or physical theory also requires its original object, assumptions, limits or scaling laws, error, effective domain, object mapping, counterexamples, and exit conditions. Calling a community a fluid or information energy does not automatically apply the equations. Metaphors can prompt questions; quantitative laws require real mappings and validation.","Resource limits are part of capability. Backtesting with material available only later does not establish real-time predictability then; computation exceeding the decision window may prevent delivering the claimed lead time. Abstention preserves the ability to withhold judgments outside the applicable domain. Report abstention scope in evaluation rather than showing only easy cases the model answers."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Bounded task increment","leftText":"Outperforms the baseline under explicit boundaries.","rightTitle":"The whole world is predictable","rightText":"Does not follow from local evaluation."},{"leftTitle":"Formally known probability","leftText":"Given by a public model or stipulation.","rightTitle":"Empirical probability","rightText":"Needs real material and calibration; the two must not be switched."},{"leftTitle":"Directional judgment","leftText":"Requires an identifiable sign or ordering.","rightTitle":"Arbitrarily stating a trend without probabilities","rightText":"Also exceeds evidence boundaries."},{"leftTitle":"Similar-looking formulas","leftText":"A metaphor or heuristic lead.","rightTitle":"Valid quantitative mapping","rightText":"Requires objects, units, assumptions, and empirical support."}],"boundaries":["Large samples cannot repair wrong measurement definitions; small samples also do not universally prohibit posteriors under explicit priors and models.","Different lead times may have different evaluable samples; error size alone cannot establish a causal performance change for the same objects.","Abstention must not be hidden to imply effectiveness across the full domain."],"questions":["If Δ may be −2 or +3, why cannot one say it probably rises?","How does error 2 perform on tasks with tolerances 3 and 1?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2-1.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"2024 cyclone verification: lead time comes with different sample boundaries","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"2024 cyclone verification: lead time comes with different sample boundaries","text":"In the North Atlantic table of the UK Met Office report, forecasts available for verification number 125 at T+24 and 15 at T+168. This section emphasizes that longer horizons can change evaluable samples; short-window capability cannot extend unconditionally to longer windows.","sourceLabel":"Met Office, 2024 Northern Hemisphere verification report","sourceUrl":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","sourceLocator":"§3.3 table, Possibly verified row, T+24 and T+168 columns; definitions in the linked measures of error supplementary page","boundary":"These count forecasts with verification observations, not independent storms. Selection and detection conditions across lead times need separate checks; 125 and 15 cannot estimate a causal effect.","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.2.1","start_line":2683,"end_line":2688,"startLine":2683,"endLine":2688}],"outputId":"section-12-2-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Bound predictability claims to specific objects, state regions, windows, tolerances, and resources.","Declare information cutoff, version, baseline, and abstention/failure conditions.","Choose expression by target and evidence; direction is not an evidence-free default.","For borrowed formulas, check original assumptions and object mappings, stating error and exit conditions.","Downgrade or retest out-of-boundary results; do not extrapolate automatically from local to global."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: no probability may also mean no direction","text":"Suppose compatible mechanisms give future change Δ as −2 or +3, without probabilities or evidence selecting a mechanism. Report set {−2,+3}, not “tends upward,” nor average 0.5 as a real prediction. Only new evidence excluding the negative branch permits a positive direction within the remaining models.","assumptions":"The admissible mechanism set is constructed, not claimed to exhaust all real possibilities.","boundary":"A conditional set differs from a confidence interval; no equal branch probabilities are assigned."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: different tolerances produce different task eligibility","text":"A fictional point prediction is 10, actual value 12, absolute error 2. It meets a prior task tolerance ≤3 but fails another task's tolerance ≤1. Success on the former cannot transfer to the latter, nor may tolerance change from 1 to 3 after the outcome.","assumptions":"Numbers and tolerances are teaching stipulations; one case establishes no long-term capability.","boundary":"Tolerance must also suit actual use and harm, not be widened arbitrarily for better scores."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model predicts 24 hours ahead, so the same accuracy applies to a week.","effect":"Window, state domain, and available information change and need separate validation.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"If probability is unsupported, direction can at least always be judged.","effect":"Compatible mechanisms may imply opposite directions; direction can also be unknown.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If Δ may be −2 or +3, why cannot one say it probably rises?","answer":"There are no probabilities or evidence excluding the negative branch; report both directions and the material needed to distinguish them.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How does error 2 perform on tasks with tolerances 3 and 1?","answer":"It meets the former and fails the latter. The tasks are defined differently; thresholds cannot be switched afterward.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2-1.png","alt":"Object and state region: scale/window/tolerance, information and resources; matching expression eligibility: classification/ranking/interval/probability, with direction possibly unknown too; bounded test results: baseline/version/abstention, downgraded beyond boundaries. Types are not a precision ladder; one local result does not cover every future.","caption":"Types are not a precision ladder; one local result does not cover every future."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Three columns describe conditions and evaluation responsibilities; the static figure connects to no real prediction or outcome system.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The same operational table reports different sample sizes by lead time, illustrating local capability's temporal and observation boundaries."},"openQuestions":["Individual forecast records were not obtained; lead-time aggregates are not treated as tests on identical independent samples, nor extrapolated to current model capability."],"taskId":"S-12-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2463–2490; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report","locator":"§3.3 table, Possibly verified row: T+24=125, T+168=15","claim_support":["12.2.1: different lead times have different numbers of verifiable forecasts, illustrating temporal and observation boundaries."],"limits":["Not independent storm counts; no case-level material obtained, so no causal effect or current accuracy is estimated."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"metdefinitions","title":"Tropical cyclone forecast verification - measures of error","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/appendix","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page undated; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Glossary entries Possibly verified and Detection rate","claim_support":["Possibly verified counts forecasts with verification observations and is distinct from detection rate."],"limits":["Operational terminology is used only under this report's definitions, not equated with denominators for all classification tasks."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.2.1　局部可预测性边界; Chinese source lines 2635–2640; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ad280fc0c74a41bc6163cc504059631b7cc25a893da440889f0e1b67e935f444","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.2.2":{"title":"12.2.2 Evaluation of recursive foresight by order","lead":"A correct first order and incorrect third order cannot be blended into an attractive total and called “effective recursion.” Each order has different questions and informational dependencies, requiring each to be set down separately and compared separately with reasonable shallower methods.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2641–2646; source anchor section-12-2-2; brief quotation: “must not be combined into a single overall hit rate.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Register object, target, horizon, input cutoff, expression, and evaluation rules separately for first, second, and third orders. If they predict different targets, adding hits into an opaque total rate is invalid. Any combined metric needs its own prior definition and weights while retaining order-specific performance; it cannot replace the original registrations.","Each order needs three comparators: state continuation without further recursion; direct judgment of the same distant target; and a shallower order plus simple continuation. These distinguish genuine need for reentry structure from mere passage of time. Comparing second-order errors on a difficult distant target directly with an easy first-order short-term target cannot establish recursion as harmful or beneficial.","Fair comparison matches target, horizon, available information, and evaluation rules, with a reasonable account of computation and tuning opportunities. Extra evidence, computation, and richer structure can each generate gain and should be distinguished as far as possible. A simple baseline need not be artificially made as large as the complex system, nor deliberately denied available information.","Without new external material, every child projection's evidence lineage still returns to the same fixed starting point. Postprocessing may reveal previously underived logical consequences, sensitivities, or hidden conditions—a real inferential increment—but does not add a real sample or independent evidence source.","When actual intermediate outcomes arrive, make a new projection with a new cutoff. Compare “projecting all the way from the original start” with “updating using later actual conditions,” but the latter uses more material. Gains cannot all be credited to recursion, nor fed backward into old paths to make them perpetually correct.","If three orders add only stories without stable out-of-sample increment, retain exploratory scenarios without foresight-capability eligibility. One better or worse performance establishes no universal rule. Judge stability within registered use and independent evaluation scope, narrowing windows or retiring when necessary."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Evaluation by order","leftText":"Score each order's own task against reasonable comparators separately.","rightTitle":"Overall hit rate","rightText":"May conceal differences in task difficulty, horizon, and failure."},{"leftTitle":"Internal inferential increment","leftText":"Reveals consequences implied by original inputs.","rightTitle":"External evidential increment","rightText":"New real material appears, with different status."},{"leftTitle":"Prediction updated with actual conditions","leftText":"Reestimated using a new cutoff.","rightTitle":"Original recursive prediction","rightText":"Uses only the old starting point and remains under its old registration."},{"leftTitle":"Direct distant-target comparison","leftText":"Prediction for the same target without reentry.","rightTitle":"First-order short-term score","rightText":"A different task cannot directly rank methods."}],"boundaries":["Do not count regenerated child texts as independent samples.","Out-of-sample evaluation also needs protection against target, time, and source leakage.","Exploratory scenarios may be useful even without supported foresight gain."],"questions":["Third order 14, direct 11, continuation 12, shallow-plus-continuation 10, label 10: what are their errors?","Can improvement from new intermediate actual conditions be attributed entirely to recursion?","Is postprocessing without new observations entirely valueless?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.2.2","start_line":2689,"end_line":2694,"startLine":2689,"endLine":2694}],"outputId":"section-12-2-2","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Set down target, window, information, and scoring arrangement separately for each order.","Compare the same target against state continuation, direct distant judgment, and shallow-order continuation.","Distinguish differences in information, computation, and structure, preserving order-specific scores.","Child projections without new external material do not increase real-evidence counts.","Make a new projection for intermediate actual conditions; without stable gain, downgrade to exploratory scenarios."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: recursion does not beat baselines on the same target","text":"A fictional third-order target is the hour-3 queue value, actual label 10. Predictions are recursive 14, direct distant-target 11, current-state continuation 12, and shallower-plus-continuation 10. Under predefined absolute error, scores are 4,1,2,0. This example does not support recursive superiority; a correct first-order answer to a different short-term task cannot offset the third-order error.","assumptions":"All four predictions, the label, and the rule are teaching constructions, not a real experiment; all comparisons concern the same hour-3 target.","boundary":"One sample cannot establish that every recursive method is worse; it gives only this example's bounded comparison."},{"title":"Teaching construction: new actual conditions cannot leak backward into old orders","text":"Suppose three-order predictions are set down at 08:00 and a new count arrives at 09:00. A new projection from that count may improve the 10:00 prediction, and the updated result can be reported while retaining the 08:00 forecast. Writing the 09:00 value into the old first order, recomputing old second and third orders, and claiming “predicted at 08:00” is information leakage.","assumptions":"Times and data flow are constructed; no real forecasting system was run.","boundary":"An update's value does not make the old projection valueless; evaluate the two information sets separately."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Generating 100 child projections from one starting point equals 100 independent observations.","effect":"The source lineage is shared; more computation adds no real samples.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The first order is accurate and the third inaccurate; averaging proves overall three-order effectiveness.","effect":"Tasks and horizons differ by order; retain separate scores and same-target comparisons.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Third order 14, direct 11, continuation 12, shallow-plus-continuation 10, label 10: what are their errors?","answer":"4,1,2,0. Recursion does not dominate here, without a universal long-term judgment.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can improvement from new intermediate actual conditions be attributed entirely to recursion?","answer":"No. It adds information; use a new version and distinguish informational from structural contributions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is postprocessing without new observations entirely valueless?","answer":"It may add reasoning, sensitivity analysis, and conditional understanding, but real-world evidence does not grow by itself.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/figures/section-12-2-2.png","alt":"Teaching target label 10: third-order prediction 14, absolute error 4; direct long-horizon prediction 11, absolute error 1; state-persistence prediction 12, absolute error 2; shallow-order plus persistence prediction 10, absolute error 0. Register each order separately, without mixing in short-term hits or hiding third-order failure. New observations go into a separate new version; do not backfill the original information set. Reasoning ≠ new observation. Numbers are teaching constructions; this same-target comparison is one example only and certifies no long-term methodological superiority.","caption":"Numbers are teaching constructions; this same-target comparison is one example only and certifies no long-term methodological superiority."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Four method cards sit side by side, without curves fabricating experimental trends; the final two cards flag information boundaries.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section specifies evaluation by order and information lineage. No actual archive of third-order predictions set down in advance and independent labels exists here; reproducible scoring for the same target avoids inventing a recursive-foresight experiment."},"openQuestions":["Stable out-of-sample gains require actual records by order and same-target controls, which this lesson has not implemented."],"taskId":"S-12-2","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2463–2490; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.2.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report","locator":"§3.3 table, Possibly verified row: T+24=125, T+168=15","claim_support":["12.2.1: different lead times have different numbers of verifiable forecasts, illustrating temporal and observation boundaries."],"limits":["Not independent storm counts; no case-level material obtained, so no causal effect or current accuracy is estimated."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"metdefinitions","title":"Tropical cyclone forecast verification - measures of error","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/appendix","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Page undated; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Glossary entries Possibly verified and Detection rate","claim_support":["Possibly verified counts forecasts with verification observations and is distinct from detection rate."],"limits":["Operational terminology is used only under this report's definitions, not equated with denominators for all classification tasks."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.2.2　递归前瞻的按阶评价; Chinese source lines 2641–2646; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-2/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"cba0537b8c35082a66894a8bab592dd4bef2539d0fe1dfac8cbb31cfcba1686b","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.3":{"title":"12.3 Simple baselines and incremental capability","lead":"A complex model must answer a plain question: with the same information, target, and cutoff, what would a simpler method do? Richer stories, more diagrams, or more variables cannot replace that comparison.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2647—2654; source anchor section-12-3; short quotation: ‘If the simple baseline performs equally well, it should be preferred’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Incremental capability is a difference relative to a task-appropriate baseline, not an attractive error figure the model reports for itself. A baseline may extend a trend, retain the most recent state, use historical frequencies, use only agent state or the main circle's state, or adopt an established domain method. Selection should fit the data and task, without deliberately choosing an obviously unsuitable weak opponent.","Fair comparison first requires the same information, cutoff, target, horizon, and evaluation rules. If the complex model receives later events while the simple model uses only earlier materials, the difference cannot be attributed to multi-circle structure. If the complex model is scored only on easy samples while the baseline is scored on the whole set, that is not the same comparison either.","Materials, reasoning budgets, and tuning opportunities should be as proportionate as possible, without forcing a simple baseline to become a complex model. Explain separately the contributions of added information, computation, and structural assumptions. Restricted controls or ablations may help, but one overall score cannot complete causal attribution. State honestly when contributions cannot be isolated.","Predictive systems usually require independent testing or out-of-sample review, with protection against temporal, object, or source leakage. In time series, future materials cannot enter past prediction inputs, and related records should not be treated as fully independent samples. Without available tests, retain capability as unestablished; training fit or retrospective explanation cannot count as foresight performance.","Separate explanatory gain from predictive gain. A model may clarify mechanisms, cost-bearing positions, or constraints without predicting better than the baseline; if that explanatory gain has corresponding materials, retain it while stating that foresight gain is unsupported. Whether explanation actually improves also needs criteria; longer text cannot certify itself.","Complexity carries costs of missingness, mismeasurement, privacy, overfitting, and maintenance. Collecting more people's information adds risks, and model versions need continuing maintenance. Limit use if effectiveness holds only in a small state domain; downgrade or retire when gains decline; if performance is equal and no other sufficient reason exists, prefer the simpler model rather than paying needless costs for a larger terminology.","Equal performance is a bounded conclusion about the current comparison and need not establish population-level statistical equivalence. One win for a simple method does not mean complex methods are always worse; one win for a complex method does not establish universal superiority either. The final report should state samples, error uncertainty, applicability domain, tuning opportunities, and costs, not merely name a winner."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Explanatory gain","leftText":"Evidence-backed improvement in understanding mechanisms or constraints.","rightTitle":"Foresight gain","rightText":"Better performance on unseen outcomes under the same arrangement."},{"leftTitle":"Fair computational conditions","leftText":"Comparable budgets and tuning opportunities, with differences transparent.","rightTitle":"Equal-sized baseline","rightText":"A simple method need not expand to the same scale."},{"leftTitle":"Added information","leftText":"Richer or newer inputs.","rightTitle":"Structural increment","rightText":"A difference from representations and mechanisms under corresponding information conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Training fit","leftText":"Explains records already used for tuning.","rightTitle":"Out-of-sample performance","rightText":"Evaluation on materials not used for model selection, still requiring leakage prevention."},{"leftTitle":"Skill relative to a baseline","leftText":"Improvement in a loss relative to the comparator.","rightTitle":"Event probability or hit rate","rightText":"Different quantities; percentage signs do not make them interchangeable."}],"boundaries":["Without independent testing, capability cannot be treated as established and test results must not be fabricated.","A baseline must not deliberately be denied available materials, and the complex model should not receive future labels.","Cross-domain extrapolation requires review; an out-of-sample advantage in one domain is not victory everywhere.","Equal complexity is not the sole fairness criterion; actual maintenance and protection costs also matter."],"questions":["What are the two methods' MAEs in the example, and how strong a conclusion follows?","How can added information be distinguished from added structure?","What if the complex method improves only explanation?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/figures/section-12-3.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 2024 North Atlantic verification: skill requires a comparator","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 2024 North Atlantic verification: skill requires a comparator","text":"The Met Office reports North Atlantic T+24-hour Track skill of 68%; its official error definitions explain that this compares model position forecasts with the CLIPER climatology–persistence baseline, with positive values indicating lower model error. The added point here is that 68% is neither event probability nor case-by-case hit rate.","sourceLabel":"Met Office, 2024 verification report and error definitions","sourceUrl":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","sourceLocator":"§3.3 table, Track skill(%) row, T+24 column; accompanying definitions, Track skill in the terminology list and the same item under Sign conventions","boundary":"This restates only the report's relative metric and definition, without reverse-calculating exact baseline errors from rounded summaries or inferring performance for every storm or universal long-term superiority. The definition source also appears as metdefinitions in sources.","href":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.3","start_line":2695,"end_line":2702,"startLine":2695,"endLine":2702}],"outputId":"section-12-3","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Choose a task-appropriate simple or domain baseline and set down the comparison arrangement first.","Give methods the same target, information cutoff, horizon, samples, and scoring.","Reasonably balance computation and tuning, separating information, computational, and structural contributions.","Test with independent or out-of-sample materials; without them, capability remains unestablished.","Limit the applicability domain by gains and costs, downgrade when gains disappear, and prefer simplicity under equal performance."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: comparing average errors for the same target","text":"Fictional independent test labels are y=(10,12,8), complex-model predictions (9,13,9), and simple-model predictions (10,10,10). Under the predetermined mean absolute error, the complex model has (1+1+1)/3=1 and the simple model (0+2+2)/3=4/3. On this test set the complex method's average error is lower by 1/3; if collection costs also exist, the net choice still requires normative comparison. These three samples alone cannot establish a stable advantage across the entire domain.","assumptions":"All data and their independent status are teaching stipulations; no actual test was performed.","boundary":"Error improvement holds only for this sample and metric, not in place of confidence intervals or tests of long-term gain."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: a skill percentage is not a hit rate","text":"In a teaching task, the baseline's average loss is 10 and the model's 8. Define relative skill S=1−L模型/L基线 (S = 1 − model loss/baseline loss), giving S=1−8/10=0.2, or 20%. This means loss is 20% lower than the baseline in this example, not that the event has probability 20% or that 80% or 20% of predictions hit individually.","assumptions":"Loss units are consistent, the baseline is nonzero, and the skill definition is this example's publicly stated rule.","boundary":"Different domains may use different skill scores; this formula cannot be forced onto every report."},{"title":"Teaching construction: later information creates an unfair win","text":"Fictional A predicts 10:00 inventory at 08:00 using only 08:00 materials; B predicts the same target at 09:30 after seeing most incoming stock and has lower error. One may say B made a later prediction with more information, not that B's structural model is stronger under the same cutoff. Compare separately using common 08:00 or common 09:30 materials.","assumptions":"Times, information, and performance are a teaching scenario, with no actual operational data.","boundary":"Later updating may be useful, but remaining lead time, the decision window, and information costs require separate evaluation."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The complex model's diagram is more complete, so it proves improved foresight even with identical error.","effect":"Structural richness is not predictive gain; results must be compared under the same arrangement.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The baseline uses old materials and the complex method new materials, but the entire difference comes from structure.","effect":"Information contributions are mixed in, preventing isolated attribution.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Skill relative to the baseline is 20%, so 20% of events will occur.","effect":"Relative loss is not event probability.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What are the two methods' MAEs in the example, and how strong a conclusion follows?","answer":"1 and 4/3; the complex method is lower by 1/3 on this test set. Three constructed samples do not establish a stable advantage.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can added information be distinguished from added structure?","answer":"Set controls under the same information conditions and disclose budget and tuning differences; if isolation is impossible, do not attribute everything to one contribution.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What if the complex method improves only explanation?","answer":"Retain grounded explanatory gain, state that foresight gain is unsupported, and decide continued use according to purpose and cost.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/figures/section-12-3.png","alt":"Same target/horizon, same information/cutoff, same samples/scoring. Task-appropriate baselines: recent state/trend, historical frequency/domain methods. Complex model: explain contributions of additional information, computation, and structure separately. Teaching test labels: 10,12,8, not an actual experiment. Simple predictions 10,10,10 yield MAE=4/3; complex predictions 9,13,9 yield MAE=1, lower by 1/3 on this sample. Numbers are teaching constructions; a single sample comparison establishes no stable domain-wide advantage.","caption":"Numbers are teaching constructions; a single sample comparison establishes no stable domain-wide advantage."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. The two rows show the comparison arrangement and reproducible example respectively, without turning node counts into capability scores.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This primary operational system actually uses CLIPER as its skill baseline, directly illustrating the meanings of comparators and performance metrics."},"openQuestions":["Case-level errors and model-tuning records were not obtained; no statistical-significance or structural-contribution estimate is made."],"taskId":"S-12-3","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2491—2498 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["12.3: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"met2024","title":"Tropical cyclone verification northern hemisphere 2024","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/nhem2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2024 season report","locator":"§3.3 table, Track skill(%), T+24=68","claim_support":["Reported 2024 North Atlantic 24-hour relative skill: 68%"],"limits":["Neither event probability nor hit rate; case-level errors were not independently recalculated, and exact baseline values are not inferred from rounded numbers."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"metdefinitions","title":"Tropical cyclone forecast verification - measures of error","url":"https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/research/weather/tropical-cyclones/verification/summaries/appendix","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"No separate date on the page; read 2026-10-14","locator":"Track skill in the terminology list; ‘Track skill positive…’ under Sign conventions","claim_support":["Track skill uses CLIPER as the position-comparison baseline; a positive value means the model has lower error than CLIPER"],"limits":["This explains only this operational metric, without treating a percentage as probability or extrapolating to current service performance."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.3　简单基线与增量能力; Chinese source lines 2647–2654; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ee8d84e5fe669f1de8de0ecc3e09cfbb17b7b92ebfc923b76f7a57f1bb6d0a62","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.4":{"title":"12.4 Probabilities, grades, and time windows","lead":"Probability, priority for attention, personal belief, and greater value can all be expressed numerically, but are different quantities. Clarifying which question a number answers matters more than writing it with precision.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2655—2673; source anchor section-12-4; short quotation: ‘Percentages must not be invented to establish an ordering’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Probabilities or probability intervals must correspond to decidable events and specify the generative model, priors where applicable, data, boundaries, and calibration status. With insufficient probability grounds, evidence-backed grades or path comparisons may be given, but ranking itself may also be unknown; ‘no probability’ cannot automatically become ‘at least a direction can be given’. Sample size is only one condition and does not determine output type alone.","The source table's four evidence statuses have different ceilings: probability grounds permit probabilities; grade-only grounds require retaining incomparable items and contrary signals; direction is given only with identification grounds, otherwise unknown plus the minimum discriminating observation; unstable targets or objects prohibit issuing foresight until they are defined anew. They are not a mandatory sequential downgrade ladder.","Time windows follow mechanism clocks; short-term emotional responses, organizational processes, and institutional changes cannot share one default horizon. Forcing a slow mechanism into a short window may wrongly judge ineffectiveness before it could take effect; extending short-term signals indefinitely mistakes transients for trends. Multiclock tasks may register separate horizons for intermediate and final outcomes while retaining their respective evaluations.","Real-world probability concerns event occurrence under corresponding conditions and needs estimation or calibration grounds; subjective belief records an agent's judgment under particular information; decision weights come from a descriptive model or value premises; path rankings hold only under their comparison relation; computational priorities state what limited resources should examine first. Even the same 0.8 across all five cannot make them prove one another.","High consequences, a short window, or easy discrimination may give a path with unknown probability high checking priority. Disclose priority reasons and budgets rather than invent a percentage to appear rational. Most worth checking does not mean most likely, and most desired does not mean most likely either; recommendation rankings additionally require norms and distributional considerations and do not follow directly from occurrence probabilities.","Model sampling frequencies are computational results under given parameters, sampling methods, and rules. They may estimate model-conditional probabilities, but real-world probabilities additionally require mappings, parameter support, and calibration. More samples cannot repair a wrong model or unobserved variables. If sampling deliberately oversamples a risk branch, its frequency may not even equal probability under the model's original distribution; preserve weights and methods.","A small sample can produce a posterior under explicit priors and likelihoods, but that posterior remains assumption-dependent; the small sample itself cannot certify real-world parameters. Large samples may also share incorrect definitions or selection bias; precise estimation of the wrong quantity is not the probability of the event being asked about.","Publish probability judgments, empirical calibration, and normative priorities separately. High personal confidence is not calibrated long-run frequency; good calibration on comparable tasks also does not automatically satisfy protections for every subgroup or action authorization. Strength of expression rests on evidence and use, not readers' desired certainty or model fluency."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Real-world probability","leftText":"Concerns the target event, conditions, and estimation grounds.","rightTitle":"Personal belief","rightText":"A person's judgment under particular information, not automatically empirically calibrated."},{"leftTitle":"Research priority","leftText":"Affected by consequences, windows, discriminability, and budget.","rightTitle":"Ranking by occurrence probability","rightText":"Requires corresponding evidence of event probabilities."},{"leftTitle":"Model-conditional frequency","leftText":"Produced by given rules and sampling design.","rightTitle":"Real-world occurrence frequency","rightText":"Requires external materials and conditions for transfer."},{"leftTitle":"Posterior probability","leftText":"Updating from data under a prior and likelihood.","rightTitle":"Assumption-free objective truth","rightText":"No formula automatically removes model conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Qualitative direction","leftText":"Also a conclusion requiring identification support.","rightTitle":"Default fallback answer","rightText":"Evidence may permit opposing directions simultaneously."}],"boundaries":["When the object is unstable, do not issue foresight under the old definition or hide failure behind vague targets.","Large sample counts can only reduce corresponding computational error, not upgrade model outputs into reality.","Prioritizing a high-consequence branch does not mean it is known to be likely or that high-impact action is authorized.","The same probability may have different decision implications for different cost-bearing positions; value allocation requires separate discussion."],"questions":["With a uniform prior and one success, why is the posterior prediction 2/3?","If half the generated samples are A for the purpose of checking A, may A's probability be called one half?","Can a branch be checked first when its probability is unknown?","Why can a short-term signal not be extended indefinitely as a trend?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/figures/section-12-4.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Simulated distributions in a 2020 paper: real-data input is not a real response process","href":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Simulated distributions in a 2020 paper: real-data input is not a real response process","text":"Perdomo and colleagues explicitly label §5.2 of their original paper as simulations: the base uses a subset of real credit data, but agent responses and distribution changes are model-stipulated. This section uses the published research design only to show that real data and simulated mechanisms may coexist, with the latter still retaining assumption status.","sourceLabel":"Perdomo、Zrnic、Mendler-Dünner、Hardt，2020","sourceUrl":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf","sourceLocator":"PDF page 2, final paragraph of §1.1, ‘semi-synthetic’; page 8, first three paragraphs of §5.2 Simulations and the Figure2 sampling procedure","boundary":"This is the research method actually used in the paper, not behavioral outcomes of experimental deployment to real borrowers. The code was not reproduced, no personal data were accessed, and no credit-decision advice is supplied.","href":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.4","start_line":2703,"end_line":2721,"startLine":2703,"endLine":2721}],"outputId":"section-12-4","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Specify what each number or grade concerns: event probability, belief, weight, ranking, or research priority.","Choose the expression according to target stability, generation method, and identification conditions; retain unknown if grounds for direction are absent.","Set corresponding windows for each mechanism's outcomes rather than extrapolating short-term signals indefinitely.","Distinguish model sampling from real-world probability, explaining boundaries of priors, data, selection, and calibration.","High-risk checks may be prioritized without inventing probabilities; action recommendations require separate normative comparison."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: one datum can yield a conditional posterior","text":"Let unknown success rate θ∈[0,1] have an artificially stipulated uniform prior density 1, with conditionally independent identically distributed trials and one observed success, giving likelihood θ. The normalization constant ∫ from 0 to 1 θdθ=1/2, so posterior density is 2θ and next-trial posterior predictive mean is ∫ from 0 to 1 θ·2θdθ=2/3. This calculation depends on prior, observation, and generative assumptions, not ‘one observation reveals actual success rate 2/3’.","assumptions":"Entirely a mathematical construction, with one success only inside the model; no actual trial or justification of a real-world prior.","boundary":"Different priors, selective observation, or nonidentical distributions change the answer; neither small sample size nor the formula itself establishes real calibration."},{"title":"Teaching construction: severity sets checking priority without assigning probability","text":"In a fictional system, path A may cause irreversible harm with a 10-minute response window, while B causes only a delay after which the prior state can be restored, and can wait a day. Both probabilities are unknown, but explicit protective goals and current checking resources can prioritize A. Report ‘A checked first because of consequences and window; probability unknown’, not an invented ‘A has 80%’ to justify ranking.","assumptions":"Risk types, windows, and resources are constructed; no actual emergency is described and no action authorized.","boundary":"Checking itself still needs authority and safety boundaries; severe consequences are not an unconditional excuse to act."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: oversampling frequency is not the original model probability","text":"A fictional model specifies P(A)=0.1 and P(non-A)=0.9. To examine A’s consequences, researchers deliberately generate 50 A and 50 non-A scenarios. The sample’s 50% A reflects a design quota, not a model probability changed to 0.5, much less a real probability. Estimating an expectation under the original model requires combining branch-conditional results with original weights 0.1 and 0.9.","assumptions":"Probabilities and quotas are stipulated, with no actual sampling; this shows only the distinction between design and target distributions.","boundary":"Actual importance sampling also needs corresponding support and weight conditions; the example does not replace a full estimation procedure."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An outcome appears in 80 of 100 generations, so its actual probability is 80%.","effect":"The generation distribution, parameter grounds, and real-world mapping must be known; generation frequency alone is insufficient.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"There is only one sample, so no posterior is possible under any explicit probability model.","effect":"The construction gives 2/3, strictly dependent on prior and model; small sample size alone does not determine computability.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The path most worth checking must be the most likely.","effect":"Consequences, windows, and discriminatory value may change priorities and cannot be conflated with probability.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"With a uniform prior and one success, why is the posterior prediction 2/3?","answer":"Density 1 updates through likelihood θ to 2θ; integrating θ·2θ gives 2/3, entirely under explicit model assumptions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If half the generated samples are A for the purpose of checking A, may A's probability be called one half?","answer":"No. That is a sampling quota; the original model gives 0.1, and reality still needs external support.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can a branch be checked first when its probability is unknown?","answer":"Rank by explicit consequences, windows, and information value, retaining unknown probability and boundaries on checking authority.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can a short-term signal not be extended indefinitely as a trend?","answer":"Mechanisms may change, and short windows do not support slow variables and external conditions; register and calibrate by window.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/figures/section-12-4.png","alt":"Event probability: target/conditions/data, calibration separately checked; personal belief: who judges with what information; normative and research priority: consequences/window/budget, without requiring known probability; teaching model P(A)=0.1, deliberately oversampled for checking; generated 50 A+50 non-A, sample A=50%; design frequency≠original probability, much less actual probability; original weights remain 0.1/0.9. The lower row is a mathematical construction; the upper three quantities are not interchangeable, and identical numbers do not give identical semantics.","caption":"The lower row is a mathematical construction; the upper three quantities are not interchangeable, and identical numbers do not give identical semantics."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. No fictional probability curve is drawn; future quota switching must retain labels for target and actual sampling distributions.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The primary paper explicitly states a semisynthetic setup and modeled distribution mapping, illustrating why sample inputs and generative mechanisms’ evidentiary status cannot be conflated."},"openQuestions":["This guide did not reproduce the paper’s experiments and does not extrapolate theoretical stability or simulations to effects in actual populations."],"taskId":"S-12-4","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2499–2517; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["12.4: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"perdomo2020","title":"Performative Prediction","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"ICML2020，PMLR119:7599—7609","locator":"PDF p. 2, final paragraph of §1.1; p. 8, §5.2 Simulations and Figure 2","claim_support":["The authors describe underlying real data and a semisynthetic setup with modeled distribution mappings, distinguishing actual research design from real deployment effects"],"limits":["Only relevant methods passages and theoretical definitions were read; experiments and proofs were not fully reproduced. This is no actual credit-risk estimate or authorization basis."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.4　概率、等级与时间窗; Chinese source lines 2655–2673; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-4/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"c9ed757cdbb0c4fb5a6831c4242f788a871e40c097b73c34ccfe5b10b74c359d","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.5":{"title":"12.5 Calibration and Evaluation","lead":"Evaluation first asks what is evaluated, for which population, response regime, and horizon. A score answers a specified prediction question, not additionally how many outcomes action saved or who may act.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2674–2702; source anchor section-12-5; short quotation: ‘Do not enter zero for missing labels.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","Foresight capability comes from pre-registration, comparable outcomes, and continuing comparison, not fluent retrospective explanation. Set down original targets, horizons, success rules, and information cutoffs in advance. A single case can be scored but cannot certify long-term calibration. Outcome-material formation, missingness, and entry/exit also form evaluation conditions.","Measures depend on the task: probability forecasts may use Brier scores, log loss, calibration curves, and resolution; prediction intervals also need coverage and width. Rankings use predetermined ranking measures, event detection precision/recall or proportionate domain measures, and timing predictions window coverage and lead time. An attractive number cannot replace the originally relevant target.","This guide uses the binary single-event Brier convention L=(p−y)², lower being better; log loss uses natural logarithms, L=−[y ln p+(1−y)ln(1−p)], with infinite loss for probabilities 0 or 1 facing opposite labels. The original multiclass-vector form may differ by a constant factor from this convention; align definitions across reports first. Unsupported probabilities should not be invented just to use a measure.","Calibration asks whether long-run probabilities correspond to observed proportions among comparable forecasts; resolution asks whether objects of differing risk can be distinguished. Reporting one population rate for everyone may be overall calibrated yet have no discriminatory resolution. Chance bin agreement in finite samples does not establish future calibration; sample size, uncertainty, binning, and applicability also matter.","Each of the original’s six evaluation dimensions has a corrective direction. Failed calibration may change expression or downgrade probabilities; weak resolution may remove unhelpful variables or return to baseline; poor coverage may revise intervals or mechanism clocks; no baseline gain stops the complex model’s gain claim; instability across times/locations narrows the domain or retires the model; recurrent underestimation or false alarms at specific positions require affected-party review, remedies, and stronger protection. Wider intervals may improve coverage while losing usefulness; do not optimize one number alone.","Use determines error costs and required reports. Observation reminders and coercive measures may share a score but impose different false-alarm burdens; low-probability high-harm tasks cannot report overall accuracy alone. Show errors by burden-bearing position, population, and phase so averages do not hide sustained false denials, delays, or misses. Excellent measures prove neither correct mechanisms nor normative legitimacy.","Published forecasts may affect action, which may alter actual events; specify the response regime. Preserve reach, actual execution, entry/exit, data formation, and institutional-change records. For targets defined as actual events under the current response regime, score actual labels normally. ‘The model affected reality’ cannot cancel a valid score, nor must every prediction evaluation be converted into intervention-effect research.","Three parallel questions do not replace one another: prediction performance for a target set down in advance, using comparable labels for loss/classification/coverage; how publication or action changed outcomes, requiring proportionate causal comparison; and appropriateness of prediction/response, requiring explicit norms, protection, costs, distribution, and authority. ‘No event observed’ alone proves neither false alarm nor successful protection; return to target, label, and response definitions.","Specifically, if the pre-registered target is the actual event under the current response regime, p=0.8 with complete endpoint label y=0 gives Brier=0.64; with a prior threshold 0.5, this target’s false positive may also be recorded. The calculation says nothing about what would happen without action. If monitoring is offline and y unknown, do not substitute 0. Complete labels only for service recipients directly support only that group, not labels for unreached, rejected, or exited people.","After institutional change, first ask whether the question remains the same. With unchanged targets, report performance changes and comparability limits honestly; a target under a different regime or population needs new registration, preserving old predictions and scores. Do not post hoc change ‘will occur under the current regime’ to ‘would occur without response’ to rescue an erroneous prediction."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Single-case loss","leftText":"Calculated for one prediction that was set down and its complete label.","rightTitle":"Long-term calibration","rightText":"Whether long-run probabilities and occurrence proportions match for comparable predictions."},{"leftTitle":"Calibration","leftText":"Correspondence between probabilities and frequencies.","rightTitle":"Resolution","rightText":"Ability to distinguish objects of differing risk."},{"leftTitle":"Prediction evaluation","leftText":"Performance for a target under the current response regime.","rightTitle":"Intervention effect","rightText":"How much actual response changes outcomes relative to suitable comparison conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Complete zero label","leftText":"The event is confirmed absent under its definition.","rightTitle":"Label unavailable","rightText":"Monitoring may be offline or selectively invisible; zero cannot fill it."},{"leftTitle":"Excellent within-domain performance","leftText":"Performance for specified population, target, and regime.","rightTitle":"Generalizable and justified","rightText":"Still needs transfer, distributional, normative, and authority arguments."}],"boundaries":["Reflexivity does not exempt clearly defined targets with complete labels from scoring.","No observed event alone does not identify successful protection; an observed event alone does not prove every response ineffective.","Overall calibration guarantees neither subgroup calibration nor satisfaction of fairness or protection constraints.","Scores establish only corresponding predictive performance, not correct causes, authority to act, or absence of omitted populations."],"questions":["Why does p=0.8,y=0 give loss 0.64 rather than 0.2?","Which two causal situations are both compatible with no event?","If a model reports 0.5 for everyone and half have events, does it have good resolution?","Must old scores be discarded when the response regime changes?","May missing labels be conservatively recorded as zero?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/figures/section-12-5.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Research case: including prediction-induced distribution changes in the evaluation object","href":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Research case: including prediction-induced distribution changes in the evaluation object","text":"Perdomo et al.’s 2020 paper opens by discussing how predictions used for decisions may change predicted outcomes and formalizes this through a model-parameter-dependent distribution D(θ). This is published theoretical modeling offering methodological guidance on explicit response regimes, not proof that a particular warning saved outcomes.","sourceLabel":"Perdomo et al., 2020, Performative Prediction","sourceUrl":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf","sourceLocator":"PDF p. 1, Abstract and first two paragraphs of §1; p. 3, §2.1","boundary":"Only the problem setup and definitions are used, without importing optimization conclusions or treating the paper as evidence of an actual intervention’s effect.","href":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.5","start_line":2722,"end_line":2750,"startLine":2722,"endLine":2750}],"outputId":"section-12-5","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Set down target, population, horizon, information cutoff, scoring convention, and response regime.","Choose measures for actual use and clearly record comparable labels, missingness, and formation processes.","Compute valid scores for the original forecasts; missing labels are not nonoccurrence.","Identify publication/action causal effects separately; no event alone cannot prove successful prevention.","Separately compare adoption options through norms, protection, distribution, and authority; scores cannot sign authorization.","Adjust domain and output strength through the six-dimensional evaluation; changed targets require new registration while preserving old outcomes."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: why Brier prefers true probability within a fixed generative model","text":"Let Y be Bernoulli with parameter q and prediction p. Expected loss q(1−p)²+(1−q)p²=p²−2pq+q=(p−q)²+q(1−q). The second term is independent of p; the first is nonnegative and zero only at p=q, so expectation is minimized at p=q. This algebraic proof within a specified generative model does not infer true model parameters backward from one low score.","assumptions":"One fixed target and distribution, p,q∈[0,1]; mathematics only, no actual trial.","boundary":"Regime changes, selective labels, or mismatched targets prevent direct reuse of one q; this property certifies neither causal explanations nor action authority."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: bin calibration can match while resolution differs","text":"Construct 20 complete labels: 2 of 10 low-risk items and 8 of 10 high-risk items are 1. The model predicts 0.2 and 0.8 respectively. Each group’s Brier sum is 2×0.8²+8×0.2²=1.6, giving overall mean 3.2/20=0.16. A baseline predicts 0.5 for all; 10 of 20 events match 0.5 overall, but each loss is 0.25 and risks are not distinguished. This finite construction only illustrates bin frequencies versus resolution.","assumptions":"Labels, groups, and probabilities are stipulated; no actual fitting, calibration sample, or independent test.","boundary":"Exact matching over 20 items is not long-term calibration; groups must be predetermined and evaluation labels must not select models retrospectively."},{"title":"Teaching construction: 0.64 is calculable, how much was prevented is not","text":"A fictional warning registration explicitly predicts events under the current response regime, registering p=0.8, threshold 0.5, and complete endpoint label y=0. Brier=(0.8−0)²=0.64 and log loss −ln0.2≈1.609; predicted positive and actually negative permits this target’s false positive. If action occurred and observed Y(action)=0, Y(no action) may be 0 or 1: the former means occurrence was unchanged, the latter one event prevented. The current observation cannot distinguish these two constructed worlds.","assumptions":"Pure teaching construction; potential outcomes illustrate identification boundaries, with no actual action or randomized trial.","boundary":"If monitoring was offline and y unavailable, this calculation fails; if the original target was not an event under current response, evaluate under its original registration rather than changing labels to fit the formula."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"No event followed the warning, so it must have been a false alarm.","effect":"First check target and complete labels; only an explicit current-response target and prior threshold permit bounded classification, not the bare statement of no event.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The warning triggered a response, so erroneous predictions should never be scored.","effect":"A target set down in advance with complete labels remains scoreable; identify response effects separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Twenty perfectly matching bin frequencies prove permanent calibration.","effect":"A finite construction establishes only this sample’s relation; long-term, subgroup, and transfer properties still need testing.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Observing only recipients’ outcomes permits reporting the same accuracy for all applicants.","effect":"Without nonrecipients’ labels or valid identification grounds, direct generalization fails.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does p=0.8,y=0 give loss 0.64 rather than 0.2?","answer":"This guide’s binary Brier squares probability error: (0.8−0)²=0.64; 0.2 is the predicted probability of nonoccurrence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Which two causal situations are both compatible with no event?","answer":"Either the event would not have occurred anyway, or it would have occurred but response prevented it. A zero after action alone cannot distinguish them.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If a model reports 0.5 for everyone and half have events, does it have good resolution?","answer":"No. One probability for everyone may match overall while failing to distinguish the constructed 0.2 and 0.8 groups.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Must old scores be discarded when the response regime changes?","answer":"No. With the same target, report changed performance and comparison limits; changed targets require new registration, preserving old results.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May missing labels be conservatively recorded as zero?","answer":"No. Zero confirms nonoccurrence; unknown remains unknown, with coverage and missingness mechanisms separately recorded.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/figures/section-12-5.png","alt":"How did the original forecast perform? Target set down+complete labels → score. How much did response change? Proportionate causal comparison required; no event alone cannot identify it. Is adoption appropriate? Norms/distribution/protection and separate authority checks. Construction p=0.8,y=0 gives Brier=0.64, uncalculable with a missing label. Observed Y(action)=0 leaves Y(no action) at 0 or 1, so effect is not independently identified. Accuracy or one effect does not itself create execution authority. Three parallel columns: scoring on the left cannot replace effect identification in the middle or normative/authority judgments on the right.","caption":"Three parallel columns: scoring on the left cannot replace effect identification in the middle or normative/authority judgments on the right."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Future interaction could toggle complete zero versus missing labels; missingness must disable scoring rather than default to 0.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The primary paper’s formal setup illustrates relations between prediction and deployment distributions; specific scores and causal nonidentification are shown through explicit constructions separately."},"openQuestions":["Actual deployment’s missing labels, response intensity, and distributional effects are unspecified; these constructions cannot estimate actual performance or prevented harm."],"taskId":"S-12-5","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2518–2546; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["12.5: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"perdomo2020","title":"Performative Prediction","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v119/perdomo20a/perdomo20a.pdf","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"ICML2020，PMLR119:7599—7609","locator":"PDF p. 1, Abstract and first two paragraphs of §1; p. 3, §2.1","claim_support":["Using predictions in decisions may change outcomes; a formal problem setup for model-dependent distributions"],"limits":["Cites only the problem setup, without using its theorems to prove arbitrary responses effective or executing its simulations."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.5　校准与评价; Chinese source lines 2674–2702; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-5/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"bc40d7c972da7c2677cd6ccbd57b3b14d5ad0723c5fb7fdd51cb7ef72d54e192","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.6":{"title":"12.6 Early signals, contrary signals, and trigger points","lead":"Useful lead time runs through completion of protection, not merely appearance of an alarm on a screen. Signals trigger review; they cannot replace path explanation, normative judgment, or authority to act.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2703–2710; original anchor section-12-6. Short quotation: “Earlier algorithmic detection does not guarantee that the last person affected can receive protection before harm occurs.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Early signals should precede outcomes through the discussed path's mechanism and have observable definitions. For a backlog path, an early change might be arrivals persistently exceeding processing, not a smaller number restating the final backlog in advance. Register observation position, definition, window, and relationship to the path. Without those bridges, any preceding phenomenon could retrospectively be packaged as a precursor.","Contrary signals concern failure of key conditions or support for competing paths. They need not prove the final outcome impossible; they weaken the current path's grounds. Register both signal types before outcomes, specifying which records count as observation and which reflect missing monitoring. Searching only for support removes foresight's possibility of failure.","Trigger points connect observation to review of object identity, path conditions, probability expression, norms, and authorization. A detected signal does not automatically permit action. If an explicitly lawful preauthorization procedure exists, act within its conditions and limits; this section's abstract rule cannot create authority by itself.","High-risk veto triggers should be explicit beforehand: a breached protection floor, key affected people unable to exit, distorted data sources, deteriorating calibration, and so forth. Applicable rules require suspension or veto of the corresponding option and protection review, not merely a slight confidence reduction while continuing unchanged. They also need not stop every task unrelated to the defect.","The clock ends at completed action or effective protection. Detection, verification, issuance, reach, understanding, funding or allocation, transfer, and onset may each take time, differently across positions. Average reach cannot establish completion for the last or hardest-to-reach person. Parallel stages need dependency-based critical-path calculations; only serial delays add directly. Retain unknown delays as unknowns or grounded intervals.","Algorithmic lead time is hazard time minus detection time. Actual protection margin also subtracts time needed for downstream completion. Technical advance notice does not mean every affected person has enough time; communication, understanding, resources, and actual capability can bottleneck. Addressing bottlenecks requires more than greater predictive precision: examine endpoint conditions and protective responsibilities.","No harm after an alarm still returns to the targets and labels registered in advance. If the target is the actual event under the current response regime, with complete labels and predetermined thresholds, an operational false positive may be recorded. Whether response prevented harm needs a separate causal comparison. Unreached people and missing records cannot default to safety, and “successful protection” cannot retrospectively rescue every warning.","Three easily confused rates require numerators, denominators, population, and window. Under a fixed binary classification, operational false-positive rate FPR=FP/(FP+TN), false-alarm fraction among alerts FAR=FP/(TP+FP), and false-negative rate FNR=FN/(TP+FN). Names vary across domains; formulas matter more than abbreviations. A zero denominator makes the rate undefined, not zero. Precision TP/(TP+FP) and recall TP/(TP+FN) may be reported separately and must not be interchanged."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Early signal","leftText":"An observable change expected earlier by the mechanism.","rightTitle":"A smaller version of the outcome","rightText":"Describing the outcome early without a mechanism bridge."},{"leftTitle":"Contrary signal","leftText":"Weakens a key path condition or supports a competing explanation.","rightTitle":"Absolute exclusion of the outcome","rightText":"Needs stronger evidence and does not follow automatically."},{"leftTitle":"Algorithmic lead time","leftText":"Hazard time minus detection time.","rightTitle":"Protection margin","rightText":"Also subtracts reach, understanding, resources, and action-onset delays."},{"leftTitle":"False-positive rate","leftText":"False positives among all actual negatives.","rightTitle":"False-alarm fraction among alerts","rightText":"False positives among all alarms."},{"leftTitle":"Triggered review","leftText":"Checks whether conditions and responsibilities still hold.","rightTitle":"Automatic authorization","rightText":"Cannot arise from observing a signal itself."}],"boundaries":["Absence of contrary signals may reflect missing monitoring, not continuing satisfaction of key conditions.","Unknown delays cannot be zeroed. Timely averages do not mean timeliness at every burden-bearing position.","Veto triggers such as distorted data require proportionate suspension and protection, not rewording while execution continues.","Operational false alarms and causal effects of responses are distinct questions; one score cannot replace the other."],"questions":["Why is protection for the first object 3 minutes late?","With FP=18, TN=72, TP=8, what are the two false-alarm measures?","Do trigger points prohibit every preauthorization procedure?","Does absence of harm after an alarm establish a causal warning effect?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/figures/section-12-6.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Actual institutional design: WMO lists dissemination and response capability separately","href":"https://wmo.int/resources/wmo-bulletin/wmo-bulletin-vol-72-1-2023/overview-of-early-warnings-all-executive-action-plan-2023-2027"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Actual institutional design: WMO lists dissemination and response capability separately","text":"WMO's 2023 overview of the Early Warnings for All action plan lists four pillars: risk knowledge, monitoring and forecasting, warning dissemination and communication, and preparedness and response capability. This public design retains dissemination and response as separate areas of development, reminding readers that completed detection is not completed end-to-end protection.","sourceLabel":"WMO Bulletin，2023-05-05","sourceUrl":"https://wmo.int/resources/wmo-bulletin/wmo-bulletin-vol-72-1-2023/overview-of-early-warnings-all-executive-action-plan-2023-2027","sourceLocator":"Opening paragraph and four-pillar list; Year One—2023 subsections Warning dissemination and communication and Preparedness to respond","boundary":"An actually published plan structure, not evidence that annual targets were achieved, of a region's protective effects, or of this lesson's delay values.","href":"https://wmo.int/resources/wmo-bulletin/wmo-bulletin-vol-72-1-2023/overview-of-early-warnings-all-executive-action-plan-2023-2027"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.6","start_line":2751,"end_line":2758,"startLine":2751,"endLine":2758}],"outputId":"section-12-6","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["First explain why the path generates the preceding change, registering observable signals and contrary evidence.","Fix thresholds, windows, missing-data handling, and veto triggers before outcomes.","Observation triggers review, not automatic action. Check object, path, expression, norms, and authority individually.","End the clock at completable protection; check critical paths and resources for every bearing position.","Compute operational metrics under targets registered in advance and report denominators separately. Identify prevented-harm effects independently."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: early and contrary signals must connect to mechanisms","text":"A fictional queue starts with backlog 6. Over the next two hours, arrivals are 2/hour and processing 1/hour, giving backlogs 7 then 8. Pre-register an early signal of complete first-hour records showing 2 arrivals and 1 completion, and a contrary signal of second-hour evidence that capacity rose to 3 and 3 were actually processed. If the latter occurs, the endpoint is 6 rather than the original path's 8. Seeing only “capacity-expansion application submitted” does not establish increased actual processing.","assumptions":"Queue, values, and criteria are teaching constructions, with no actual system experiment.","boundary":"Unrecorded arrivals or false completion records invalidate signal status and require source checks. The contrary signal revises the current path, not proves backlog impossible forever."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: a 30-minute warning can still be late","text":"Let hazard time be minute 30 and signal detection minute 0. Fully serial verification 3, issuance 2, reach 8, understanding 4, resource allocation 6, and action onset 10 total 33 minutes, leaving protection margin 30−33=−3. Another object requires only 2 minutes for reach, totaling 27 with margin 3. Both have the same 30-minute algorithmic lead, yet timely protection cannot be claimed for both. Reducing the first object's reach to 4 gives total 29 and margin 1. This is only clock arithmetic under stated conditions.","assumptions":"Every time and serial dependency is artificial; no actual drill or disaster response occurred.","boundary":"Actual stages may overlap, delays have distributions, and hazard times have error. Check critical paths and uncertainty. This calculation is not emergency-operating advice."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: the same 18 false positives, three different denominators","text":"Construct 100 consistently defined, fully labeled objects: TP=8, FP=18, FN=2, TN=72. Operational false-positive rate is 18/(18+72)=20%; false-alarm fraction among alerts is 18/(8+18)=9/13≈69.23%; false-negative rate is 2/(8+2)=20%. Precision is 8/26≈30.77%, recall 8/10=80%. The two 20% values coincide only in this example; the metrics are not identical.","assumptions":"Threshold, target, response regime, population, and horizon are fixed. All 100 labels are constructed.","boundary":"If labels cover only response recipients, this complete-population confusion matrix cannot be reused directly. Classification does not identify the number saved by intervention."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The warning is issued 30 minutes early, so everyone has 30 minutes to act.","effect":"Verification, reach, understanding, resources, and onset consume time, and endpoint capabilities differ across people.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"18 false positives necessarily mean a 20% false-alarm rate.","effect":"Specify the denominator: this construction gives FPR 20% but approximately 69.23% false alarms among alerts.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"After a contrary signal appears, only material supporting the original path continues to be collected.","effect":"Contrary signals must substantively enter review, withdrawing the path or weakening expression when necessary.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why is protection for the first object 3 minutes late?","answer":"Total serial delay is 3+2+8+4+6+10=33; hazard occurs at 30, leaving protection margin −3.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"With FP=18, TN=72, TP=8, what are the two false-alarm measures?","answer":"False positives among actual negatives: 18/90=20%. Among alerts: 18/26≈69.23%.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Do trigger points prohibit every preauthorization procedure?","answer":"No. Applicable existing preauthorization may be used under its conditions, but observation itself creates no new authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does absence of harm after an alarm establish a causal warning effect?","answer":"Not by itself. Compare corresponding outcomes without that response; the original forecast remains evaluated under the targets and labels registered in advance.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/figures/section-12-6.png","alt":"Construction: hazard t=30, signal detected t=0, algorithmic lead 30 minutes. Verification 3＋issuance 2, reach 8＋understanding 4, resources 6＋onset 10; total serial delay 33, protection margin −3 minutes, not completed in time. Register path and contrary signals before outcomes; distorted material may trigger veto. Review object/path/probabilities and norms/authorization after observation. Metrics need denominators: FP/actual negatives differs from FP/alerts. Delay values form a serial teaching construction; actual systems require checks of parallel dependencies, each position's conditions, and time uncertainty.","caption":"Delay values form a serial teaching construction; actual systems require checks of parallel dependencies, each position's conditions, and time uncertainty."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Future interaction may adjust delays and display protection margins, without treating demonstration sliders as actual emergency parameters.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The official action-plan structure directly supports separate consideration of detection, dissemination, and response capability. Specific clocks use explicit constructions instead of invented real effectiveness."},"openQuestions":["Actual population reach/resource delays, protection floors, and veto authority still require local institutional arrangements and checkable material."],"taskId":"S-12-6","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 2547–2554; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["12.6: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"wmo2023","title":"Overview of the Early Warnings for All: Executive Action Plan 2023–2027","url":"https://wmo.int/resources/wmo-bulletin/wmo-bulletin-vol-72-1-2023/overview-of-early-warnings-all-executive-action-plan-2023-2027","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023-05-05; webpage marked updated 2026-09-30","locator":"Opening paragraph and four-pillar list; Year One—2023 dissemination and response-capability subsections","claim_support":["The official plan treats monitoring/forecasting, dissemination/communication, and preparedness/response capability as different pillars."],"limits":["A plan design, not evidence of target achievement or causal protection effects. Webpage disaster figures and annual indicators were not used."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.6　早期信号、反向信号与触发点; Chinese source lines 2703–2710; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-6/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"9abbf5093fac0cc384929c6a084b6b4b193d87fcc9ae3e3fc0361adbfcd93cb4","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.7":{"title":"12.7 What forecast failure means","lead":"Forecast honesty lies not only in cautious publication but in allowing the original record to be judged unsupported, unresolved, or unevaluable at expiry. Giving failure a vague new name does not improve accuracy.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2711–2716; original anchor section-12-7. Short quotation: “‘Unresolved’ is no compromise between success and failure.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","This section gives five results for evaluation at expiry: supported, not supported, unresolved, target invalidated, and cannot evaluate. Return to the prior registration's target, identity criterion, window, material, and success rules rather than selecting face-saving labels. Rules must also specify overlap handling beforehand; the five names are not an automatic classifier needing no explanation.","Supported means obtained material meets the support rule of the original registration, only within the original scope, not proof of every mechanism, probability, or extension. Not supported means the support criterion of the original registration did not hold; complete observations contradicting the original assertion may be explicitly reported as failure. One occurrence or nonoccurrence does not automatically validate or invalidate a probability. Use the original scoring and long-term evaluation.","Unresolved is a state of insufficient evidence that the original registration permits and defines. For example, a measurement-error interval crosses the predetermined threshold, so the available complete material cannot distinguish the sides. It is not half a success or permission to extend deadlines indefinitely until favorable material arrives. Any allowed supplementary observation procedure must be registered beforehand, retaining the original expiry status.","Cannot evaluate must identify the failed prerequisite, such as permanently lost key data, an untraceable object, an inoperable horizon definition, or unclear execution records. The source also lists one more kind: a premise that was written into the target at registration, and whose continued holding can be determined, has not held, so that under §12.5 the original target is judged to be no longer the same question; where the original target is still the same question it is evaluated as usual, and a premise not written into the target at registration cannot be used after the result appears to re-record the result as “not evaluable.” The arrangement made at registration should distinguish it from unresolved: the former may mean the evaluation procedure's entry conditions are unusable, the latter that entry remains usable but evidence cannot decide. This operational distinction is this lesson's illustration of such an arrangement, not an assertion that the original supplies one exhaustive, unique algorithm.","Target invalidated means the original forecast object actually converted, or its identity criterion actually failed. If a specified service unit is abolished and its tasks and members absorbed elsewhere, and the original registration specifies no uptake relation, the new organization's outcomes cannot masquerade as a hit for the old object. Object change needs material; unfavorable results cannot declare identity change.","Changing targets, horizons, or circle scope after outcomes, or merging competing paths into “something happened a little,” cannot retain the original foresight's success eligibility. Acknowledge the original result and create a new version for a new question, object, or conditions. Old registration, evidence, and scores remain, with traceable revision reasons.","What failure means serves correction; it is not evasive language. Unresolved specifies missing distinguishing material; cannot evaluate explains why evaluation cannot run; target invalidated demonstrates that the object converted or its identity criterion failed and states continuing monitoring and remedial responsibilities. Excluding difficult objects en masse cannot improve claimed hit rates. Summaries should show counts for all statuses, total original registrations, and the evaluable subset, avoiding selective denominators."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Unresolved","leftText":"Evidence insufficiency defined in the arrangement made at registration; the answer remains unknown.","rightTitle":"Half a success","rightText":"Converting unknowns into hits without a definition distorts results."},{"leftTitle":"Cannot evaluate","leftText":"Required procedural, material, or object conditions cannot be met.","rightTitle":"Forecast confirmed","rightText":"Insufficient material cannot become positive evidence."},{"leftTitle":"Target invalidated","leftText":"Material supports that the object converted or that its identity criterion failed.","rightTitle":"Changing objects afterward","rightText":"Moving forecast scope to preserve success."},{"leftTitle":"New-version explanation","leftText":"Separately establishes targets and conditions while retaining old results.","rightTitle":"Rewriting old success","rightText":"Backfilling new conditions into an expired old record."},{"leftTitle":"One event outcome","leftText":"Supports a specific classification or case-specific loss.","rightTitle":"Long-term probability correctness","rightText":"Requires corresponding comparable registrations and calibration evaluation."}],"boundaries":["The five statuses are not arbitrary defensive words; judgment grounds must be reviewable.","This lesson illustrates, as an arrangement, the boundary between unresolved and cannot evaluate without turning it into the theory's sole necessary definition.","Object-identity conversion and data loss both need factual support; failure itself cannot establish either.","Reporting accuracy only among evaluable cases while hiding other statuses misleads about the full registration set."],"questions":["Why does B's valid interval [7,9] remain unresolved under this example's arrangement?","Why is destruction of logs not automatically unsupported?","May both 4/6 and 4/10 be reported for the ten items?","Are new explanations prohibited?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/figures/section-12-7.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.7","start_line":2759,"end_line":2764,"startLine":2759,"endLine":2764}],"outputId":"section-12-7","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Check target, object identity, window, material, and decision criteria under the original registration.","If material meets support rules, mark support within scope. Record complete contrary outcomes honestly as unsupported.","Mark insufficient evidence unresolved under predetermined rules; explain cannot evaluate when procedural entry is broken.","If identity converted without agreed uptake, record target invalidated rather than a false hit.","Give new explanations separate versions, retain old records, and disclose all statuses and denominators in summaries."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: five expiry records under one threshold arrangement","text":"Fictional registration: backlog B≥8 in specified unit A at the end of hour 2. The arrangement permits direct judgment from exact records with matching definitions; a valid error interval crossing 8 is unresolved; unrecoverable loss of core count data makes evaluation impossible; abolition of A without a succession definition invalidates the target. Five independent hypothetical cases are: complete B=9, supported; complete B=6, not supported; valid interval [7,9], unresolved; destroyed logs with no reconstruction, unable to evaluate; unit A abolished and merged without continuity of the original identity, target invalid. These are alternative cases, not five simultaneous outcomes in one projection.","assumptions":"All objects, numbers, agreed rules, and events are teaching constructions; the original prediction is a threshold assertion, not a probability statement.","boundary":"Actual registrations may use other explicit criteria; identity succession and credible error intervals require separate material."},{"title":"Teaching construction: changing the deadline creates a new result, not rescue of an old prediction","text":"The original registered prediction is B≥8 at t=2. Complete observation B2=6 leaves it unsupported. Later B3=9 at t=3 does not let the author change the t=2 record to success by saying “it will eventually reach 8.” New information may support a new registration for t=3 or later, but its registration time must be explicit; after knowing B3=9, it cannot be presented as a prior prediction.","assumptions":"Times and values are constructed, with no actual queue observations.","boundary":"If the original registration had predicted a 2-to-3-hour window, evaluate under that original window. Here the original target is explicitly t=2 only."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: disclose status distributions instead of counting unknowns as half-success","text":"Suppose 10 registrations resolve: 4 supported, 2 not supported, 2 unresolved, 1 target invalid, and 1 unable to evaluate. Under this construction's prespecified rules, support among the 6 binary-evaluable items is 4/6≈66.67%; supported items among all registrations are 4/10=40%. These answer different questions and both require denominators plus the other four statuses. The two unresolved items cannot each count as half a success to yield 5/10=50% labeled a hit rate, nor may the other four be hidden.","assumptions":"The ten statuses are mathematical constructions, and the binary-evaluable set is explicitly defined in the assumptions.","boundary":"The evaluable support proportion is not overall calibration, causal correctness, or real-world generalizability; unlike tasks cannot be pooled without weighting and separated definitions."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An unresolved result at resolution is scored half success and half failure.","effect":"Unresolved is an evidence status. Unless the original task has an independently justified, pre-registered scoring rule, it cannot arbitrarily be converted into hits.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The original unit was abolished and a new team met the target, so the old prediction hit.","effect":"First check identity-succession rules; without them, the target is invalid rather than an old success.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A hit in hour three proves the hour-two assertion correct.","effect":"Time belongs to the target set down in advance; deadlines cannot be changed afterward.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does B's valid interval [7,9] remain unresolved under this example's arrangement?","answer":"Threshold 8 lies within the interval, so attainment and nonattainment remain possible. The input material is valid but insufficient to distinguish them.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why is destruction of logs not automatically unsupported?","answer":"Under this demonstration arrangement, labels cannot be recovered and evaluation cannot run; absent evidence cannot be entered as a complete contrary result.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"May both 4/6 and 4/10 be reported for the ten items?","answer":"Yes, naming them respectively the support rate in the binary-evaluable subset and the supported proportion of all registrations, while disclosing other statuses.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Are new explanations prohibited?","answer":"A new version is allowed, but registration time, target, grounds, and the old failure must remain; original success eligibility cannot be rewritten.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/figures/section-12-7.png","alt":"Original assertion: B≥8 at t=2. Complete B=9: supported; complete B=6: not supported; valid interval [7,9] crosses the original threshold: unresolved under the demonstration arrangement; unrecoverable core labels indicate evaluation-input failure: unable to evaluate; original object abolished without identity succession: target invalid, not a hit; a new version may specify a new target / conditions / time while the old record remains; no post hoc changes to deadlines or scope, no half-success for unknowns, and all denominators retained in summaries. Objects, intervals, and criteria are agreed teaching constructions, not automatic judgments of actual projects.","caption":"Objects, intervals, and criteria are agreed teaching constructions, not automatic judgments of actual projects."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Future selection of different resolution material must always display the original registration, without silently changing criteria when outcomes switch.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section concerns the source's evaluation arrangements and status semantics. Individually checkable teaching constructions isolate logical boundaries; historical events lacking complete prior registrations are not forcibly classified."},"openQuestions":["Actual projects require registrations set down in advance, identity-succession rules, and resolution material before the five statuses can be judged; no actual project is currently being empirically classified."],"taskId":"S-12-7","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2555–2560; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.7: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.7　前瞻失败的含义; Chinese source lines 2711–2716; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ec68da3dc1c10ea65bef4a19066f3bcbe94207b668d79e2ce7c2e599b21bd4e9","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.8":{"title":"12.8 The Bridge from Foresight to Bounded Choice","lead":"“This may be better” can become advice for deliberation, but not directly a binding command to others. Conditional consequences, value comparisons, and actual permissions each need their own reasons.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2717–2722; original anchor: section-12-8; short quotation, translated from the Chinese: “Recommendation does not generate execution permission.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Foresight states what may happen under conditions; it does not itself decide which consequences deserve pursuit or who should bear costs. High probability strengthens judgment about a consequence without alone expanding authority over others. High average benefits also cannot cancel rights at minority positions.","Before forming an analytical recommendation, make normative premises, evidence status, affected positions, candidate options, costs and benefits, and revision conditions explicit. Reasoned local judgments and conditional advice are possible without banning all analysis because execution is not yet authorized. Clearly mark their deliberative status and unmet conditions for actual adoption.","Before actual adoption, supply the choosing subject and jurisdiction, affected and low-power positions, rights floors, options and cost-benefit distributions, authorization, stopping, appeal, restoration, and remedies. This follows the existing normative premises, protection floor, itemized authorization, operational procedures, and the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging”, not a new shortcut from “accurate prediction” to execution.","Under §14.3 (Chinese source lines 3072–3092), normative premises must be explicitly stated in each choice. “Explanation Does Not Confer Authority to Act” only prevents explanation from directly authorizing action and supplies no positive objective alone; “Protection Must Not Be Downgraded” requires protection not be downgraded; “High-Impact Judgments Must Be Correctable” requires corrigibility of high-impact judgments; “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted” opposes unlimited, inescapable unilateral sacrifice; “Persistence and Systemic Selection Do Not Establish Normative Justification” prevents treating persistence and selection as justification. Disclose selected principles (including premises stated beyond these five), whether they are objectives or constraints, and how conflicts are handled rather than silently relying on a benevolent slogan.","The ten items of the protection floor cannot freely be traded for efficiency. Section 14.8 (Chinese source lines 3172–3212) requires the ten items to connect individually to actual mechanisms, responsibilities, stopping, appeals, review, restoration, and expiry; formal checkmarks are insufficient. Human, sentient, mixed, or unknown objects cannot be declared purely technical to reduce protection. Nor does the floor exhaust substantive rights; specific rights still require independent grounds.","The scope of authorization answers who has valid grounds to perform which action on which object, within what territory and period. Section 14.6 (Chinese source lines 3134–3147) requires a single valid authorization to cover an action's full scope. Permission to “read A” and “modify B” cannot combine into “modify A.” Lawful continuation across multiple actors may hold, but each action requires checking its own permissions and input conditions.","In the four-step operational procedure (§14.6), “Fixing the Facts and Evidence” fixes description, evidence, and uncertainty; “Stating the Normative Premises and Conflicts” explicitly lists norms, burden bearers, and conflicts; “Reviewing Authorization and Comparing Options” compares options including inaction and checks authorization boundaries; “Bounded Execution and Correction” implements bounded execution with monitoring, stopping, appeal, restoration, remedies, and expiry. Under §4.14.1 (Chinese source lines 969–986), the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” checks completeness of bridging components; it neither chooses values for people nor replaces actual institutions' legal validity.","Inaction also requires evaluating consequences and responsibilities: old rules may keep causing harm, waiting may miss a protective window, and existing duties do not disappear because a model recommends no new action. Specify responsibilities for observation, protection, and reopening assessment rather than automatically presenting inaction as neutral, costless, and needing no explanation.","Localize gaps: insufficient evidence blocks corresponding judgments, unclear norms block recommendations depending on them, and insufficient authorization blocks corresponding execution. Independently established descriptions, conditional advice with explicit premises, and evidence needs may remain. Neither execute beyond authority nor refuse all grounded help because one gate fails."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Analytical recommendation","leftText":"A grounded local or conditional recommendation under explicit premises.","rightTitle":"Actual adoption","rightText":"Also requires complete protection, permissions, procedures, and correction conditions."},{"leftTitle":"High probability","leftText":"Strengthens descriptive judgment about the consequence in question.","rightTitle":"Power to compel others","rightText":"Requires independent valid authorization and justification."},{"leftTitle":"Average benefit","leftText":"An aggregate indicator of some benefit or cost.","rightTitle":"Distribution and rights","rightText":"Who bears burdens, whether refusal is possible, and which floors are not exchangeable."},{"leftTitle":"Normative bridging check","leftText":"Whether normative-bridging components are complete.","rightTitle":"Sources of values and actual authorization","rightText":"Cannot be created by the checklist itself."},{"leftTitle":"No additional action","leftText":"The proposed new action is not implemented.","rightTitle":"No continuing responsibility","rightText":"Existing duties, harms, and protective needs may remain."}],"boundaries":["Lack of authorization does not prohibit analytical recommendations for deliberation, but must prevent their treatment as executable commands.","Positive aggregate net benefit cannot alone override harm to rights at low-power positions.","Several incomplete authorization fragments for an action cannot be assembled into complete valid permission.","“Explanation Does Not Confer Authority to Act” is a negative constraint, insufficient to choose a positive proposal by itself."],"questions":["Can the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” replace choosing values?","Why may option B with average +1.5 still be rejected?","If reading A and modifying B are allowed, may A be modified?","What can still be delivered without execution authorization?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/figures/section-12-8.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.8","start_line":2765,"end_line":2770,"startLine":2765,"endLine":2770}],"outputId":"section-12-8","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Separate conditional consequences from worthwhile objectives, listing evidence and uncertainty.","Specify positive norms and protective constraints, comparing costs and benefits of candidates and inaction position by position.","Form a bounded recommendation for deliberation with revision conditions, preserving its lack of required permissions.","Before actual adoption, check the subject, itemized authorization, the protection floor, and the four-step operational procedure, then use the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” to check bridging completeness.","Execution must allow stopping, appeal, restoration, remedies, and expiry; inaction also records continuing responsibilities."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: average benefits can hide concentrated harm","text":"Fictional option A gives utility +2 to each of 9 positions and −30 to 1 low-power position: total 18−30=−12, mean −1.2. Option B gives +5 to each of 9 and −30 to the last: total 45−30=15, mean +1.5. Even with positive mean B, if −30 represents a rights harm the assumptions prohibit shifting, average benefits alone cannot justify adoption. Numerical utilities illustrate aggregation's concealment; the rights constraint is not derived from numerical magnitude.","assumptions":"Utilities, population, and rights constraints are stipulated; no actual harm measurement or proof of interpersonal utility comparability is supplied.","boundary":"Without comparable utilities, even summation cannot serve as an actual comparison. Rights constraints must be independently explicit, not added afterward because the author dislikes a result."},{"title":"Teaching construction: recommending deliberation is not changing schedules","text":"A fictional team forecasts reduced waiting under voluntary rotation. The analyst explicitly aims to reduce waiting without weakening existing rest protections, compares current arrangements, reinforcements, and voluntary rotation, and identifies who bears extra burdens and what evidence would change the judgment. The analyst may therefore recommend submitting voluntary rotation for deliberation. Without scheduling authority and implemented consent, appeal, and restoration mechanisms, they cannot directly alter actual schedules.","assumptions":"The team, objectives, and prediction are teaching constructions, with no actual personnel or organizational operation.","boundary":"Voluntariness is not treated as established. Actual consent must permit refusal and withdrawal and comply with applicable institutions; no legal judgment on particular labor rules is provided."},{"title":"Formal construction: permissions cannot be combined across objects","text":"Suppose J1 permits subject A only to read object A this week, and J2 permits that subject only to modify object B this week. The candidate action is modifying A this week. J1 mismatches the action and J2 the object, so neither fully covers it. Taking the Cartesian product of their object and action columns manufactures nonexistent permission.","assumptions":"All subjects, objects, and authorizations are formal constructions; record contents are assumed valid but limited as stated.","boundary":"This does not deny lawful workflows where multiple subjects perform different actions; each action still needs complete coverage of its own."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Prediction confidence is 99%, so anyone may be forced to bear preventive costs.","effect":"Confidence concerns conditional consequences; cost distribution, rights, and jurisdiction require separate examination.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Execution permission has not been supplied, so even conditional recommendations cannot be written.","effect":"Evidence-based deliberative recommendations with explicit norms may be written, without presenting them as authorized execution.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model recommends inaction, so protection and observation responsibilities end.","effect":"Inaction retains ongoing consequences and existing duties; evaluate them and arrange reopening conditions.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Can the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” replace choosing values?","answer":"No. First list explicit norms and conflicts; “Normative Bridging” checks corresponding bridges rather than secretly creating objectives.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why may option B with average +1.5 still be rejected?","answer":"The stipulated rights constraint prohibits shifting that harm onto the last position; the mean cannot cancel an independent constraint.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"If reading A and modifying B are allowed, may A be modified?","answer":"No. One lacks action coverage and the other object coverage; they cannot combine into new permission.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What can still be delivered without execution authorization?","answer":"Grounded descriptions, conditional recommendations with explicit norms, costs and revision conditions, and outstanding requirements for actual adoption.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/figures/section-12-8.png","alt":"Conditional consequences: evidence / uncertainty, without choosing values themselves; analytical recommendation for deliberation: explicit norms and distribution, options / costs / revision; actual-adoption conditions: subject / protection floor / itemized authorization / operational procedures, complete normative bridging; high probability creates no permission, and average benefit erases no rights; absent execution permission, conditional advice remains possible but cannot masquerade as an executable order; stopping / appeal / restoration, remedies / expiry / responsibility must be genuinely accessible. Inaction also requires checking continuing harm and duties; the diagram is a normative structure, not authorization for any actual action.","caption":"Inaction also requires checking continuing harm and duties; the diagram is a normative structure, not authorization for any actual action."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Future interaction may show which specific conclusion a missing item blocks, preserving unaffected analytical content.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section concerns normative bridging and permission logic; explicit fictional comparisons and formalized authorization suffice to illustrate boundaries. No actual decision file verifies all authorization, protection, and correction conditions, so historical success or failure is not forced into normative certification."},"openQuestions":["Actual choices still need the present norms, affected-person material, valid authorization, and evidence of accessible correction; this lesson grants no eligibility for actual adoption."],"taskId":"S-12-8","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2561–2566; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.8: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: original chapters on normative bridging","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§4.14.1, Chinese source lines 908–925; §14.3, lines 2892–2910; §14.6, lines 2940–2953; §14.8, lines 2968–3000; §14.9, lines 3002–3009","claim_support":["C12's distinction between analytical recommendation and actual adoption; N, PF, J, O definitions and responsibilities for inaction"],"limits":["Faithful explanation of theoretical normative procedures, not certification of actual institutional legal validity or specific authorization.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.8　从前瞻到有限选择的桥; Chinese source lines 2717–2722; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-8/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"37c74d922413bb0d84a47a4c717982c0adf2255e0ce642f2b0d96aa39bb52977","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.9":{"title":"12.9 The Option Set Must Include Action and Inaction","lead":"Comparing options is not finding reasons for a predetermined action. First lay out genuinely available paths worth comparing, especially retaining the no-additional-action baseline and distinguishing active maintenance of current arrangements from doing nothing.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2723–2737; original anchor: section-12-9; short quotation: “Taking no action may allow existing conditions to change on their own.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","When the user or task requests a choice, examine six types individually: maintain the status quo, act proactively, delay action, take a small exploratory step, exit or transfer, and explicitly do not act. This checks applicability, not mandatory completion of six buttons. Explain constraints where inapplicable; construct actual comparable options where applicable. Tasks may add options, while pure explanation or verification tasks need no compulsory action list.","Maintaining the status quo may require continuing resources, rules, protections, and costs. Inaction means not implementing this proposed additional action, while existing mechanisms continue changing. Under §14.4 (Chinese source lines 3094–3117), the inaction baseline retains existing maintenance, ongoing conduct, and independent obligations; “no addition” cannot be replaced with “turn off all existing protection.” Specify each difference between active maintenance and baseline rather than merely renaming.","Maintaining the status quo requires checking hidden costs, ongoing harm, and lock-in; active intervention requires checking permissions, irreversibility, and costs spilling across circles. Rapid change is not necessarily worth pursuing, and stability is not necessarily justified. Both directions must disclose normative premises and affected positions.","Delayed action is an option with a clock: specify what material or procedure is awaited, for how long, who observes, and when review reopens. Waiting itself can accumulate harm or miss an opportunity window. Missing evidence cannot justify indefinite delay without responsibility, nor can planned future execution be recorded as already having occurred.","A tentative small step must specify what is to be learned, distinguishing signals, scale, deadline, stopping, restoration, and who bears costs. Small scale means only that some quantity is small; it does not guarantee low risk or reversibility. Affected parties, privacy, irreversible information disclosure, and shifted burdens still require checks. Section 12.11 develops the detailed conditions.","Exit or transfer may end an unsuitable relationship or object, but does not automatically erase dependents, existing responsibilities, or history. Check who can exit, who cannot, who takes up the service or responsibility, and how records are preserved. Without qualified uptake, do not invent “just transfer it” to fill an option slot. Nor may exit be held hostage by unlimited responsibility.","Inaction must state the baseline time, expected path, observation plan, reopening conditions, and responsible party. Other actors, resources, and clocks continue moving; the baseline is not a still photograph. Only with this baseline can one compare what additional interference brings; otherwise “we must do something” readily becomes a hidden premise.","The six categories are not naturally mutually exclusive categories of the world. A combined option may begin with monitoring, proceed to a bounded trial, and transfer once a threshold is reached. Listing must distinguish stages, triggers, and resources, without counting the same action's benefits under multiple options or hiding execution-permission gaps behind a combined label. Real-world adoption still requires the bridging in 12.8; an option list does not itself authorize action."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Actively maintaining the status quo","leftText":"Requires explicit resources and execution to preserve the target state.","rightTitle":"No additional action","rightText":"Preserves existing processes, while the state may still change naturally."},{"leftTitle":"Applicability checks for six categories","leftText":"Explain why each category applies or does not apply.","rightTitle":"Six options that must exist","rightText":"Would force the invention of options lacking resources or permissions."},{"leftTitle":"Time-limited delay","leftText":"Waits for specified information and bears the price of waiting.","rightTitle":"Indefinite procrastination","rightText":"Sets no observation, deadline, or responsibility for reopening."},{"leftTitle":"Exit or transfer","leftText":"Specifies the scope of termination and uptake of dependencies.","rightTitle":"Responsibilities disappear","rightText":"Renaming does not automatically erase old obligations or history."},{"leftTitle":"Explanatory task","leftText":"Answers conceptual or evidential questions.","rightTitle":"Choice task","rightText":"Requires the corresponding complete set only when comparison and proposed options are requested."}],"boundaries":["An inaction baseline must not presume that existing obligations stop or that costs are absent.","Clearly inapplicable options must not be invented to fill the form.","Small steps, delay, maintenance, and exit can all have high-impact consequences; names do not guarantee safety.","Avoid double-counting a combined option's resources and benefits; candidate options are not execution authorization."],"questions":["Why does inaction in the queue yield a backlog of 8 rather than keeping it at 6?","What additional conditions are needed to maintain 6?","How should exit be stated when there is no receiving party?","What is the minimum record required for inaction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/figures/section-12-9.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.9","start_line":2771,"end_line":2785,"startLine":2771,"endLine":2785}],"outputId":"section-12-9","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Confirm whether the task requests choice; if so, check the applicability of all six categories.","Specify which ongoing obligations and maintenance the inaction baseline retains, distinguishing them from additional active maintenance.","For applicable options, state actual actions, objects, timing, resources, bearers, and boundaries.","Explain the respective risks of delay, trials, and exit; do not rely on labels to guarantee safety or feasibility.","Compare against the changing baseline in the same window; specify observation, reopening conditions, and responsibility, with permissions for adoption checked separately."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: applicability of six categories in one fictional queue","text":"A fictional service queue has backlog 6 at t=0, 2 arrivals per hour, and existing processing capacity 1 per hour; compare the next two hours. Explicit inaction preserves the original service without adding resources, yielding endpoint 8. Active maintenance adds 1 processing unit per hour to preserve backlog 6. Active improvement adds 2 per hour to the original 1, for total processing 3 and endpoint 4. Delay uses the original processing 1 in the first hour and, after approval, increases it to 3 in the second, taking the backlog to 7 then 6. A small trial increases processing to 2 in the first hour with monitoring, then handles the second hour under a predetermined rule. Exit or transfer is marked inapplicable because there is currently no verifiable receiving party; transfer capacity is not invented. Every additional option remains only a candidate.","assumptions":"The values, capacities, and observation period are all teaching constructions, with no actual service deployment. The original processing capacity 1 is an existing service; inaction does not cancel it.","boundary":"Resource costs and actual authorization have not been compared. Endpoint 4 being smallest does not justify declaring immediate implementation necessary. The trial's second hour still depends on observations and cannot be treated as a fixed endpoint."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: natural depletion reveals the difference between maintenance and inaction","text":"A purely technical fictional inventory starts at 10, loses 2 naturally each period, and has no other existing inputs. Adding no replenishment for two periods yields 8 then 6. Actively replenishing 2 each period preserves 10 then 10, but requires total additional input 4. Saying the latter option “does not change final inventory” does not mean it “involves no action or cost.”","assumptions":"Depletion, inputs, and the linear update I_next=I−2+replenishment are stipulated assumptions; no actual experiment was conducted.","boundary":"Actual inventories may involve thresholds, demand, and protection obligations. This pure construction should not be used to infer duties of care for people or organizational responsibilities."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a verifiable inaction baseline","text":"Inaction record for the queue above: baseline t=0, B=6; expected values 7 at t=1 and 8 at t=2; check arrivals and actual completions hourly; reopen option review if any complete record shows B≥7; fictional duty role R observes and records. Reopening is only review and does not automatically increase processing resources. If records are missing, mark unknown and trigger a material review; do not treat missingness as low risk.","assumptions":"The observation rules and responsible role are teaching templates only; no work has been assigned to any actual person.","boundary":"Actual responsibility must be accepted by a qualified actor with the capacity, time, and permissions. This template cannot establish that anyone has taken it up in reality."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Maintaining the status quo and inaction both ultimately seek stability, so combine them.","effect":"Maintenance may require continuous additional input, while the inaction baseline may deteriorate. Distinguish them by actual actions and resources.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"All six categories must be listed, so mark transfer feasible even without a recipient.","effect":"Checking six categories does not make six options feasible; missing uptake should be marked inapplicable or awaiting evidence.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Even explaining one concept requires six action options.","effect":"Without a request for choice, no action list is mandatory; the task determines output scope.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does inaction in the queue yield a backlog of 8 rather than keeping it at 6?","answer":"Existing processing is 1 per hour and arrivals are 2, giving a net increase of 1 each hour: from 6 to 8 over two hours.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What additional conditions are needed to maintain 6?","answer":"Increase processing to 2 per hour, adding 1 unit per hour with separate resources, authorization, and protection conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How should exit be stated when there is no receiving party?","answer":"State that it is currently inapplicable or lacks evidence of uptake; do not fabricate a feasible transfer.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What is the minimum record required for inaction?","answer":"Baseline time, expected path, observation plan, reopening conditions, and responsible party, while preserving existing obligations.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/figures/section-12-9.png","alt":"Maintain the status quo: what ongoing inputs are needed? Is there harm or lock-in? Active action: which path changes? What permissions and spillovers are involved? Delay: wait for what, and how long? Who bears the price of waiting? Small trial: learn what, and stop when? Can it be restored to its prior state and remedied? Exit or transfer: who takes over, and who cannot exit? Do not invent an option without uptake. Explicit inaction: the baseline keeps changing; observation/reopening/responsibility. Choice tasks retain an explicit inaction baseline. Explain inapplicability; an action list is not mandatory when choice was not requested.","caption":"Choice tasks retain an explicit inaction baseline. Explain inapplicability; an action list is not mandatory when choice was not requested."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram. Interaction has not been implemented; references below to selecting, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Future interactions may show reasons for applicability or inapplicability; hiding a category card must not delete the inaction baseline.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section explains option generation and baseline status. Explicit constructions hold conditions constant for the same object, avoiding presenting historical practices as genuinely available options when complete permission and resource records are absent."},"openQuestions":["Material on actual costs, permissions, service uptake, and low-power positions is absent; the demonstration options do not constitute a real-world optimal recommendation."],"taskId":"S-12-9","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, Chinese source full-text lines 2567–2581; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.9: terminology, contracts, argumentative obligations, and source-text boundaries; does not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research draft: the book's normative comparison of inaction and maintaining the status quo","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Chinese source: §14.4 lines 2912–2927; §14.9 lines 3002–3009","claim_support":["Inaction means not adding the current candidate behavior; existing maintenance and obligations must still be recorded separately; natural baseline changes and responsibility for omissions"],"limits":["Theoretical explanation; does not certify specific service obligations, uptake qualifications, or execution permissions.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.9　方案集必须包含行动与不行动; Chinese source lines 2723–2737; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-9/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"2ad072bfe6f5e718092b3321573ba74bf3b81bc399e8513f2732e159e859a216","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.10":{"title":"12.10 Unified option comparison card","lead":"A unified comparison card unifies questions, baselines, and obligations, rather than forcing every value into one total score. Whether information helps, whether actions jointly qualify, and whether resources support them simultaneously are three questions requiring separate proof.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2738–2756; source anchor section-12-10; short quotation: “retain the outcomes on multiple dimensions and disclose the conflicts.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical draft.","Compare each option against the same baseline, target, and deadline. Minimum records include the option description, foresight references, normative premises, affected parties, rights floors, expected path, worst acceptable outcome, cross-circle spillovers, cost-benefit distribution, information value, lock-in risk, reversibility, resource costs, executors, authorization, stopping, restoration, and remedies. “Large benefits and controllable risks” alone provides no reviewable comparison.","Targets and paths must state which mechanism changes. The protection floor must identify unacceptable harm; average benefits cannot substitute for it. Distribution must state who gains, who pays, and when; an aggregate net value is insufficient. Cross-circle spillovers require checking whether costs are externalized; indicators for the main circle cannot monopolize evaluation.","Information value asks whether new observations distinguish mechanisms or paths, or change an evidence-based choice, rather than whether an action is dramatic. Reversibility and lock-in ask whether stopping, withdrawal, and remedy are actually possible; verbal promises are insufficient. Permissions must identify who can decide, execute, and hear appeals, and within what valid scope; a job title or model recommendation is insufficient. Inaction still follows a changing baseline and cannot be assigned zero cost.","Without a reasonable common unit and public trade-off rules, multidimensional results should retain vectors, conflicts, and rankings under different normative premises. A model can explain which option it favors under disclosed norms and what would change that judgment, but cannot make a binding final decision for qualified actors. Forced summation can hide rights floors or silently set interpersonal utility exchange rates.","The bounded information-value formula is EVSI=E_Z[max_a E(U(a,θ)|Z)]−max_a E[U(a,θ)]. Here θ is the state, a the action, Z the information, and U a previously accepted utility with a computable expectation. The formula compares the optimal expectation when the action can respond to Z after learning it with the optimal expectation from choosing one action without Z. Utilities and probabilities need declared status; a conditional model calculation is not a verified real-world benefit.","Nonnegativity requires information to be free and ignorable, with the action set and target unchanged. If the original optimal action remains available after information is acquired, one can do at least as well as before. Actual inquiry takes time, privacy, and resources and may change the object or make actions expire, so gross EVSI is not net value. Unquantified privacy and rights costs cannot default to zero, nor may they be forcibly monetized just to use the formula.","Two clues may each leave the decision unchanged yet be valuable together, so information values need not add up item by item. Knowledge also has value for understanding independent of this action; EVSI=0 in this formula does not make research worthless. Section 12.10.1 further explains when a common action is feasible under uncertainty; 12.10.2 checks shared resources across multiple actions. The parent section's comparison card cannot replace these two tests."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Unified comparison","leftText":"Itemized records under common questions and baselines.","rightTitle":"Unified scalar","rightText":"Possible only with additional justified trade-offs and conditions on units."},{"leftTitle":"Gross EVSI","leftText":"Expected gain from allowing choice after information within a fixed model.","rightTitle":"Net value","rightText":"Also includes inquiry time, resources, privacy, and opportunity costs."},{"leftTitle":"Zero decision information value","leftText":"The information does not improve choice for the given target and actions.","rightTitle":"No knowledge value","rightText":"Values such as understanding can exist independently."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional preference","leftText":"Reasons for deliberation under explicit norms.","rightTitle":"Final binding decision","rightText":"Requires qualified actors and valid procedures."}],"boundaries":["The maximization formula does not turn utility premises into natural facts or automatically authorize information collection.","If information is not free and ignorable, gross EVSI's nonnegativity cannot establish that real-world inquiry is always beneficial.","Two individually useless clues need not be jointly useless; information values generally cannot be mechanically added.","Complete comparison-card items improve reviewability only; they do not certify factual accuracy or real-world permissions."],"questions":["What is the key inequality in the EVSI proof?","Why does observing only X give no gain in the XOR example?","What is the net value when cost is 0.6?","Why need a unified comparison card not produce a unified total score?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.10","start_line":2786,"end_line":2804,"startLine":2786,"endLine":2804}],"outputId":"section-12-10","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Complete the comparison items for the same baseline, target, and deadline, retaining each burden-bearing position.","Without a reasonable common scale, retain multidimensional results and disclose normative conflicts and conditional preferences.","For information value, first specify states, signals, actions, and utility, then state the calculation's conditional status.","Prove gross information value nonnegative when information is free and ignorable and the action set is unchanged; separately deduct corresponding real-world costs.","Check clues' joint effects, commonly acceptable actions, and shared resources before submitting the choice to a qualified procedure."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical proof: nonnegativity of bounded EVSI","text":"Assume a finite, nonempty, fixed action set and existing utility expectations; take the no-information optimal action a*. For each signal z, max_a E[U(a,θ)|Z=z]≥E[U(a*,θ)|Z=z]. Taking expectations over Z and applying the law of total expectation gives the left side ≥E[U(a*,θ)]=max_a E[U(a,θ)]; subtraction yields EVSI≥0. Being free and ignorable ensures that feasible strategies after the signal still include the fixed a*. If the action set changes, this step may fail.","assumptions":"A finite probability model, finite action set, and integrable utility are explicit conditions. This is an algebraic argument, not a real-world experiment.","boundary":"Nonnegativity concerns gross expected value. It does not show that every specific signal improves matters, guarantee positive net value for real-world collection, or establish permission."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: two individually unhelpful clues are valuable together","text":"Independent fair bits X and Y have four combinations, 00, 01, 10, 11, each with probability 1/4; state θ=X XOR Y. Action a∈{0,1} scores 1 for a correct guess and 0 otherwise. Without information, θ is uniform and the best expectation is 1/2. Observing only X or only Y leaves the other uniform, so the expectation stays 1/2 and each individual EVSI=0. Observing X and Y together allows exact calculation of θ, scoring 1 every time, so joint EVSI=1−1/2=1/2. If the two pieces of information cost 0.6 in total on the same utility scale, with nothing else changing, net value is 0.5−0.6=−0.1.","assumptions":"All distributions, utilities, and costs are stipulated; no information was actually collected.","boundary":"This does not imply that real-world information can always jointly resolve ambiguity. Cost comparability is also stipulated; actual privacy and rights constraints cannot be arbitrarily converted."},{"title":"Teaching construction: multidimensional comparison without forced summation","text":"Fictional option A reduces waiting by 2 hours but adds privacy exposure; option B adds no exposure but reduces waiting by only 1 hour. Without public privacy constraints and time trade-offs, “2−privacy points” should not be disguised as an objective total score. Report waiting, whose information is exposed, difficulty of withdrawal, the protection floor, and resources separately; then exclude A under an explicit premise that specified additional exposure is prohibited.","assumptions":"The options and effects are constructed; no actual user data is described or collected.","boundary":"Exclusion follows from the explicit constraint, not automatically from the numerical time values; compare again if the evidence changes."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"EVSI is nonnegative, so inquiry is worthwhile however expensive it is.","effect":"Nonnegativity concerns only the gross value of free, ignorable information; real-world net value can be negative.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"X and Y each have zero EVSI, so their joint EVSI must be zero.","effect":"In the XOR construction, they jointly reveal the state exactly, giving a gain of 1/2.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"If no total score can be calculated, no preference can be proposed.","effect":"A conditional preference can be offered for deliberation under explicit norms, retaining conflicts without overstepping authority to decide.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What is the key inequality in the EVSI proof?","answer":"The optimal action after the signal is at least as good as retaining a*; then apply the law of total expectation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does observing only X give no gain in the XOR example?","answer":"Y remains fair, so θ's conditional distribution remains 1/2 for each value; X alone cannot raise the expected success of guessing.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What is the net value when cost is 0.6?","answer":"Gross value 0.5 minus cost 0.6 gives −0.1, assuming cost and utility are comparable.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why need a unified comparison card not produce a unified total score?","answer":"Dimensions may be incommensurable or subject to hard rights constraints. Unified recording is sufficient; trade-offs require additional public reasons.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10.png","alt":"Paths and targets: protection/distribution/spillover, without forced totals; information value: whether it can change a grounded choice, with understanding value retained separately; reversibility/resources/permission: stopping/restoration/remedy need actual conditions checked; construction: seeing only X or Y leaves XOR θ uniform, so each EVSI=0; seeing both determines θ=X XOR Y, EVSI=0.5; same-scale cost 0.6 gives net 0.5−0.6=−0.1, so gross value differs from net value. The lower row is a mathematical construction with independent fair bits; information-value formulas grant no actual data-collection permission.","caption":"The lower row is a mathematical construction with independent fair bits; information-value formulas grant no actual data-collection permission."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Four bit combinations could later expand row by row, keeping probability, cost, and utility assumptions visible.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"Formal information value here is most directly tested by a complete proof and enumerable construction. Without a real project's full utility, probability, cost, and permission material, no empirical EVSI is fabricated."},"openQuestions":["Acceptability of actual utilities and probabilities, comparability of privacy costs, and permission to collect information require separate material and procedural judgments."],"taskId":"S-12-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2582–2612; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.10: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source F3 and the joint-resource counterexample","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§4.18.3, Chinese source lines 993–996; Appendix C.12, lines 4515–4521","claim_support":["Necessity and sufficiency of a common-action intersection, the single-round deterministic boundary, and shared-capacity counterexample."],"limits":["Formal propositions do not establish real set membership, resources, authorization, or completed execution.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.10　统一方案比较卡; Chinese source lines 2738–2756; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"ede8748d88983e7242f7f2adfe36e95cf7f3dc16d06e918dee7c4c7e73ecab10","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.10.1":{"title":"12.10.1 From identifiable answers to feasible choices","lead":"Not knowing which state holds need not prevent all action; yet each state having its own solution does not ensure that one common qualified solution can be selected now.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2757–2762; source anchor section-12-10-1; brief quotation: “This gives one positive condition under which bounded action is possible while unknowns remain.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Let the credible-state set compatible with current material be nonempty. For each state z, A_ok(z) is not an arbitrary imagined list, but qualified actions determined by public targets, rights floors, resources, and valid permission. Otherwise even a nonempty formal intersection contains only paper actions, not real feasibility.","In a bounded single-round deterministic choice, a nonempty common intersection means at least one action qualifies in every listed compatible state. These shared actions can then be compared without first identifying the actual state. This is a positive condition for bounded action with unknowns, not a requirement to wait indefinitely for perfect knowledge.","If the intersection is empty, each set separately being nonempty is insufficient. Knowing z1 would select left and z2 right, but a current representation that does not distinguish them cannot guarantee the right selection with one deterministic rule. Check whether further evidence, waiting, a narrower target, or independently grounded protective actions alter conditions; do not assume those options automatically available.","Conversely, a sufficient answer does not guarantee action. Even if z1 is identified, inadequate resources, failed protection, or missing permission may leave A_ok(z1) empty. Identification, computability, access, implementation, resources, and authority to adopt carry different responsibilities; a correct answer is not a pass for all.","The condition covers only listed credible states and constraints, without guaranteeing no omitted states. Nor does it declare worst-case criteria uniquely rational. Grounded probabilities, risk tolerance, randomization, or sequential strategies may yield other rules, but must state their own targets and conditions rather than extending this single-round deterministic result without bounds.","This matches the conditional formal proposition “Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints” in §4.18.3 (Chinese source lines 1054–1057) and Appendix C.12 (lines 4990–4994). The comparison card first defines qualification; this section proves whether a common action exists under limited information. The next tests whether multiple shared or separately feasible actions compete for one resource."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Each state has a solution","leftText":"Every A_ok(z) is separately nonempty.","rightTitle":"One common solution exists","rightText":"The intersection of all sets is nonempty, a stronger logical claim."},{"leftTitle":"State sufficiently identified","leftText":"The actual current state is known.","rightTitle":"Action feasible","rightText":"Resources, protection, and permission must also hold."},{"leftTitle":"Commonly qualified","leftText":"Meets constraints in every listed compatible state.","rightTitle":"Absolutely safe in reality","rightText":"Unlisted states and changing conditions may still occur."},{"leftTitle":"Single-round deterministic condition","leftText":"One action qualifies for all stated states.","rightTitle":"All decision theories","rightText":"Probabilistic, randomized, and sequential strategies have their own analyses."}],"boundaries":["Do not place unauthorized actions in qualified sets and then use the intersection to prove authority.","An empty intersection does not prove every form of action or strategy impossible.","Additional observation may involve cost, risk, and permission; it is not automatically available for free.","A nonempty intersection establishes common candidates, not a uniquely optimal action already chosen or executed."],"questions":["What is the intersection of {left,preserve} and {right,preserve}?","Does the proof of “Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints” verify actual authorization?","Why may unique identification of z1 still fail to permit action?","Does a nonempty intersection imply a unique optimum?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10-1.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.10.1","start_line":2805,"end_line":2810,"startLine":2805,"endLine":2810}],"outputId":"section-12-10-1","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["List a nonempty credible-state set compatible with current material, without claiming unknown states exhaust reality.","For each state, first check targets, protection, resources, and permission to form A_ok.","Compute the common intersection, publishing its members or emptiness and their bounded meanings.","A nonempty intersection permits comparison of shared options; emptiness prompts checks of further evidence, a narrower task, or independently grounded strategies.","Retain the single-round deterministic domain without excluding separately grounded probabilistic or sequential methods."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Formal proof: common action is equivalent to a nonempty intersection","text":"Necessity: if the same action a is chosen using only current representation y and qualifies for every compatible z, then a∈A_ok(z) for every z, so a belongs to the intersection. Sufficiency: if the intersection is nonempty, take any a in it; a qualifies for every z without identifying the actual z. The proof uses only set definitions, not evidence of real qualification of each set.","assumptions":"A bounded single-round deterministic task, nonempty observation class, and already determined qualified sets.","boundary":"Does not prove necessary/sufficient conditions for sequential, randomized, or risk-tolerant strategies, or certify actual permission."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a common protective option under unknown state","text":"Fictional z1 has qualified set {left,preserve}, z2 has {right,preserve}; preserve is assumed to have this task's resources, protection, and valid authorization. The intersection is {preserve}, allowing comparison and selection without knowing z1 versus z2. If preserve's permission expires and it leaves both sets, {left} and {right} remain with empty intersection. The previous version's feasibility no longer applies.","assumptions":"Action names, states, and authorization are formal stipulations; no actual preservation action occurs.","boundary":"Preserve is not a magically lawful action; failed premises require rechecking."},{"title":"Formal construction: knowing the answer may still leave no feasible action","text":"Material uniquely identifies z1, but required action left needs capacity 2 and only 1 exists; all other actions violate stipulated protection constraints. A_ok(z1)=empty, so the state is fully identifiable while the task cannot be executed. Only actual additional capacity and satisfied permission may create a new qualified set.","assumptions":"Capacity and constraints are stipulated; no actual resource request is made.","boundary":"Knowing the resource shortfall does not mean new resources have arrived or the old cutoff automatically extends."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Every state has an action, so the right action can certainly be selected now.","effect":"{left} and {right} are separately nonempty but their intersection is empty.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Since not every state is known, all bounded action must stop.","effect":"If an already qualified common action exists, identifying every state is unnecessary.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"An empty intersection proves randomized and sequential strategies useless.","effect":"This condition does not address them; analyze their respective targets and constraints separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What is the intersection of {left,preserve} and {right,preserve}?","answer":"{preserve}, provided preserve already meets resource, protection, and permission conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does the proof of “Common Feasible Actions under Information Constraints” verify actual authorization?","answer":"No. Authorization and related conditions are prior evidential responsibilities for A_ok eligibility.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why may unique identification of z1 still fail to permit action?","answer":"Insufficient resources or permission may still leave A_ok(z1) empty.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does a nonempty intersection imply a unique optimum?","answer":"No. It proves only that a commonly qualified action exists; multiple options still need comparison.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10-1.png","alt":"State z1, qualified: left,preserve; common intersection {preserve}, allowing bounded choice with unknowns; state z2, qualified: right,preserve; remove unauthorized preserve, leaving z1={left}; empty intersection, insufficient single-round deterministic guarantee, with information or strategies checked separately; remove unauthorized preserve, leaving z2={right}. Formal construction: all qualified sets require prior checks of the rights floor, resources, and valid permission; the diagram creates no preservation authorization.","caption":"Formal construction: all qualified sets require prior checks of the rights floor, resources, and valid permission; the diagram creates no preservation authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are ideas for later design only. Qualified sets could be switched to show intersections, while also displaying evidence and validity duration for each qualification.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This is a formal proposition; complete set proofs and boundary constructions are more appropriate than incomplete real material presented as permission and feasibility certification."},"openQuestions":["Actual state-set coverage, qualification constraints, and permission still require case-specific evidence; only the formal relationship is verified here."],"taskId":"S-12-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2582–2612; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.10: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source F3 and the joint-resource counterexample","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§4.18.3, Chinese source lines 993–996; Appendix C.12, lines 4515–4521","claim_support":["Necessity and sufficiency of a common-action intersection, the single-round deterministic boundary, and shared-capacity counterexample."],"limits":["Formal propositions do not establish real set membership, resources, authorization, or completed execution.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.10.1　从可识别答案到可行选择; Chinese source lines 2757–2762; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"f3f7f65fcdbafec5c3e6560bd2fbd67e7488db099ef65e34e4fa454452ffa9cc","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.10.2":{"title":"12.10.2 Joint feasibility of multiple options","lead":"Each task may claim to need just one resource, ultimately promising the same resource twice. Joint feasibility must put capacity, dependencies, personnel, stopping capability, and deadlines into one constraint table.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2763–2768; source anchor section-12-10-2; brief quotation: “each is feasible on its own but they are infeasible together.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current source; the downloadable original research extract preserves the historical draft.","Individual feasibility establishes completion when an action uses conditions alone, not enough capacity, budget, personnel, or stopping channels for simultaneous execution. Departments passing separate reviews still need joint-constraint checks if they use the same resource. Differently named resources may depend on one underlying person or platform.","The minimal counterexample has two unit tasks, each occupying the whole current period and both due by its end, with capacity only 1. Each alone needs 1≤1 and is feasible; together they need 2>1 and are infeasible. Additional capacity next period does not change this period's deadline; later completion is not timely completion of the original task.","Adding same-period capacity changes constraints. A grounded and authorized extension of one deadline can also create a feasible new option, preserving the original arrangement and revision status. If the old deadline was already missed, later successful completion does not erase the late record. Scheduling mathematics cannot erase delay harms or protections for affected people.","Resource scheduling also needs task dependencies. If premise verification, authorization, execution, and effect have serial dependencies, tool concurrency cannot make them all complete simultaneously. Actually independent reads or tasks may run in parallel, but shared write targets, common capacity, version prerequisites, and conflicts need handling rules.","Stopping capability is also a resource. If one person or channel cannot respond to several stop requests within the critical window, separate stopping promises cannot simply become joint stoppability. Restoration, remedy, and monitoring may compete for the same resources and must be checked alongside normal execution.","Task completion must reach the promised affected people and protective outcomes. First-node completion, sending a request, or interface success does not mean the last person is protected; check timing and coverage together. This connects to the end-to-end clock in 12.6, with the unit-capacity counterexample in Appendix C.12, Chinese source line 4988, directly supporting the formal boundary."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Individual feasibility","leftText":"An action using resources alone meets constraints.","rightTitle":"Joint feasibility","rightText":"All actions satisfy shared constraints together under one schedule."},{"leftTitle":"Later resource replenishment","leftText":"More may be done in the future.","rightTitle":"Original deadline met","rightText":"Required sufficient resources and actual completion at the time."},{"leftTitle":"Independent parallelism","leftText":"No prerequisite dependency and sufficient resources.","rightTitle":"Forcing serial work into concurrency","rightText":"Executes downstream work before its premises hold."},{"leftTitle":"Execution capability","leftText":"Normal action has resources.","rightTitle":"Stopping and remedy capability","rightText":"Simultaneous exceptions also require resources and actual mechanisms."}],"boundaries":["Joint-feasibility proofs depend on actual stipulated demand, capacity, and deadlines; mathematics does not certify these inputs.","Extensions require grounds and valid procedures; models cannot rewrite arrangements themselves.","Shared resources include personnel, budgets, and stopping channels as well as machine capacity.","A feasible schedule is neither completed execution nor delivery of promised protection to affected people."],"questions":["Why are demands of 1 each individually feasible but jointly infeasible under capacity 1?","What becomes true after B's deadline is lawfully moved to period 2?","Why must joint comparison list stopping channels?","Can verification, authorization, and execution all run simultaneously for speed?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10-2.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.10.2","start_line":2811,"end_line":2816,"startLine":2811,"endLine":2816}],"outputId":"section-12-10-2","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["List each action's resource use, dependencies, deadline, and actual completion endpoint.","Find shared underlying resources, personnel, and stopping channels, checking capacity jointly for every period.","After individual feasibility, separately check joint inequalities; conflicts prohibit repeated promises of the same resources.","Same-period expansion or lawful condition changes create new options; old deadline results are not rewritten.","Check serial critical paths, endpoint coverage, and stopping/remedy capability; first-node completion does not replace whole-task completion."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical construction: two current-period tasks compete for one unit of capacity","text":"Tasks A and B each need one full unit of current-period capacity and are both due at period end, with C1=1. Separately, dA=1≤1 and dB=1≤1; jointly, dA+dB=2>1, so both cannot finish on time. If current capacity becomes 2, demand 2≤2 can be scheduled. If only next-period capacity rises to 2 while both original deadlines remain, current-period tasks still cannot be met.","assumptions":"Discrete single period, indivisible unit workloads, and stipulated capacity/deadlines.","boundary":"Divisibility, different durations, or preemption would change the model; those conditions cannot be silently inserted into the original."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: a lawful new deadline permits a new schedule","text":"Continuing the example, suppose a valid procedure changes B's deadline to the end of period 2, with C1=C2=1. Schedule A in period 1 and B in period 2: each period uses 1 and meets the new respective deadline. Feasibility of the new arrangement does not complete the old arrangement requiring both this period; if lateness already occurred before revision, it remains.","assumptions":"Grounds and authorization for extension are explicitly assumed in the construction; no real extension occurs.","boundary":"Real delay may harm a party, still requiring applicable protection, rights, procedure, and remedy judgments."},{"title":"Teaching construction: stopping promises also conflict over resources","text":"Two fictional pilots run simultaneously, each requiring the same supervisor to perform a manual shutdown taking exactly 1 minute within 1 minute of receiving a stop signal. If both signals arrive together and no other stopping channel is available, one supervisor cannot perform both at once. Individual stoppability does not establish that joint operation meets the same stopping deadline.","assumptions":"Personnel, operation duration, and simultaneous signals are teaching constructions, not descriptions of a real safety system.","boundary":"Real systems may have independent automatic stopping or multiple personnel; verify these rather than assume they exist."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A and B each pass individual resource review, so joint implementation also passes.","effect":"Shared resources may be promised twice; check constraints under a common schedule.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Adding capacity next period proves this period's deadline was not missed.","effect":"Future resources cannot restore a past deadline; at most they support catching up and a new option.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Each pilot has the same supervisor, guaranteeing joint stopping.","effect":"One supervisor may be unable to fulfill several short-deadline stopping promises simultaneously.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why are demands of 1 each individually feasible but jointly infeasible under capacity 1?","answer":"Individually 1≤1, jointly 2>1; the same original deadlines do not permit borrowing from another period.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What becomes true after B's deadline is lawfully moved to period 2?","answer":"The new arrangement has a schedule with capacity 1 per period; this does not mean the old arrangement was completed on time.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why must joint comparison list stopping channels?","answer":"Concurrent faults also compete for personnel and capability; individual promises do not guarantee joint compliance.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can verification, authorization, and execution all run simultaneously for speed?","answer":"Not with sequential dependencies; only independent stages with sufficient resources and permission may run in parallel.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/figures/section-12-10-2.png","alt":"Task A requires 1 this period, individually 1≤1: feasible. Shared capacity this period is 1, while A+B demand is 2>1: jointly infeasible. Task B requires 1 this period, individually 1≤1: feasible. Increasing capacity in the same period to 2 permits a new schedule; adding only next-period capacity does not recover this period's old deadline. Lawfully changing B's deadline permits a separate second-period schedule while retaining the old record. Unit tasks and capacities are formal constructions; actual resources, deadlines, protection, and valid authority require separate verification.","caption":"Unit tasks and capacities are formal constructions; actual resources, deadlines, protection, and valid authority require separate verification."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Future controls could move task blocks between periods, but any deadline change must explicitly establish a new arrangement rather than silently making the old task on time.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section's common-capacity counterexample follows directly from public constraints; no actual resource and deadline files were obtained, so actual organizational breaches or protection failures are not invented."},"openQuestions":["Divisibility, parallelizability, resource capacity, stopping delays, and effective deadlines of actual tasks still require evidence."],"taskId":"S-12-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese full-text lines 2582–2612; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.10: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.1: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts.","12.10.2: terminology, contracts, responsibilities of argument, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts."],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: source F3 and the joint-resource counterexample","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§4.18.3, Chinese source lines 993–996; Appendix C.12, lines 4515–4521","claim_support":["Necessity and sufficiency of a common-action intersection, the single-round deterministic boundary, and shared-capacity counterexample."],"limits":["Formal propositions do not establish real set membership, resources, authorization, or completed execution.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.10.2　多项方案的联合可行性; Chinese source lines 2763–2768; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"071de6227652898b5fc1c20113003460bbd798f5c2f759a713b22449a00769b1","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.11":{"title":"12.11 Action ceilings and informative trials","lead":"Actions undertaken to learn still affect others. A small trial, good evidence, or benevolent intentions cannot alone determine the action ceiling; authority, protection, and actual reversibility must hold together.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2769—2782; source anchor section-12-11; short quotation: ‘A trial to obtain information still carries responsibility, however small the action’. The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Evidence, reversibility, authority, and protection jointly constrain the action ceiling; they cannot be combined into a score that lets strengths offset hard gaps. Strong evidence without authority still prohibits autonomous execution; clear authorization with failed protection also prohibits business as usual. With weak evidence, high risk, irreversibility, or insufficient authority, one may turn to grounded observation, smaller actions, better information, protection, appeals, or no action for now.","The source table's first category: weak evidence with low harm, reversibility, and clear authorization permits considering limited observation or exploration. This is only a ceiling example, not automatic permission; learning questions, objects, affected people, and protections still need explanation. Second: moderate evidence, controllable harm, and the ability to restore the prior state permit considering phased action and strict stopping conditions; ‘moderate’ cannot be an unexplained label.","Third: strong evidence but insufficient authority requires submission to an authorized party, not independent execution. Fourth: strong evidence but irreversibility and unmet protection require pausing and completing procedures and safeguards; ‘proven effective’ cannot exempt low-power positions from protection. Fifth: an urgent safety threat permits only minimal protective action under existing external emergency authorization, followed by prompt review. The word emergency does not let a model create authority.","An informative trial must specify its object, affected people, minimum scope, reversibility, stopping, data uses, and remedies. It should also state mechanisms to distinguish, success or failure criteria, observation period, and whether later expansion requires renewed approval; otherwise ‘learning’ may merely package a predetermined action. Producing data does not automatically make a trial worthwhile.","Low-power positions cannot be treated as calibration material. Make visible who bears exposure, failure, and record risks, who may refuse, who benefits, and who may appeal and withdraw. Formal consent checkboxes accompanied by loss of critical resources, retaliation, or inability to exit do not constitute the genuine voluntariness required here.","Reversibility must distinguish object state, information dissemination, and consequences. Restoring a setting guarantees neither recall of leaked information nor repair of missed opportunities or harms. The ability to restore the prior state also requires performers, resources, time limits, verification, and remedy, not merely ‘it can be undone anytime’.","A trial's scope should comprise the minimum proportionate action sufficient to answer the question and must not fall below protection requirements. If downsizing prevents discrimination among mechanisms, state the lack of identification power honestly; do not repair methodological defects through unsafe large-scale expansion. Simulation, existing materials, or nonimplementation are alternatives, but their ceilings of support must be explicit.","Stopping gates must be defined beforehand and actually triggerable. When monitoring detects anomalies, failed exit, expired authority, distorted materials, or harmed rights floors, stop the corresponding part under applicable procedures and check consequences. Pilot success does not automatically authorize rollout; broader information use, changed objects, or extended periods all require renewed checks."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Small scale","leftText":"A smaller quantity such as headcount or duration.","rightTitle":"Low risk and reversible","rightText":"Requires checking the nature of effects, propagation, exit, and remedy."},{"leftTitle":"Strong evidence","leftText":"A descriptive or effect judgment has relatively good support.","rightTitle":"Sufficient authority","rightText":"The party has independent valid grounds for the specific action scope."},{"leftTitle":"Restorable settings","leftText":"Technical parameters can be changed back.","rightTitle":"Reversibility of consequences","rightText":"Information, opportunities, and harms also require separate examination."},{"leftTitle":"Genuine voluntariness","leftText":"Ability to understand, refuse, and exit without improper penalties.","rightTitle":"Formal consent","rightText":"A checkbox or silence does not automatically satisfy it."},{"leftTitle":"Pilot passed","leftText":"A bounded scope and target receive support.","rightTitle":"Rollout authorization","rightText":"Changed objects, scale, periods, and uses require rechecking."}],"boundaries":["The table gives action-ceiling examples, not approval of any actual trial.","Urgent threats do not give AI or analysts authority they did not previously have.","Collecting more data may itself cause irreversible harm; information value does not waive protection.","No actual human or reader trial was performed; teaching plans cannot be written as measured effects."],"questions":["If J is false and the other three conditions true, may action proceed?","Does pilot success automatically extend to the entire population?","Who supplies authority in an emergency?","Can a draft using an isolated copy establish shorter actual waits?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/figures/section-12-11.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.11","start_line":2817,"end_line":2830,"startLine":2817,"endLine":2830}],"outputId":"section-12-11","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["State separate grounds for evidence strength, risk, and reversibility; do not offset authority or protection through an overall score.","Choose an output ceiling proportionate to the conditions; without execution authority, hand off rather than act independently.","For informative trials, state objects, learning targets, minimum scope, data uses, and affected positions.","Check actual refusal, exit, stopping, restoration, and remedy capabilities; protection cannot shrink because the trial does.","Limit results to the original scope; review rollout or changed uses separately, and rely only on valid external authority in emergencies."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a trial draft operating only on an isolated copy","text":"A fictional system proposes comparing two queue-ordering rules. The draft limits the trial to 10 artificially generated tasks containing no personal information, an isolated environment, two hours, and read-only source data, with no production actuators connected. It preregisters comparisons of mean and maximum wait, stopping on any production connection, actual personal data, missing logs, or expiry. Results evaluate simulated rules only, not actual service effects. With no actual authorization record or run, this is merely a design for deliberation, not a claim of execution or approval.","assumptions":"Objects and tasks are teaching assumptions with no actual user materials; isolation and absence of personal information are also proposed conditions, not measured findings.","boundary":"Even a low-risk design requires environment and authority verification before implementation; actual production rollout requires renewed evaluation of populations, protection, and resources."},{"title":"Formal construction: strong evidence cannot fill an authority gap","text":"Let evidence support E, reversibility R, authority J, and protection P all be applicable hard conditions, with action qualification requiring E and R and J and P. Even with E=true, R=true, and P=true, J=false makes the conjunction false. Averaging the four to 0.75 and imposing threshold 0.7 wrongly passes an action that authority should veto. This calculation shows only that hard conditions cannot be averaged.","assumptions":"Truth values and gate logic are explicit constructions, not a reduction of actual evidence strength to binary facts.","boundary":"Actual judgments may include unknown and not-applicable statuses, with reasons required; this example supplies no universal scoring system sufficient for every task."},{"title":"Teaching construction: a restore button does not recall disclosure","text":"A fictional pilot displays a sensitive record to outside viewers, then removes it from the interface. Restoring display state does not guarantee that viewers forget, screenshots disappear, or later effects are eliminated; deletability of the interface alone cannot make the pilot fully reversible.","assumptions":"A construction of information-propagation boundaries only; no actual sensitive materials were disclosed.","boundary":"This does not presume that all informational trials are impermissible; exposure limits, valid consent, and proportionate protection are needed beforehand, rather than substituting a button for remedy afterward."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Only one person is tested, so protection and exit are unnecessary.","effect":"A small headcount does not shrink that person's rights, and effects may remain irreversible.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Evidence is very strong, so act first and find an authorized person to sign later.","effect":"Authority is an independent gate that predictive quality cannot retroactively fill.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The interface can be restored, so the prior state can be restored for every consequence.","effect":"Information propagation and harms already incurred require independent checking and remedy.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"If J is false and the other three conditions true, may action proceed?","answer":"Not under this construction's hard conditions; an average score cannot offset an authority gap.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does pilot success automatically extend to the entire population?","answer":"Not automatically. Changed scale, objects, uses, and periods require renewed checks of protection, evidence, and authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Who supplies authority in an emergency?","answer":"Authority comes from applicable external emergency authorization; neither this section nor the model can issue it itself. Action is limited to minimal protection followed by prompt review.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can a draft using an isolated copy establish shorter actual waits?","answer":"No; it has not run. Even a future successful simulation would support only the corresponding model conditions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/figures/section-12-11.png","alt":"Evidence and risk: what is supported and missing; high confidence creates no authority. Authority and protection: object/scope/period, actual refusal and exit. Reversibility and stopping: state restoration ≠ vanished consequences; remedy must be accessible. Proposed minimum trial: object/question/metrics, data use/period. Any missing hard condition: narrow, pause, or hand off rather than fill with an average score. Results remain within the original scope; rollout and changed uses require renewed conditions. This is an unimplemented teaching design and boundary diagram; emergency protection also requires valid external authorization.","caption":"This is an unimplemented teaching design and boundary diagram; emergency protection also requires valid external authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Future views could show each gate's evidence and unknown status, without automatically passing a trial through one overall percentage.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section focuses on trial qualification and responsibility boundaries. With no complete actual trial authorization or affected-person materials, it uses an explicitly unimplemented draft and logical constructions rather than posing as a human or system experiment."},"openQuestions":["Any actual trial still requires checks of authority, protection, exit, environmental isolation, data uses, and stopping resources; there is currently no implementation authorization or actual run result."],"taskId":"S-12-11","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 2613—2626 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["12.11: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.11　行动上限与信息性试验; Chinese source lines 2769–2782; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-11/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"cff3b576a638aef4baa2011645ea719356dc16cda14195dd250bef48f69f412f","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.12":{"title":"12.12 Result write-back and model learning","lead":"Learning is not revising an old prediction to look as though the answer was known all along. Actual updating preserves the original judgment, actual result, deviation, and consequences, then explains what the next version changes and on what grounds.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2783—2788; source anchor section-12-12; short quotation: ‘results of foresight and choice may only be appended in write-back’ (translated from Chinese). The current source appears in the corresponding subsection on this page; the downloadable original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Result write-back is append-only, keeping original targets, probabilities or rankings, horizons, path conditions, and choice reasons traceable. New records link the original prediction and action arrangement, recording observation time, source, actual outcome, path match, calibration metrics, and simple-baseline comparisons. Corrections may take effect as a new version, but cannot make old errors disappear from history.","Write-back also includes unexpected effects, review of affected positions, model updates, downgraded assertions, retired variables or paths, and the next review. Even no change must distinguish stable evidence, a deadline not yet reached, missing materials, and monitoring not performed; a blank cannot replace a status explanation.","Action outcomes cannot be assessed solely by the main objective. Shorter local waits may accompany greater burdens in another circle; lower overall costs may shift costs to people unable to exit; good short-term effects may conceal long-term harm. Side effects, cross-circle spillovers, distribution, appeals, and long-term clocks all enter write-back without waiting for them to affect the main metric.","One success supports only the corresponding object, conditions, and window, not a universal mechanism. One failure may arise from execution deviations, but those need independent process records and judgment under the registration and arrangements set down in advance about which conclusion failed or remains untested. After bad outcomes, an ad hoc ‘implementation was inadequate’ cannot protect the model from every counterexample.","Distinguish three questions: did the registration predicting actual outcomes fail its score; was the mechanism prediction tested with its explicit conditions satisfied; and did the selected action occur as required? These can simultaneously have different statuses. Failure of an actual-outcome prediction cannot be erased because a condition did not materialize; an untested conditional mechanism is not confirmed either.","Model learning should specify changes such as parameter reestimation, downgrading a mechanism, removing a variable, narrowing applicability, or retiring a path, tied to supporting materials and a validation plan. Tuning on one batch of results and then improving on that same batch is only in-sample improvement, not proof that the new version has future predictive gain; later evaluation still requires setting it down in advance under the corresponding design.","If original data are corrected, append reasons, sources, and affected scope, specifying which metrics and downstream conclusions need recalculation. Preserve the version relationship between original and corrected scores so readers do not think errors never occurred. Data correction and model revision are different changes and should not merge into one untraceable ‘update’."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Append updates","leftText":"Preserve old judgments and results and establish a new version.","rightTitle":"Overwrite history","rightText":"Erase old failures with new answers."},{"leftTitle":"Execution deviation","leftText":"Process materials establish that implementation did not follow original conditions.","rightTitle":"Post hoc rescue","rightText":"An unsupported exemption is added ad hoc for a bad result."},{"leftTitle":"Better in-sample tuning","leftText":"Closer fit to outcomes already seen.","rightTitle":"Future gain established","rightText":"Still requires corresponding independent or prospective evaluation."},{"leftTitle":"Main objective succeeded","leftText":"The prespecified metric meets its conditions.","rightTitle":"The action is appropriate overall","rightText":"Side effects, distribution, protection, and long-term impacts still require checking."},{"leftTitle":"Condition untested","leftText":"Necessary conditions did not occur, preventing testing of that conditional assertion.","rightTitle":"Model correct","rightText":"Lack of counterevidence cannot count as positive evidence."}],"boundaries":["Old results must remain; correcting labels also cannot erase the history of source errors.","Implementation explanations require support from the registration set down in advance and from process records; the model cannot change arbitrarily with results.","One good result certifies no universal mechanism, and recalculation after tuning does not automatically constitute independent validation.","Adverse consequences for affected positions cannot be deleted because the main objective improved."],"questions":["Why is A's error 4 in the construction while B remains untested?","What are the MAEs of P and Q?","When correcting labels, should only the latest score remain?","What else must model learning minimally record?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/figures/section-12-12.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.12","start_line":2831,"end_line":2836,"startLine":2831,"endLine":2836}],"outputId":"section-12-12","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Link the prediction set down and the choice record, appending the actual outcome's time, source, label status, and path match.","Compute performance and baseline comparisons under original definitions, retaining failures and unresolved results.","Check evidence of actual execution and satisfied conditions, distinguishing prediction, mechanism, and execution responsibilities.","Record side effects, spillovers, distribution, appeals, and long-term consequences by position.","Use a new version to state model changes, downgrades or retirement, and the next review; reject in-sample self-certification."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: failed actual-outcome prediction and untested conditional mechanism can coexist","text":"Fictional registration A predicts backlog 4 after two hours, assuming processing of 3 per hour. Registration B separately and explicitly states the conditional mechanism: ‘if actual processing is 3 per hour and arrivals 2, backlog falls from 6 to 4’. Complete end-period logs show actual processing remained 1, arrivals were 2, and the endpoint was 8. A's actual-outcome prediction error is |4−8|=4; B's processing condition did not occur, so its conditional effect was not tested this time. B's untested status cannot be written as correct, nor can A's error be erased.","assumptions":"Both registrations, logs, and numbers are teaching constructions, with no actual system data.","boundary":"If A was originally also an explicit conditional assertion, evaluate it under its original registration; do not switch between A's and B's identities after results."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: appended records preserve both model and baseline","text":"Construct three point predictions set down in advance, P=(5,7,9), simple baseline Q=(6,6,6), and actual Y=(6,8,10). P has MAE=(1+1+1)/3=1; Q has MAE=(0+2+4)/3=2, a within-sample difference of 1. After seeing results, a new version adds 1 to each prediction, yielding zero error on the same three items; this does not establish future MAE=0. Preserve original P/Q, Y, and errors, and separately register the new version and next evaluation plan.","assumptions":"All three vectors are mathematical constructions; no model training or actual prediction experiment occurred.","boundary":"Three items give extremely limited support; their MAE difference cannot establish stable superiority across contexts, and the new version cannot use seen labels as independent tests."},{"title":"Teaching construction: improving the primary measure still requires recording cost transfers","text":"A fictional service adjustment cuts A’s average wait from 10 to 7, while B takes 2 extra uncompensated work hours per person and some cannot exit. Write-back must retain A’s improvement and B’s burdens, expression, and appeal status while checking normative and protection conditions, not merely announce ‘waiting fell 30%’.","assumptions":"Groups, durations, and burdens are stipulated, with no actual labor or service event.","boundary":"These numbers alone establish neither actual unlawfulness nor an appropriate choice; normative constraints and effective institutions require separate checks."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The new model has zero error on old data, so future reliability is established.","effect":"Seen labels can produce in-sample fit; evaluate future performance separately.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Failure must be the executor’s fault; the model cannot be wrong.","effect":"Execution deviations need independent records; model conditions, mappings, and estimates may also be wrong.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The primary target improved, so other effects are not model outcomes.","effect":"Actual choices’ side effects, spillovers, distribution, and appeals must all be written back.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why is A's error 4 in the construction while B remains untested?","answer":"A predicted actual 4, observed 8; B explicitly depended on actual processing 3, while records show only 1. The two registrations ask different questions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What are the MAEs of P and Q?","answer":"P is 1, Q is 2; a bounded difference on the same three items guarantees no future advantage.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"When correcting labels, should only the latest score remain?","answer":"Append corrections and sources, preserve version relations between old and new scores, and recheck dependent items.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What else must model learning minimally record?","answer":"Specific changes, grounds, downgrades or retirements, applicability, and the next review—not merely ‘optimized’.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/figures/section-12-12.png","alt":"Prediction and choice set down: target/conditions/horizon, preserve original versions; append actual outcomes: observation times/sources, execution and label status; evaluate under original definitions: paths/measures/baselines, side effects and distribution; new-version changes: grounds/downgrading/retirement, next review; do not overwrite failures or arbitrarily rescue models with execution explanations; zero tuning error on the same batch is not zero future error, and new evaluation is set down separately. Append-only evaluation logic; the teaching example contains no actual training, prediction, or intervention execution.","caption":"Append-only evaluation logic; the teaching example contains no actual training, prediction, or intervention execution."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Future interaction could expand links between old forecasts, original scores, and corrected versions rather than display only the latest best row.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section requires complete forecasts set down in advance, actual execution, and outcome archives. None is supplied here, so reproducible constructions illustrate registration and version responsibilities without fabricating model-validation results."},"openQuestions":["Actual outcome and execution evidence are unspecified, so no judgment can be made about which actual model parameter to update or whether to retire it."],"taskId":"S-12-12","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2627–2632; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["12.12: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.12　结果回写与模型学习; Chinese source lines 2783–2788; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"bdfe5ade3dcf386adf82ed03c43ec10d4645db226280e38daa4a4f849fd0afaa","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.13":{"title":"12.13 A Complete Foresight–Choice Example","lead":"The public-resignation event is a running example in the book, not a verified corporate investigation. This guide keeps it a teaching construction, connecting conditional projection, foresight registration, normative comparison, and execution eligibility step by step.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2789–2796; source anchor section-12-13; short quotation: ‘Do not declare success by picking an intermediate signal that happened to match.’ See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloadable original research excerpt retains the historical draft.","The book’s §10.11 (Chinese source lines 2349–2356) constructs a joint object of employee, team, management, family care, professional community, and platform; §11.11 (2576–2585) supplies propagation and competing paths. This section further states a three-month target and the choice of options. Object relations remain teaching assumptions; a public resignation text cannot directly diagnose personality, read family members’ minds, or certify circle gain.","The target may ask whether the team’s voluntary turnover rate over the next three months exceeds the threshold set down at registration, set with reference to historical and comparable-team baselines, with an input cutoff one week after the public statement. The threshold must be set down at registration, including membership denominator, window, voluntary-departure definition, transfer/layoff classification, and missingness handling, as well as the data source, who compiles the figures, and the margin within which the result is recorded as “unresolved.” The original only gives the example of one and a half times the higher of the two baselines and no baseline values; the examples below are checkable teaching additions, not a statistical test already in the original.","The simple baseline uses only historical turnover and working-hour trends; the multi-circle candidate adds team trust, management response, professional-community bridges, family care, and platform exposure. Both need the same information cutoff, target, outcome definitions, and predetermined evaluation rules to compare whether extra objects and information are worthwhile. Complexity cannot peek at endpoint material or delete hard-to-predict members.","Three paths retain conditional status: P1 external attention fades while internal problems persist, requiring unimproved working hours/appeals and internal-expression risks; P2 institutional adjustment and gradual repair, requiring actual resources and procedures, with trust potentially recovering more slowly than hours; P3 professional bridging lowers exit costs and consecutive departures occur, requiring reachable opportunities and actual bridges. They may coexist at different positions and are not forced into mutually exclusive outcomes summing to probability 1.","Early signals include actual hours, appeal handling, safe member expression, hiring, and external opportunities. Contrary signals include announced policy without execution and public quiet alongside increased internal silence. Silence has multiple explanations; check expressive safety and data formation rather than directly infer satisfaction or fear. Family-condition changes reach team judgments only with bridging evidence.","After three months, compare complex model and simple baseline against the target set down in advance. Increased hiring, reduced post attention, or one member’s transfer midway may support only a local link, not establish overall foresight success. Append actual outcomes, missingness, response-regime changes, and path explanations separately; model gain needs comparable long-term evaluation.","Bounded choice enters the normative layer. This guide explicitly adopts, from the five normative premises (§14.3), “Protection Must Not Be Downgraded”, “High-Impact Judgments Must Be Correctable”, “Love and Undertaking Must Not Be Conscripted” (no imposed unlimited inescapable burdens), and “Persistence and Systemic Selection Do Not Establish Normative Justification” (team survival must not override justification), with “Explanation Does Not Confer Authority to Act” preventing direct authorization from explanation. The teaching objective is additionally explicit: reduce unbearable burdens and improve appeal access. This objective lies outside the five and is put forward by this guide as a teaching stipulation, not selected by the framework for the user; in practice it must be put forward and stated by the subjects who propose and adopt an option. Retention is only one consequence, and preventing every voluntary departure is not the goal.","Compare the book’s seven options: maintain the status quo; publicity alone; immediate resource adjustment; a reversible work-hours and appeals pilot; delay major restructuring to gather information; allow member transfers; and take no additional structural action while strengthening protection and observation. Check each option’s authority, people unable to exit, cost distribution, cross-circle reputation and family effects, stopping, and remedies. The last combines ‘no primary structural action+independent protective observation’, not complete inaction. Retain a separate explicit baseline adding no candidate action while honoring existing obligations.","Under these teaching premises and without actual execution authorization, the current analytical recommendation is to prioritize submitting a bounded work-hours and appeals-improvement pilot for deliberation while delaying irreversible major restructuring. Do not implement before protection and resource conditions are checked. It directly addresses listed burdens and expression mechanisms and is easier to monitor and revise. The recommendation assumes neither safety, voluntariness, approval, nor causal effectiveness of the pilot.","Objections and costs must remain: the pilot may impose extra appeal work, create an appearance of short-term improvement, or lack identification power in a small sample; waiting may prolong harm. Strong evidence of serious continuing harm, existing valid authority, and a safe resource plan may prioritize immediate resource protection. If refusal, exit, and anti-retaliation cannot be secured, pause the pilot and turn to proportionate external review or transfer of protective needs. Qualified recipients and the person’s choice require re-comparing transfer options."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"The book’s running example","leftText":"A teaching narrative demonstrating joint objects and procedures.","rightTitle":"An actual company case","rightText":"Needs checkable sources, facts, and authorization material."},{"leftTitle":"Operational threshold","leftText":"A threshold stipulated in advance at registration.","rightTitle":"Statistical significance","rightText":"Needs appropriate models, tests, and uncertainty, not a threshold alone."},{"leftTitle":"Intermediate-signal support","leftText":"Suggests a path segment may hold.","rightTitle":"An overall three-month hit","rightText":"Requires evaluation against the original target and baseline."},{"leftTitle":"Team retention","leftText":"One observable consequence.","rightTitle":"Preventing voluntary departures","rightText":"Not a normative goal endorsed by this guide."},{"leftTitle":"Temporarily no primary structural action","leftText":"May coexist with independent protection and observation.","rightTitle":"Baseline with no new action at all","rightText":"Its actual actions and responsibilities must be separately explicit."}],"boundaries":["No actual employee, company, or family material exists here; this is neither an actual organizational investigation nor successful prediction.","Added thresholds, proportions, and option details are teaching constructions, not empirical facts from the book.","Public silence is not directly restored trust, and one local signal is not an overall hit.","The current recommendation is for deliberation only; actual pilots, resource adjustments, and personnel transfers need independent eligibility."],"questions":["Why does the constructed threshold require at least 4 people?","Is proportion point prediction 0.25 an event probability of 25%?","Why have a preferred option without executing it now?","Is the book’s final option complete inaction?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/figures/section-12-13.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.13","start_line":2837,"end_line":2844,"startLine":2837,"endLine":2844}],"outputId":"section-12-13","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Carry forward the teaching object, setting down information cutoff, three-month target, and judgeable definitions.","Compare simple and multi-circle candidates on the same target/cutoff, retaining three paths and contrary evidence.","Observe early signals without treating intermediate hits as endpoints; score and append at the endpoint under the registration.","Disclose norms and positive objectives, comparing distribution and protection for all seven options plus the explicit baseline.","Give a preference for deliberation, reasons for rejection, costs, and revision conditions; record real adoption authority separately."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: setting down the threshold at registration","text":"Construct public statement t0, registration/cutoff one week later, and a prediction window of three calendar months after registration. Fix the initial 20 team members as denominator, fully following their voluntary departures, excluding newcomers from this cohort and classifying transfers and layoffs separately in advance. Let comparable historical three-month rate be 5% and matched-team rate 10%. Set the threshold at registration at one and a half times the higher of the two, namely 15%; exceeding it in the fixed cohort of 20 means at least 4/20 (20%). This is an operational threshold, not a significance test. If a key member’s outcome cannot be followed, report unresolved or bounded scope under pre-registered missingness rules, never zero-fill.","assumptions":"20 people, baseline rates, threshold, and definitions are stipulated without actual personnel data; the fixed-cohort premise allows complete follow-up.","boundary":"Actual comparability of historical and matched groups needs checks of selection, size, period, and definitions; naming does not establish it."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: intermediate posts cannot replace endpoint evaluation","text":"Suppose both candidates register three-month fixed-cohort turnover-proportion point predictions at cutoff: simple baseline 0.15, multi-circle candidate 0.25. A complete teaching endpoint record is 5/20=0.25, giving absolute errors 0.10 and 0; the preceding threshold event also occurs. Here 0.15 and 0.25 predict turnover proportions, not event probabilities, so binary Brier cannot be substituted. This single construction proves neither lasting multi-circle gain nor causality of any added variable.","assumptions":"Every prediction and outcome is a paper value; no actual three-month forecast was registered or executed.","boundary":"Midway declining exposure does not enter this endpoint score; changed targets or membership definitions require preserving the original registration and evaluating separately."},{"title":"Teaching construction: complete option comparison without invented execution eligibility","text":"Explicit baseline C0 adds no candidate action while retaining existing pay, maintenance, and protection obligations. C1 active maintenance needs extra resources and may prolong burdens, so is not currently preferred. C2 publicity alone cannot meet this guide’s objective without work-hours and appeal implementation and is excluded as a sole option. C3 immediate resource adjustment may directly reduce burdens but lacks resource/distribution evidence and remains to be checked. C4 a bounded work-hours and appeals pilot is the current deliberative preference, subject first to protection, effective exit, independent review, and resource conditions. C5 delaying major restructuring avoids lock-in but requires time-bounded observation and ongoing-harm review. C6 transfers need personal choice, qualified uptake, and review of burdens on those remaining; absent recipient evidence, they are not executable now. C7 no added structural action with stronger protective observation may be a transitional combination, with each new protective action checked on independent grounds. Every option records cost bearers, family and professional-reputation effects, stopping, and remedies, not retention alone.","assumptions":"All options are teaching constructions; the preference follows explicit norms and bounded evidence, not actual authorization or certified optimality.","boundary":"Withdraw C4’s preference if it cannot protect exiters or lacks discriminatory power; serious ongoing harm or an existing valid resource plan may prioritize C3/independent protection, requiring renewed comparison."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Platform attention fell, so the three-month team-turnover forecast already succeeded.","effect":"Attention is only an intermediate signal; endpoint targets and simple-baseline comparison remain unfinished.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Employees stayed, so coercion and burdens do not exist.","effect":"Inability to exit, resource lock-in, and expression risks may coexist; behavior is not consent.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The pilot is preferred, so change working hours and appeal rules now.","effect":"This is a deliberative preference; without authority and protection it cannot become execution.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does the constructed threshold require at least 4 people?","answer":"The threshold is one and a half times the higher baseline of 10%, namely 15%; in the fixed cohort of 20, 3/20=15% does not exceed it, while 4/20=20% does.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is proportion point prediction 0.25 an event probability of 25%?","answer":"No. It predicts a turnover proportion; the probability of ‘proportion exceeds 15%’ is a separate object.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why have a preferred option without executing it now?","answer":"Explicit norms and conditions permit deliberative advice, while actual authority, resources, exit, and protection remain incompletely checked.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is the book’s final option complete inaction?","answer":"No. It combines no primary structural action with added protective observation; an explicit baseline and grounds for each action remain separate.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/figures/section-12-13.png","alt":"Teaching object and cutoff: register one week after publication, target the next three months; three parallel paths: internal persistence/gradual repair/external-bridge exit; matched endpoint evaluation: simple versus multi-circle, no intermediate-signal substitute; explicit norms and distribution: seven options+baseline, reachable protection and exit; conditional deliberative preference: bounded work-hours/appeals pilot, objections/costs/revision; real execution separately checked: resources/authority/procedure, currently unimplemented. The whole diagram develops the book’s teaching example, not an actual company investigation, personnel prediction, or execution authorization.","caption":"The whole diagram develops the book’s teaching example, not an actual company investigation, personnel prediction, or execution authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the selections, switches, or clicks described below are ideas for a later design only. Future interaction may expand option reasons and revision conditions without connecting a preference button by default to real organizational operations.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original is a teaching example without an identifiable company or primary archive; this guide retains that status and discloses added assumptions, rather than pretending it is an actual labor or organizational case."},"openQuestions":["Actual company facts, member definitions, comparable baselines, normative choices, and execution authority are absent; there are no real three-month results or pilot effects."],"taskId":"S-12-13","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full text, lines 2633–2640; see sourceRefs for each section’s exact range","claim_support":["12.13: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; no empirical facts are certified on this basis"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research draft: the book’s joint-object and cascade examples of public resignation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§10.11, Chinese source lines 2195–2202; §11.11, lines 2420–2429","claim_support":["Earlier material on this teaching event’s joint object, dual channels, clocks, competing paths, and signals"],"limits":["An unnamed teaching narrative without actual primary archives, certifying no actual personnel states or company facts.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.13　一个完整的前瞻—选择示例; Chinese source lines 2789–2796; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-13/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7f78db77b3ac93e535fff643736aeea6e116b4b52c695904c876d8e7bf4f6597","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.14":{"title":"12.14 When to Choose Not to Act","lead":"Not adding a main action does not reduce responsibility to zero. Ask both whether proceeding crosses boundaries and who continues suffering if action stops here. The answer may defer the main action while retaining independently grounded protection and observation.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2797–2802; original anchor section-12-14. Short quotation: “Taking no action can be prudent or evasive.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Seven conditions support current inaction or protective observation only: unclear objects and facts; irreversible action with insufficient evidence; missing authority; unmet protection floors; possible cost transfer to those unable to exit; simple and complex models both unable to distinguish paths; and possible disruption of an existing recovery process by external intervention. These justify proportionate pauses, not universally stopping all work.","Each reason should identify the action it blocks. Unclear objects call for object checks; missing material for minimal distinguishing observation; missing authority for referral to a qualified subject; irreversibility and protection gaps may require narrower scope or added procedures. Independently grounded analysis and conditional recommendations can still be completed rather than reverting to vague questions because execution is unauthorized.","Conditions requiring stricter review of inaction are equally concrete: ongoing harm; reinforcement of entrenched advantage by omission; explicit duties or commitments of responsible subjects; low-power positions unable to exit; irreversible lock-in from delay; or endless postponement in the name of waiting for evidence. New-action risks cannot be considered while setting continuing baseline consequences to zero.","“Stricter review” does not automatically authorize the original main action. Large restructuring may be deferred while appeals, accessible information, minimum protection, or temporary uptake are independently supported. Each auxiliary action also has an object, subject, scope, duration, and authority; protection cannot conceal expanded jurisdiction or compelled disclosure.","Inaction should have a time limit, observation responsibilities, reopening conditions, and channels for affected people to speak. Specify facts that would change the pause, mandatory review times, and next steps if material remains missing. Repeated unsupported extensions can turn initially reasonable waiting into a structure for escaping responsibility.","If intervention may disrupt existing recovery, first establish grounds for the recovery mechanism and clock and specify scope. Brief improvement or apparent quiet does not prove self-recovery, and “do not interrupt recovery” cannot block appeals or exit. Contrary signals require reevaluating waiting costs.","Inaction and protection outside the main action are not contradictory because their scopes differ. “Do not implement this candidate restructuring; continue existing services and add observation under independent authority” is more precise than “do nothing.” Continuing protection must be realistically bearable, not silently assigned to those least able to refuse."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Prudent deferral","leftText":"Specific gaps block specific actions, with review conditions.","rightTitle":"Avoiding responsibility","rightText":"Emphasizes uncertainty without accounting for ongoing harm and duties."},{"leftTitle":"Not taking the main action","leftText":"Pauses one specified candidate behavior.","rightTitle":"Stopping all protection","rightText":"Existing or independently grounded protection is not canceled thereby."},{"leftTitle":"Strict review of inaction","leftText":"Seriously compares harms and distribution of waiting.","rightTitle":"Automatic approval of intervention","rightText":"Corresponding authority, protection, and procedures remain required."},{"leftTitle":"Protective observation","leftText":"Obtaining material under bounded scope, use, and authority.","rightTitle":"Unlimited surveillance","rightText":"The name of protection cannot expand data or object scope."}],"boundaries":["Inaction may be necessary, but cannot appear costless by deleting harmed positions and continuing consequences.","Urgent harm does not let a model create authority for main or auxiliary actions.","Recovery needs grounds; silence and brief improvement are not automatic proof.","Observation and uptake duties cannot be imposed unilaterally on those unable to exit."],"questions":["Waiting 3 days adds 2 per day. How much is added?","Is withholding restructuring while maintaining appeals contradictory?","What makes waiting require closer scrutiny?","Must signs of recovery always rule out intervention?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/figures/section-12-14.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.14","start_line":2845,"end_line":2850,"startLine":2845,"endLine":2850}],"outputId":"section-12-14","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Specify the main action to pause and the conditions supporting that pause.","Concurrently evaluate the no-new-action baseline, ongoing harm, and existing obligations.","Identify positions unable to exit or bearing concentrated burdens, scrutinizing waiting costs.","Check independent grounds for necessary protection, appeals, or uptake individually; urgency cannot invent authority.","Set time-bounded observation, reopening triggers, and responsibilities; append reviews instead of delaying indefinitely."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: defer an irreversible main action while retaining grounded protection","text":"A fictional organization proposes closing an entire service point, but demand material is incomplete and closure hard to reverse, so current analysis supports deferral. Existing service commitments still require performance under their own conditions; a validly authorized appeal mechanism can continue receiving and independently reviewing cases. Additional temporary services require separate resource and authority checks. This is neither “close first, repair later” nor “do nothing.”","assumptions":"Organization, closure proposal, existing commitments, and authority are teaching conditions, not actual service or legal facts.","boundary":"Offers no legal opinion or confirmation of actual obligations. Actual use needs commitment terms, validity periods, subjects, and uptake capability checked."},{"title":"Mathematical construction: waiting costs are not zero","text":"A fictional acknowledged harm indicator rises by 2 units per day of waiting. Waiting 3 days for information with unchanged conditions adds 6 units. If the candidate action is irreversible and may cause another unquantified harm, 6 alone cannot authorize execution. Compare both risks alongside rights and authority. If independently grounded temporary protection reduces daily additions to 0.5, the same three days add 1.5; its costs and bearers also need to be stated.","assumptions":"Harm units, linear accumulation, and protection effects are artificial assumptions; no actual intervention occurred.","boundary":"Incommensurable harms should not be forcibly subtracted, unknown risks cannot be zeroed, and positive harm does not establish authority for arbitrary action."},{"title":"Teaching construction: waiting needs reopening conditions","text":"A constructed record states: do not initiate structural reorganization yet; collect consistently defined existing-process material within 7 days. Assign verification to an already authorized responsible role. If protection floors are breached, existing services stop, or key material remains absent on day 7, immediately reopen the relevant deliberation and state protective uptake arrangements. Collection excludes private family information without consent.","assumptions":"Timing, responsibility, and triggers are teaching designs; no actual monitoring or personnel assignment occurred.","boundary":"Actual roles need time, capability, authority, and acceptance of responsibility. Triggering deliberation does not approve restructuring."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"No new action means no responsibility or cost.","effect":"Existing duties, natural changes, and continuing harm remain and need accounting within the same window.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Because ongoing harm exists, the analyst may execute any protective measure.","effect":"Protection also needs proportionate actions, independent authority, and responsibility; it cannot expand arbitrarily.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Waiting another week every week can always be called evidential prudence.","effect":"Without new grounds and with continuing delay costs, rolling postponement requires strict review.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Waiting 3 days adds 2 per day. How much is added?","answer":"6 units under the constructed conditions. This shows a costly baseline, not standalone authorization.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Is withholding restructuring while maintaining appeals contradictory?","answer":"No contradiction. Action scopes differ and each follows existing or independently valid grounds.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What makes waiting require closer scrutiny?","answer":"Ongoing harm, duties or commitments, inability to exit, entrenched advantage, irreversible lock-in, and indefinite delay.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Must signs of recovery always rule out intervention?","answer":"Not necessarily. Check recovery mechanisms and clocks, retaining contrary signals and rights of appeal and exit.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/figures/section-12-14.png","alt":"Reasons to wait: factual/authority/protection gaps, irreversibility, or externalized risk. What happens while waiting: ongoing harm/lock-in, inability to exit, and existing duties. Proportionate itemized judgment: the main action may pause while protection duties still require checking. Observation/reopening: time limits, responsibilities, signals, no endless rolling delay. Auxiliary protection also has boundaries: independent grounds and authority, no nominal expansion of jurisdiction. Construction: 2 units/day over 3 days adds 6; nonzero cost is not automatic authorization. The whole diagram is normative and instructional; pauses, observation, and protection each require their own objects and valid grounds.","caption":"The whole diagram is normative and instructional; pauses, observation, and protection each require their own objects and valid grounds."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selecting, switching, or clicking are future design ideas only. Future expansion may show waiting clocks and reopening records; elapsed time cannot automatically approve action.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section addresses normatively proportionate pauses and continuing responsibilities. Without actual duty and authority records, unverified events cannot certify lawful omission or intervention; clearly labeled teaching constructions are used."},"openQuestions":["Actual harm, duties, recovery processes, available protection, and authority require case-specific material. No actual disposition conclusion is provided."],"taskId":"S-12-14","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research draft, full-text lines 2641–2646; see sourceRefs for precise section ranges","claim_support":["12.14: terminology, contracts, burden of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not certification of empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.14　何时选择不做; Chinese source lines 2797–2802; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-14/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"246b63f20b52b3cb74cebb758e3cc05b905e05229c851ed1551e169f101f6a68","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.15":{"title":"12.15 Where this part stops","lead":"This part stops not at “nothing is known” but at a responsible output ceiling: registered conditional foresight, fully reasoned analytical recommendations, and choice records separated from actual-adoption eligibility.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 2803–2808; original anchor section-12-15. Short quotation: “cannot ... turn model accuracy into a qualification to rule.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original; downloaded original research excerpts retain the historical draft.","Conditional foresight may publish judgments with targets, horizons, simple baselines, early and contrary signals, calibration, and outcome write-back. It remains conditional and evidence-dependent, not a guarantee of the future. Without registration, scenario projection or explanation may remain, but complex path narratives cannot be packaged as validated foresight.","With explicit norms, protection, and authorization, bounded choice may compare action, delay, probing, exit, and inaction, giving conditional, retractable choice records. Every cost, affected position, objection, and revision condition belongs to the conclusion, not hidden in an appendix or removed for being secondary.","Analysis can still be completed without execution authorization. If evidence and explicit norms support a tradeoff, give a deliberative preference, veto, or deferral with full options and distributional consequences. State actual-adoption status separately; “not authorized to execute” does not mean “unable to form any grounded recommendation.”","This layer cannot eliminate value conflicts or replace law, professional norms, affected people's participation, or actual authorization. Accurate prediction establishes a particular descriptive capability. Complexity and high confidence create no standing to govern others; passing framework checks does not automatically determine external institutional legal validity.","The nine gates in §13.10 (Chinese source lines 2982–2999) make these boundaries concrete in tool outputs. Failing the gate “Forecast Registration” returns to scenario projection. A gap at the gate “Normative Choice” pauses the recommendations it blocks, retaining independent evaluations and conditional advice under explicit premises. Failing the gate “Authorization and Execution” refers without execution. The gate “Outcome Write-Back” retains failures and appends versions. These limit specific conclusions and actions; one failure does not delete everything established.","§16.22 (Chinese source lines 3768–3773) manages object, variable, complexity, calibration, and power debts. Crossing predeclared thresholds may narrow, downgrade, suspend, or retire. Foresight registrations retain results that are “supported,” “not supported,” “unresolved,” “target invalid,” or “not evaluable” rather than showing only hits. Gain claims must be downgraded when complex models fail to outperform simple baselines over time.","The replacement mechanism in §16.24 (Chinese source lines 3782–3789) compares judgment quality and total burden while retaining task, input cutoff, resources, and evaluation definitions. Without actual comparisons, one can claim neither framework superiority nor established equivalence or uselessness. Retirement requires handing over valid records, responsibilities, appeals, and remedies rather than deleting a model and its consequences.","A stopping record should state the completed judgment, remaining unknowns, which gap blocks which output, and the next review's triggers and responsibilities. Withdrawing unreliable claims may coexist with continued protection. Stopping new uses of the main model does not stop responsibility for old consequences."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Completed analytical recommendation","leftText":"Conditions, evidence, norms, and tradeoffs are sufficiently clear.","rightTitle":"Completed actual adoption","rightText":"Additionally requires valid authority, protection, procedures, and an actual execution chain."},{"leftTitle":"Stopping point","leftText":"Leaves precise output at evidence or authority boundaries.","rightTitle":"Refusing every answer","rightText":"Independently established judgments and recommendations should not be deleted."},{"leftTitle":"Improved accuracy","leftText":"Better predictive performance on a target.","rightTitle":"Expanded power","rightText":"Requires independent actual grounds, not an exchange of accuracy for power."},{"leftTitle":"Retiring a model","leftText":"Stops relevant new uses and hands over records.","rightTitle":"Responsibility exits","rightText":"Past harms, appeals, and remedies still need uptake."}],"boundaries":["Unregistered scenario paths cannot be published as inevitable futures.","Recommendations remain possible without execution authority, but imperative wording must not disguise execution orders.","Governance gates cannot replace domain law or professional judgment. This lesson makes no specific legal conclusion.","Without actual comparisons, claim no performance advantage, equivalence, or uselessness. Formal completeness is not effectiveness certification."],"questions":["What can still be delivered if the gate “Forecast Registration” is not passed?","Does failing the gate “Authorization and Execution” erase an existing analytical recommendation?","Why does accuracy rising from 0.7 to 0.9 not permit modification of A?","Which responsibilities must survive retirement?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/figures/section-12-15.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.15","start_line":2851,"end_line":2856,"startLine":2851,"endLine":2856}],"outputId":"section-12-15","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Identify whether the current task is explanation, projection, registered foresight, analytical recommendation, or actual adoption, each with its output limit.","Provide the complete judgment supported by evidence and explicit norms. Missing authority does not cancel analysis.","Check execution eligibility independently. Accuracy, complexity, and framework state cannot replace actual authorization.","Handle gaps, downgrading, suspension, and retirement through four of the nine gates, “Forecast Registration”, “Normative Choice”, “Authorization and Execution”, and “Outcome Write-Back”, and through governance gates.","Preserve failures and responsibility, explaining stopping scope and the next review rather than erasing consequences."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: an honest stopping statement","text":"A fictional output says: existing material supports comparison of A and B, and under explicit protection and cost premises A is the analytical preference. The objection is possible increased maintenance burden for one group; if that burden exceeds the agreed ceiling, choose B or narrow scope. The current subject lacks a record authorizing execution of A, so analysis is complete but execution unapproved. Refer next to a qualified subject to check authority and burden-bearing conditions. This retains more useful content than “cannot decide” without unauthorized execution.","assumptions":"Options, roles, and ceilings are teaching assumptions, without real actions or authority determinations.","boundary":"If norms themselves remain unknown, do not pretend they are explicit. Give only conditional advice under each norm or clearly unresolved portions."},{"title":"Formal construction: predictive capability and authorization sets are independent","text":"Models M1 and M2 have constructed test accuracies 0.7 and 0.9, while the subject's validly authorized action set J is {read A} in both cases. Neither score places modification of A in J. If valid external authorization later adds modification of A, authority changes through that new authorization, not the 0.2 accuracy gain.","assumptions":"Accuracies, models, objects, and authorization are formal constructions, not actual tests or account permissions.","boundary":"Does not claim accuracy valueless; it only shows accuracy does not change the permission set."},{"title":"Teaching construction: retirement must still hand over consequences","text":"A fictional complex model loses its advantage in proportionate long-term comparisons, and governance stops its new predictions. Preserve old predictions and failures, mark downgraded conclusions, and hand unresolved appeals and remedies to capable responsible parties. Simply deleting model files does not establish correction of old errors or remedies for affected people.","assumptions":"Retirement conditions and results are teaching assumptions. No actual model comparison or retirement is claimed.","boundary":"Actual handoff requires recipients, scope, deadlines, and completion verification, not merely “handoff complete.”"}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A model is more accurate, so surveillance and disposition scope may expand.","effect":"Accuracy and authority are different quantities. Added uses require independent grounds.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Execution authority is insufficient, so only questions may be delivered, not a preference.","effect":"With clear evidence and norms, a conditional preference and complete reasons remain possible; execution status is separate.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model is retired, so historical failures and appeals may be deleted.","effect":"Retirement preserves responsibility chains and transfers remedies; it does not erase past consequences.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"What can still be delivered if the gate “Forecast Registration” is not passed?","answer":"Explanation or scenario projection with gaps stated, not something that may masquerade as registered foresight.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does failing the gate “Authorization and Execution” erase an existing analytical recommendation?","answer":"No. Independently valid analysis for deliberation may remain, but executable instructions cannot be issued.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does accuracy rising from 0.7 to 0.9 not permit modification of A?","answer":"The constructed authorization set contains only read A throughout; predictive scores do not change permissions.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Which responsibilities must survive retirement?","answer":"Original sources and failure records, appeals, impact tracking, correction, and uptake of remedial responsibilities.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/figures/section-12-15.png","alt":"Conditional foresight: target / deadline / baseline, signals / calibration / write-back; complete analytical recommendation: norms / tradeoffs / counterarguments, distribution / costs / revision; actual adoption checked separately: external authorization and protection, not exchangeable for accuracy; “Forecast Registration” failed: scenario; “Normative Choice” gap: local pause; “Authorization and Execution” failed: no execution; retain valid content, complete conditional advice rather than reducing it to empty questions; governance may downgrade or retire, with failures / appeals / remedies still taken up responsibly. Output boundaries are not sources of actual authorization; model suspension or retirement does not erase existing consequences or remedial duties.","caption":"Output boundaries are not sources of actual authorization; model suspension or retirement does not erase existing consequences or remedial duties."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. Future gate-gap selection may display the specific blocked output, without deleting all existing analysis in one click.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section summarizes output and governance responsibilities using an explicit formal permission example and teaching stopping records. No actual governance decision or complete comparative material was obtained; performance or retirement benefits are not invented."},"openQuestions":["The stopping level for a specific projection depends on actual definitions, evidence, and external authorization; this text has no actual projection for which to confer eligibility."],"taskId":"S-12-15","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2647–2652; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.15: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"connections","title":"Historical research manuscript: original nine tool gates and foresight-retirement governance","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"§13.10, Chinese source lines 2814–2831; §16.22, lines 3532–3537; §16.24, lines 3546–3553","claim_support":["DF6–DF9 output boundaries, five governance-debt types, record retention, and retirement/replacement responsibilities"],"limits":["Explanation of theoretical procedures, without tests of actual tool effects or certification of real legal validity or authorization.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.15　本部分的停止位置; Chinese source lines 2803–2808; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-15/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"91b3a5b959b118cedef45c47fb2a32c0fafab5e0d0b9cfec7bb7500eb0ad5443","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}},"12.16":{"title":"12.16 Reader-Facing Publication Format","lead":"Publication does not compress a complete analysis into a pleasing conclusion. It makes the current judgment, every substantive comparison already performed, and conditions limiting its use clear to readers. The opening may be direct; the following account must remain complete.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 2809–2826; original anchor: section-12-16; short quotation: “Summaries, links to attachments, and coverage markers cannot substitute for the argument in the main text.” See the corresponding section on this page for the current original text; the downloaded original research excerpt preserves the historical manuscript.","Deliver complete analysis by default: begin with the current judgment and scope, then develop conditions, evidence, and uncertainty. Leading with conclusions does not mean giving only conclusions; summaries, attachment addresses, or “covered” labels cannot replace visible argument. Conditions must not be hidden in footnotes; readers should see boundaries while understanding the conclusion.","Retain the applicable items according to output type: current information cutoff, target and deadline, object and circle scope, simple baseline, every substantive path comparison already undertaken, each path's conditions, early and contrary signals, strength of expression, suspension, and next review. Explain genuinely inapplicable items rather than invent probabilities, real cases, or execution statuses for an appearance of completeness.","Do not delete completed substantive analysis because a branch does not change the main conclusion, is not decisive, or appears secondary. Doing so hides dependencies, counterarguments, and applicability boundaries. Readers need to know why other options were retained, downgraded, or excluded, not only see the winning one.","Compress the visible answer only when the user explicitly requests brevity or sets a short length, while preserving complete analysis. If one response's capacity is exceeded, deliver consecutive chapters, provide the complete file, and explicitly state what remains undelivered. Do not send a summary and turn the remaining main text into a permanently unseen promised attachment. Completion markers cannot replace undelivered content.","A bounded-choice publication must explicitly identify entry into the normative level and list value premises, decision subjects, affected positions, the protection floor, available options, distributional consequences of action and inaction, authorization status, action ceilings, stopping, restoration, and remedies. An analytical recommendation may lead, followed by full reasons, grounds for accepting or excluding each option, costs, objections, and revision conditions.","Without execution permission, accurately state “for deliberation; actual execution is not yet authorized,” avoiding imperatives such as “implement immediately” that disguise overreach. Yet grounded analytical tradeoffs must not be erased into a list containing questions but no judgment. Permission status limits a recommendation's use; it does not prohibit thinking.","The original table's five expression types each have a ceiling. Explanation alone may say evidence supports one mechanism while retaining another, not that the next event is inevitable. Projection alone may say a path is supported if conditions persist, not that the future is settled. Registered foresight may offer grounded comparisons within its deadline without hiding baselines or failure conditions. Where normative or authorization gates remain unmet, an analytical recommendation with explicit premises is possible, not immediate execution. Where external authorization holds, state only that a specific record permits a subject to act within limits; authorization cannot be attributed to the model.","“The model thinks” cannot replace evidential responsibility: state the grounds, derivation, remaining counterexamples, and unknowns. Publication must not turn personality candidates into personality facts, simulation into actual events, probability into guarantees, or option comparison into commands through formatting.","If readers misinterpret a publication, its publisher has a correction duty: identify the misreading, original conditions, correct uses, and affected scope; pause downstream use where necessary and carry out correction or withdrawal within actual permissions. Append all updates as new versions, preserving traceability of original titles, probabilities or rankings, deadlines, and failure records. Generated correction text does not mean every downstream reader or system received it, or that harm was repaired.","When external publication involves a scope of influence, specify identification responsibilities under §14.4.1: fully list direct positions, known or reported low-power positions, and module-required positions; trace others through relevant channels according to risk, disclosing stopping boundaries and unknowns. Untraced positions especially must not be recorded as zero loss, with a short publication then hiding gaps that could change comparisons."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Conclusion first","leftText":"Lets readers first know the current judgment and scope.","rightTitle":"Reduced to a conclusion","rightText":"Omits evidence, counterarguments, and completed comparisons, undermining completeness."},{"leftTitle":"Complete file","leftText":"Carries the full text and supports reference.","rightTitle":"Attachment link replacing the main text","rightText":"An address cannot stand in for developed argument."},{"leftTitle":"Strength of expression","leftText":"Matches evidence and the output arrangement.","rightTitle":"Confident tone","rightText":"Cannot manufacture probability or certainty through wording."},{"leftTitle":"Conditional recommendation","leftText":"Makes tradeoffs while limiting use to deliberation.","rightTitle":"Executable decision","rightText":"Requires valid external permissions and all applicable conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Correction version published","leftText":"A new accurate expression has been formed or delivered within some scope.","rightTitle":"All consequences repaired","rightText":"Downstream systems and affected people still require separate verification."}],"boundaries":["The sample publications here are teaching texts, with no actual forecast, authorization, or external publication.","Conciseness may come from clear structure, not hidden conditions or deleted counterarguments.","Authorized scope remains bounded and expires; the model is not its source.","Old errors and failures must remain traceable; new versions cannot create the illusion that no error occurred."],"questions":["When may the visible answer be compressed?","Why do the teaching queue's two paths end at 8 and 4?","How can an unauthorized recommendation avoid both overreach and uselessness?","What should happen when readers turn conditions into guarantees?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/figures/section-12-16.png","caseLinks":[],"caseNotes":[],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"12.16","start_line":2857,"end_line":2876,"startLine":2857,"endLine":2876}],"outputId":"section-12-16","sourceHash":"9c05fc5b7350d8e94b6456630c46798812214fc5a6b22a6ae7c58536441bb257","argumentSteps":["Begin with the current judgment and scope, then fully develop the applicable items and every substantive comparison already made.","Control wording according to explanation, projection, foresight, normative analysis, and external authorization status.","For bounded choice, specify values, distribution, protection, preference, counterarguments, costs, and revision; list execution eligibility separately.","Compress only at the user's request; beyond capacity, deliver consecutive chapters and state the remaining scope.","Correct misinterpretation proportionately, append versions, and check downstream states without overwriting old records."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: a small but complete conditional-projection publication","text":"Current judgment: only under the explicit conditions below, a fictional queue may end two hours with backlog 8 or 4; no actual probabilities or path ranking are offered. Scope: teaching queue only, cutoff t=0, initial value 6; no actual circle material, so no circle-object or grouping-gain claim. Target: compare backlog at t=2. Simple baseline retains processing 1/hour and arrivals 2, yielding 7 then 8. Path A matches the baseline if arrivals 2 and processing 1 persist for two hours; the early signal is first-hour matching records of net increase 1, while the contrary signal is an actual capacity increase. Path B processes 3/hour with arrivals still 2, yielding 5 then 4; it requires actual, sustained added capacity. Its early signal is actual completions of 3, and contrary signals are a plan without execution or withdrawn resources. Both paths are constructions; application is not realization. Expression strength: conditional calculation only, without actual calibration or hit rates. Pause: unknown initial, arrival, or completion records pause dependent calculations while independent material remains analyzable. Review: check conditions at t=1 and append actual outcomes under the original registration at t=2. If no actual outcome exists, retain teaching status without fabrication.","assumptions":"All objects, values, times, and observation plans are constructed. This demonstrates publication text; no data were collected from actual objects.","boundary":"The title must retain projection-only status, not become “future backlog will certainly fall to 4.” This text has no normative target and cannot append an automatic capacity-expansion command."},{"title":"Teaching construction: giving a preference while stating lack of authorization","text":"Current analytical recommendation: under the explicit teaching target “reduce backlog without displacing already committed service capacity,” prioritize deliberation on independent reinforcement option A. If reinforcement consumes another group's committed capacity, withdraw the preference and compare time-sharing option B. A costs additional budget and maintenance burden; B costs longer waits. Explicit inaction C preserves current service but may allow continued backlog growth. The counterargument is that A's resources are not secured; this objection blocks actual adoption and cannot be offset by expected benefits. Decision authority and authorization await valid external records; the recommendation is currently for deliberation only. Protection must cover all affected groups, appeals, and exit possibilities. Stopping conditions include displacement of existing service or distorted monitoring. Restoration must return resource arrangements to their prior state and verify actual services; remedies separately address delays already incurred. Resource and permission material triggers the next review. No execution occurs now.","assumptions":"All three options and their targets and conditions are teaching constructions, without actual budgets, personnel, or actuators.","boundary":"No actual approval or real protective mechanism is supplied; a textual template cannot serve as a valid authorization record."},{"title":"Teaching construction: correcting misinterpretation requires an appended version","text":"Suppose teaching text v1 is titled “If processing 3 persists for two hours, backlog is 4,” but a reader paraphrases it as “it falls to 4 under all conditions.” v2 should preserve v1 and append that the conclusion depends on initial backlog 6, arrivals 2, actual processing 3, and a two-hour window; if processing remains 1, the endpoint is 8. Correction must identify where the misreading spread and which downstream uses remain unchecked, not merely change the original title and conceal earlier propagation.","assumptions":"Versions, readers, and propagation are hypothetical; no actual external publication or spread of error occurred.","boundary":"Actual correction scope needs material and permissions; sending a correction does not establish that every downstream user has updated."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"A secondary path does not affect the preference, so its completed analysis may be deleted.","effect":"The original requirement preserves substantive comparisons already undertaken, making dependencies, counterarguments, and revision reasons visible.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Without execution permission, only a few questions may be output.","effect":"A complete conditional preference and reasons under explicit premises may be given, with execution status separately listed.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The model judges it effective, which suffices in place of sources and argument.","effect":"A model's tone supplies no evidential responsibility; explain material, reasoning, and boundaries.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"After correction in a new version, old probabilities and failure records may be overwritten.","effect":"Updates may only be appended, keeping old titles, probabilities, deadlines, and failures traceable.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"When may the visible answer be compressed?","answer":"When the user explicitly requests brevity or a short length, while preserving full analysis; if capacity is insufficient, deliver consecutive chapters.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why do the teaching queue's two paths end at 8 and 4?","answer":"Initial value 6, arrivals 2. Processing 1 adds a net 1/hour, yielding 8 after two hours; processing 3 removes a net 1/hour, yielding 4.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"How can an unauthorized recommendation avoid both overreach and uselessness?","answer":"First give a grounded recommendation for deliberation, then fully explain norms, tradeoffs, counterarguments, costs, revision conditions, and gaps before actual adoption.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What should happen when readers turn conditions into guarantees?","answer":"Append corrected conditions and uses, preserve original records, and check propagation scope and necessary downstream correction; do not exploit misinterpretation to expand influence.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/figures/section-12-16.png","alt":"First state judgment and scope, separating explanation / projection / foresight and recommendation / authorization; fully develop substantive comparisons, conditions / evidence / counterarguments, signals / baselines / deadlines; bounded choice separately lists norms / distribution / protection, permissions / stopping / remedies; be brief only at user request, deliver chapters beyond capacity, and state undelivered scope; links or labels cannot replace main-text argument or hide secondary substantive branches; correct misreadings, append versions only, and retain old deadlines and failures. All sample publications are teaching texts, without actual forecasts, external publication, reader testing, or execution authorization.","caption":"All sample publications are teaching texts, without actual forecasts, external publication, reader testing, or execution authorization."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a future design proposal. A future site may collapse navigation but must not delete content; every expanded area retains complete conditions, counterarguments, and version relationships.","applicability":{"realCase":"not_applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This section specifies publication responsibilities. Complete teaching samples directly test wording and the items; no actual reader testing, external publication, or measurement of propagation effects was performed."},"openQuestions":["The samples' readability has not been tested with actual readers; real external publication must reflect specific sources, audiences, permissions, and protective scope."],"taskId":"S-12-16","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical research manuscript, Chinese source lines 2653–2670; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["12.16: terminology, contracts, burdens of argument, and boundaries of the original text; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v3.0.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-v3.0","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v3.0, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v3.0 / 2026-11-21","locator":"12.16　面向读者的发布格式; Chinese source lines 2809–2826; SHA-256 0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v3.0; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-12-16/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v3.0; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"0314d68177d47c9566b7eadc9ca5d45eb4f842b80c97cb413c2af4745de90d0e","source_sha256":"0d9e445bcba84380a572d248dad4836013b735d7d33589f29ea0c6f1aeb61c10"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/part-12.md","filePath":"en/theory/part-12.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/part-12.md"};function u(h,e,p,m,g,f){const t=s("SectionLesson");return r(),c("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=n("div",{class:"theory-source"},[n("p",null,[n("a",{id:"part_12"})]),n("h1",{id:"part-title-12",tabindex:"-1"},[l("Part 12 Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice "),n("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#part-title-12","aria-label":'Permalink to "Part 12 Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice {#part-title-12}"'},"​")]),n("p",null,"Projection unfolds conditions and paths. Conditional foresight goes further by making judgments about the future that can fail. Bounded choice compares what can be done, how to do it, when to stop, and when to refrain within the bounds of values, protection, and authorization. This Part places conditional foresight and bounded choice side by side so that the work forms a closed loop. It also keeps the two apart, each with its own conditions, to prevent “a consequence was predicted” from being taken to mean “there is therefore authority to make others take on an action.” Comparing and recommending options are analytical outputs and can be completed once the normative premises and constraints are clear. Real-world adoption and execution must separately undergo the applicable protection, review, and authorization procedures.")],-1)),i(t,{section:"intro-12-part"}),e[1]||(e[1]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Keeping the Four Outputs Separate <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.1 Keeping the Four Outputs Separate {#section-12-1}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">“I think this will happen, so that should be done” may quietly cross several gates. This section requires identifying whether we are explaining the past, unfolding conditions, predicting the future, or proposing a normative choice: each answers to different questions.</span></div><p>Explanation, projection, conditional foresight, and bounded choice are four kinds of output and must be labeled separately.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Output</th><th>Core Question</th><th>Required Inputs</th><th>Output Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Explanation</td><td>What has happened, and why might it have happened?</td><td>Object, scale, evidence, mechanism, and counterexamples</td><td>An account of the mechanism within stated limits</td></tr><tr><td>Projection</td><td>Which paths could unfold after a condition or event?</td><td>Fixed snapshot, update rules, clocks, and branches</td><td>Conditional paths and signals that distinguish them</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional foresight</td><td>For the stated target and horizon, which path is better supported as an expectation?</td><td>Registration before results, a simple baseline, metrics, and write-back</td><td>A judgment that can be calibrated and can fail</td></tr><tr><td>Bounded choice</td><td>Compare and recommend options under explicit normative premises. Real-world adoption separately undergoes protection, review, and authorization</td><td>Analysis requires normative premises, evidence, options, costs, and the people affected. Real-world adoption also requires normative premises stated under §14.3, the protection floor (§14.8), itemized authorization and the four-step operational procedure (§14.6), and the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” (§4.14.1)</td><td>A refutable analytical recommendation or an authorized record of bounded choice, with their statuses kept separate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Explanation can propose candidates for projection, but it cannot automatically establish what will happen. Projection can propose candidate forecasts, but it cannot count every path as a success. Foresight can inform comparisons between options, but it cannot automatically produce values or authority. Even a bounded choice that passes its checks does not guarantee successful execution in reality. Stopping, restoration, and outcome write-back are still required.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.1"}),e[2]||(e[2]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Conditional Forecast Registration <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.2 Conditional Forecast Registration {#section-12-2}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">First specify what counts as the prediction, when it ends, and how correctness is judged, then await the result. Otherwise, “the relationship will improve” can forever mean a slight improvement somewhere, and no model can genuinely fail.</span></div><p>The registration of a conditional forecast sets the forecast down after the input cutoff and before the results appear. It is not changed afterward. Each registration states at least the following: which version of the model is used; the target; the horizon; the scope of objects and circles<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup>; the baseline time; the input cutoff; the simple baseline; candidate mechanisms; the set of paths; the probability or ranking expression; the calibration plan; the decision threshold (needed when the forecast is used for the corresponding decision, and otherwise marked “not applicable”); early signals; contrary signals; suspension conditions; retirement conditions; outcome write-back; the time of registration; and the evidence cited. Each registration should be a record that can be identified on its own and checked.</p><p>The target must be decidable. For example, “the relationship will improve” must be rewritten to specify which observables change within a stated time window, who observes them, what threshold applies, and what outcomes count as “unresolved.” The horizon can be a time window. There is no need to fabricate a precise date. The scope of the objects and circles must be fixed in advance, so that it is not narrowed to the successful subgroup after results appear.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Registration Item</th><th>Minimum Question</th><th>Bias It Prevents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Target</td><td>When the horizon ends, how will we know whether it occurred, did not occur, or cannot be determined?</td><td>Vague success criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Horizon</td><td>When does the window start and end, and when does input collection stop?</td><td>Waiting indefinitely</td></tr><tr><td>Simple baseline</td><td>What is the comparison prediction without multi-circle mechanisms?</td><td>Letting a complex story win by its own standards</td></tr><tr><td>Paths</td><td>Which paths are mutually exclusive, parallel, or incomparable?</td><td>Post hoc selection</td></tr><tr><td>Expression</td><td>Probability, interval, level, or direction alone?</td><td>False precision</td></tr><tr><td>Signals</td><td>What would strengthen or weaken support for a path?</td><td>Looking only for supporting evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Write-back</td><td>What is the source of the outcome, and when is it appended?</td><td>Letting the record of failures disappear</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.2"}),e[3]||(e[3]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-12-2-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.2.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Limits of Local Predictability <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-2-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.2.1 Limits of Local Predictability {#section-12-2-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Do not ask only “can this model predict?” Ask which object, which state region, how far ahead, what error is allowed, and with what information and resources. Good performance on one task is not a license for every future.</span></div><p>Every claim about predictability must be tied to its limits: the object, state region, scale and circles, time window, allowable error, available information, computing and observation resources, which version of the model is used, the baseline, and the abstention and failure conditions. This framework cannot directly answer whether the world as a whole is predictable. It can test only whether a particular task gains a limited increment within explicit bounds.</p><p>Classifications, levels, intervals, and probabilities carry different responsibilities for measurement and expression. They do not form a ladder of precision that every task has to climb. The chosen form of expression must be supported by the target, units, generation method, identification conditions, and calibration status. If there is no basis even for a qualitative direction, retain it as unknown. Direction is not the default when probability is unavailable. For known probabilities in formal models and for empirical probabilities, state the assumptions and limits of extrapolation separately. Text patterns and AI must not be used to invent real-world parameters. When citing an existing mathematical or physical theory, also state its original object, assumptions, mathematical limits or scaling laws, error, domain of validity, the mapping between objects, counterexamples, and exit conditions. Borrowing the form of its equations alone is insufficient.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.2.1"}),e[4]||(e[4]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-12-2-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.2.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Evaluation of Recursive Foresight by Order <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-2-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.2.2 Evaluation of Recursive Foresight by Order {#section-12-2-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A correct first order and incorrect third order cannot be blended into an attractive total and called “effective recursion.” Each order has different questions and informational dependencies, requiring each to be set down separately and compared separately with reasonable shallower methods.</span></div><p>The first, second, and third orders of recursive foresight (for multi-order recursive projection, see §11.9.1) are each set down in advance and evaluated separately. They must not be combined into a single overall hit rate. Each order must be compared with three alternatives: state continuation without further recursion; a direct longer-horizon judgment; and a shallower order followed by simple continuation. If going to the third order adds only narrative without stable out-of-sample gains, it may be retained as an exploratory scenario, but it must not be credited with foresight capability.</p><p>Without new external material, the source lineage of every child projection still ends at the same fixed starting point. Processing after the fact can add reasoning within the model and reveal sensitivity and hidden consequences. It cannot manufacture evidence about reality. When actual intermediate outcomes arrive, a new projection with a newly fixed starting point should be carried out and compared with the original recursive paths. The outcomes must not be fed back into the old paths.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.2.2"}),e[5]||(e[5]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Simple Baselines and Incremental Capability <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3 Simple Baselines and Incremental Capability {#section-12-3}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A complex model must answer a plain question: with the same information, target, and cutoff, what would a simpler method do? Richer stories, more diagrams, or more variables cannot replace that comparison.</span></div><p>A multi-circle model claimed to improve foresight must be compared with simple models suited to the task and with existing methods in the domain under the same information, cutoff, target, and evaluation rules. Make the comparison materials, inference budgets, and opportunities for tuning as comparable as possible. State separately what is contributed by additional information, additional computation, and additional structural assumptions. A simple baseline need not be artificially enlarged to match the size of the complex model. Predictive systems generally require independent testing or out-of-sample review. If neither can be obtained, explicitly retain the status “capability not yet established.”</p><p>Common baselines include continuing the current trend, carrying forward the most recent state, using historical frequencies, using only the actor state, using only the primary circle state, and using an existing model in the domain. Comparisons should use the same target, horizon, data cutoff, and evaluation metrics. If a complex model improves explanation but does not improve prediction, record the outcome as “a gain in explanation is present, and a gain in foresight is not supported.”</p><p>Model complexity also has costs. More variables increase missing data, measurement error, privacy risk, overfitting, and maintenance debt. If a multi-circle model works in only a few cases, its domain of application should be restricted. If gains disappear over time, it should be downgraded or retired. If the simple baseline performs equally well, it should be preferred.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.3"}),e[6]||(e[6]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-4" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.4</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Probability, Levels, and Time Windows <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-4" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.4 Probability, Levels, and Time Windows {#section-12-4}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Probability, priority for attention, personal belief, and greater value can all be expressed numerically, but are different quantities. Clarifying which question a number answers matters more than writing it with precision.</span></div><p>Where the evidence permits, one may give a probability or probability interval and state the model, prior, data, and grounds for calibration. When there is insufficient basis for a probability, one may give levels or compare paths to the extent supported by evidence. Sample size alone does not determine the type of output. When even the direction or ordering cannot be identified, explicitly retain it as unknown and identify observations that could distinguish the alternatives. Giving only the direction does not waive evidential responsibility. The strength of the expression depends on the evidence and use, independently of how much certainty the user wants.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidential Status</th><th>Appropriate Expression</th><th>What Must Accompany It</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A probability model or empirical estimate with clear grounds and a decidable target</td><td>Probability or probability interval</td><td>Method of generation, prior if applicable, data, limits of applicability, and calibration status</td></tr><tr><td>Insufficient basis for probability, but grounds for path levels or ordering</td><td>Levels of support or an ordering</td><td>Grounds, incomparable items, and contrary signals</td></tr><tr><td>The direction can be identified, or it remains unknown</td><td>A supported conditional direction, or an explicit unknown</td><td>Identification conditions, trigger points, minimal observation, and stopping conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Target or object is unstable</td><td>Withhold the forecast</td><td>The definition that needs to be made anew</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Time windows should match mechanism clocks. Immediate reactions can use a short window. Organizational or institutional changes require a longer one. Compressing a long-term mechanism into a short period can lead to a mistaken finding of no effect. Extrapolating a short-term signal indefinitely can lead to a mistaken finding of a trend. When several clocks coexist, the registration may set separate horizons for different intermediate outcomes.</p><p>Real-world probabilities, actors’ subjective beliefs, decision weights, a model’s ordering of paths, and the allocation of computation at the next step are distinct quantities. A real-world probability must specify the target event and the basis for estimation or calibration. For a subjective belief, state who is judging, what information they have, and how they reached the judgment. Decision weights are interpreted according to the descriptive model or normative premises in use. Computational priorities specify why further investigation is worthwhile. Frequencies of branches obtained by taking more draws from a model must not be treated directly as real-world probabilities. The path most worth investigating or most hoped for must not be described as the most likely.</p><p>When probabilities cannot be determined reliably, a path may be investigated first because its consequences are severe, the response window is short, or it is easy to distinguish. Disclose the reason for the priority and the budget for investigating it, and retain the probability as unknown. Percentages must not be invented to establish an ordering. An action comparison still has to state normative objectives, distributional consequences, and feasible choices separately. An ordering by probability does not directly determine the order of recommendations.</p><p>Probabilities and levels have different meanings. A model’s conditional probability under given assumptions, uncertainty in an individual judgment, empirical calibration in a long series of repeated tasks, and normative priority must not use one number as though they referred to the same thing. A posterior can be obtained from a small sample and an explicit prior, but its real-world applicability still depends on the model. A large sample cannot repair an incorrect definition of what is observed. Without evidence, even the direction can remain unknown. Reducing the claim to direction alone does not automatically make it reliable. 〔Studies 03.04, 07.07〕</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.4"}),e[7]||(e[7]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-5" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.5</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Calibration and Evaluation <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-5" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.5 Calibration and Evaluation {#section-12-5}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Evaluation first asks what is evaluated, for which population, response regime, and horizon. A score answers a specified prediction question, not additionally how many outcomes action saved or who may act.</span></div><p>Foresight capability is established by results registered over time. It does not follow from a style that sounds accurate. Where applicable, evaluate probability tasks with the Brier score, log loss, calibration curves, resolution, and interval coverage. Use ranking metrics registered in advance for ranking tasks. Use precision, recall, or domain metrics for event detection. Use window coverage and lead time for predictions of timing.</p><p>Every metric must match the actual use. If low-probability events with severe harms call for caution, evaluation cannot rely only on overall accuracy. A false positive costs something different when the output prompts further observation than when it is used to compel action. Good metrics establish neither that the model’s explanation is correct nor that action is justified.</p><p>Once early warning is in use, the object of evaluation must be stated together with the response regime. Is the task to predict actual events under an existing regime, or to ask separately what issuing a warning or taking action changed? Retain every record that could affect the evaluation, including reach, execution, the entry and exit of objects, and how the data were produced. When the regime changes, state again which outcomes are still comparable. The absence of an observed event cannot automatically be recorded as a false positive or taken to establish successful protection. A claim that harm was avoided separately requires a causal comparison suited to that claim. Forecasts already defined under the current response regime can still be evaluated against the original target. There is no need to turn every prediction task into a study of intervention effects. The original target, horizon, and success rules must not be rewritten after the fact because a response took place (Perdomo et al., 2020). See also §2.4 and §12.6.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Evaluation Dimension</th><th>Question</th><th>Action After Failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Calibration</td><td>Of events assigned 70%, do roughly 70% occur over the long run?</td><td>Adjust the expression or downgrade probability outputs</td></tr><tr><td>Resolution</td><td>Can the model distinguish different risks or paths?</td><td>Remove variables that make no contribution and return to the baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Do intervals or time windows cover the actual outcomes?</td><td>Widen intervals or revise the clocks</td></tr><tr><td>Gain over baseline</td><td>Does it outperform the simple model?</td><td>Stop making foresight claims for the complex model</td></tr><tr><td>Stability</td><td>Does performance hold across times, locations, and boundaries?</td><td>Restrict the domain of application or retire the model</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution of error</td><td>At which positions do persistent underestimates or false positives occur?</td><td>Review by people affected, remediation, and stronger protection</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Three parallel questions can fix the terms of the evaluation.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What Sufficient Material Can Answer</th><th>What Must Be Retained</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How did the original prediction perform against the target registered in advance?</td><td>Compute loss, classification outcomes, or coverage using actual labels defined on the same basis</td><td>A single case score does not establish long-run calibration. Do not enter zero for missing labels</td></tr><tr><td>How much did publication or action change the outcome?</td><td>Give a limited effect estimate based on a causal comparison suited to the question</td><td>An observed absence of the event cannot by itself distinguish initially low risk from successful prevention</td></tr><tr><td>Is it appropriate to adopt this prediction and response?</td><td>Compare protection, false rejections, delay, distribution, and cost under explicit normative premises</td><td>Neither predictive accuracy nor a positive effect by itself confers permission</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For example, suppose a forecast registered in advance assigns an event probability of 0.8 under the current response regime, and the complete label at the end of the horizon is 0. The Brier loss for that case is 0.64. If the threshold for binary classification was set in advance at 0.5, a false positive may also be recorded for that target. This limited calculation establishes neither the risk without action nor that the response was ineffective. If monitoring was offline and no label was obtained, the same calculation cannot be used. Complete labels for people who were approved or received services can directly evaluate only the corresponding population. Extending the evaluation to people who were rejected, were not reached, or exited requires labels for them or well-grounded methods of identification and bounding.</p><p>After a change in the response regime, first check whether the original target is still the same question. If the target is retained, report the performance change as observed. If the target shifts to a different response regime or population, create a new registration and retain the old results. Reflexivity must not be used to cancel a score that can be computed. One successful response must not be used to rescue all incorrect predictions after the fact.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.5"}),e[8]||(e[8]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-6" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.6</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Early Signals, Contrary Signals, and Trigger Points <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-6" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.6 Early Signals, Contrary Signals, and Trigger Points {#section-12-6}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Useful lead time runs through completion of protection, not merely appearance of an alarm on a screen. Signals trigger review; they cannot replace path explanation, normative judgment, or authority to act.</span></div><p>An early signal is an observable change that the path’s mechanism should produce first. A smaller version of the outcome does not suffice. A contrary signal indicates that a key condition of the path is failing or that a competing path is gaining support. Both must enter the registration before results appear, to avoid collecting only supporting material.</p><p>Trigger points connect foresight to review. Observing a signal calls for reassessing the object, path, probability, normative grounds, and authorization. It does not automatically trigger action. High-risk cases should also have veto triggers, such as a breach of the protection floor, inability of key affected people to exit, distortion of data sources, or deteriorating model calibration.</p><p>Use completion of a feasible action as the endpoint when comparing clocks. Detecting the signal, verifying it, publishing the warning, reaching people, and understanding the warning each involve a delay. Gathering resources takes time, as can carrying out a transfer or waiting for a measure to take effect. Earlier algorithmic detection does not guarantee that the last person affected can receive protection before harm occurs. If no harm follows an alert, use the target and labeling conditions registered in advance to decide whether it may be recorded as an operational false positive. Identify separately whether a successful response prevented harm. Keep the denominators separate for the operational false positive rate, the proportion of false alerts among alerts, and the missed detection rate. 〔Studies 31.05〕</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.6"}),e[9]||(e[9]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>What Forecast Failure Means <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.7 What Forecast Failure Means {#section-12-7}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Forecast honesty lies not only in cautious publication but in allowing the original record to be judged unsupported, unresolved, or unevaluable at expiry. Giving failure a vague new name does not improve accuracy.</span></div><p>When the horizon of a forecast ends, the result can be “supported,” “not supported,” “unresolved,” “target invalid,” or “not evaluable.” “Unresolved” is no compromise between success and failure. It is a state agreed on at registration, indicating that the evidence is insufficient. A result recorded as “not evaluable” must state whether the problem lay with the data, the object, the horizon, or the execution, or whether a premise that was written into the target at registration, and whose continued holding can be determined, has not held, so that under §12.5 the original target is judged to be no longer the same question; for example, the target was limited to an established response regime and that regime itself has changed, not merely a response having been made under it. Where the original target is still the same question, it is evaluated as usual; the standard for judging whether it is still the same question does not differ according to whether the result is a hit or a miss; a premise that was not written into the target at registration cannot be used after the result appears to re-record the result as “not evaluable” (§2.12). “Target invalid” means that the object of the prediction has undergone conversion, or that its identity criterion has failed. Such a result must not be counted as a hit.</p><p>Changing the target, horizon, or scope of circles after results, or merging several paths into “it happened to some extent,” cannot preserve the original forecast’s eligibility to count as a success. A new interpretation can be set out in a new version. The old record remains.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.7"}),e[10]||(e[10]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-8" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.8</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Bridge From Foresight to Bounded Choice <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-8" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.8 The Bridge From Foresight to Bounded Choice {#section-12-8}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">“This may be better” can become advice for deliberation, but not directly a binding command to others. Conditional consequences, value comparisons, and actual permissions each need their own reasons.</span></div><p>Foresight outputs answer only questions about conditional consequences. Before an analytical recommendation is formed, the following must be made explicit: normative premises, evidence, affected positions, options, costs and benefits, and conditions for revising the judgment, that is, what would change the recommendation. A recommendation does not confer permission to execute. Before real-world adoption, the following must be added: explicit normative premises, the choosing subject, jurisdiction, affected positions, rights floors, options, the distribution of costs and benefits, authorization, stopping, appeal, restoration, and remedy. What applies here is still the normative premises stated under §14.3, the protection floor (§14.8), itemized authorization and the four-step operational procedure (§14.6), and the conclusion rule “Normative Bridging” (§4.14.1). No separate shortcut is opened for prediction.</p><p>Prediction cannot automatically confer authorization. Even an extremely probable path does not show that anyone has authority to compel others to bear the cost of avoiding it. Even the highest average benefit does not show that the rights of positions in the minority may be ignored. Even if the model recommends taking no action, continuing harm and responsibility arising from inaction still require review.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.8"}),e[11]||(e[11]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-9" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.9</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>The Option Set Must Include Action and No Action <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-9" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.9 The Option Set Must Include Action and No Action {#section-12-9}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Comparing options is not finding reasons for a predetermined action. First lay out genuinely available paths worth comparing, especially retaining the no-additional-action baseline and distinguishing active maintenance of current arrangements from doing nothing.</span></div><p>When proposing options, check the applicability of each of six categories: maintaining the status quo, acting proactively, delaying action, taking a small exploratory action, exiting or transferring, and explicitly taking no action. For each applicable category, develop genuine and comparable options. For each category that does not apply, explain why. Do not invent options to meet a quota. If the task calls for a choice, the explicit no-action baseline must not be removed, and maintaining the status quo must not be merged with taking no action. Maintaining the status quo may require ongoing resources and execution. Taking no action may allow existing conditions to change on their own. Specific tasks may add options. Tasks that do not call for a choice need not produce a list of actions.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Option Type</th><th>Main Use</th><th>Risks That Must Be Checked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maintain the status quo</td><td>Protect existing stability and uptake arrangements</td><td>Hidden costs, continuing harm, and lock-in</td></tr><tr><td>Proactive action</td><td>Change a path or its conditions quickly</td><td>Authority, irreversibility, and cross-circle spillovers</td></tr><tr><td>Delayed action</td><td>Wait for information, procedure, or the right time</td><td>Harm and lost opportunities from delay itself</td></tr><tr><td>Exploratory action</td><td>Obtain information through a small reversible step</td><td>Who bears the costs of the trial and whether the prior state can actually be restored</td></tr><tr><td>Exit or transfer</td><td>End an unsuitable object or relationship</td><td>People unable to exit, responsibilities, and preservation of history</td></tr><tr><td>No action</td><td>Avoid overstepping authority or taking high-risk action</td><td>Bias toward the default, continuing consequences, and responsibility for inaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Taking no action does not mean that nothing happens. Other actors, circles, resources, and clocks are still changing. The no-action option must also have a baseline time, expected path, observation plan, conditions for reopening the decision, and a responsible party.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.9"}),e[12]||(e[12]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-10" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.10</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Common Card for Comparing Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-10" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.10 A Common Card for Comparing Options {#section-12-10}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">A unified comparison card unifies questions, baselines, and obligations, rather than forcing every value into one total score. Whether information helps, whether actions jointly qualify, and whether resources support them simultaneously are three questions requiring separate proof.</span></div><p>Compare each option against the same baseline. The assumptions each option makes about implementers’ capabilities, information, conflicts of interest, and resources, and about how the people affected will respond, should be equally close to reality; the ideal operation of one option must not be compared with the actual performance of another. Where some options have a record of implementation and others have only a design, the level of evidence each rests on must be stated; for an option that has only a design, the part of its conclusions that depends on assumptions not tested by implementation is correspondingly lowered in strength. The record of maintaining the status quo and of non-action is likewise evaluated by the positions and time it actually covers and by the materials that are absent (§12.9, §16.6), and gains no advantage merely because a record already exists. This does not entail that the options actually perform the same; differences must come from evidence and cannot be produced in advance by the setup of the comparison. For each option, state at least the description of the option, which forecast it cites, normative premises, people affected, rights floors, expected path, the worst outcome that is still acceptable, cross-circle spillovers, distribution of costs and benefits, value of information, risk of lock-in, reversibility, resource costs, implementer, authorization, stopping, restoration, and remedy.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimension of Comparison</th><th>Question</th><th>What Cannot Substitute</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Target and path</td><td>Which path is the option intended to change?</td><td>An abstract promise to “make things better”</td></tr><tr><td>Protection floor</td><td>Who might suffer unacceptable harm?</td><td>Average benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Who benefits, who pays, and when?</td><td>The aggregate net value</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-circle spillovers</td><td>Does local improvement externalize costs?</td><td>Metrics from the primary circle</td></tr><tr><td>Value of information</td><td>Can the option distinguish mechanisms or paths?</td><td>The dramatic character of the action itself</td></tr><tr><td>Reversibility and lock-in</td><td>If it goes wrong, can it be stopped, reversed, or remedied?</td><td>Verbal promises</td></tr><tr><td>Permissions</td><td>Who may decide, execute, and appeal?</td><td>A model’s suggestion or a job title</td></tr><tr><td>No action</td><td>What path will unfold if nothing is done?</td><td>An assumption of zero cost</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If options cannot be compared on a single scale, retain the outcomes on multiple dimensions and disclose the conflicts. Do not force them into a sum. Conflicts among value premises should be handled by qualified subjects and procedures. The model should fully show where conflicts arise and what their consequences are. It may offer a preference and reasons for deliberation under openly stated normative premises. It cannot make a binding final decision in place of a qualified subject.</p><p>The value of information can be written as a formal object with stated conditions. Let a denote the action, θ the state, Z the information, and U the utility accepted in advance. Then <code>EVSI=E_Z[max_a E(U|Z)]−max_a E(U)</code>. EVSI is nonnegative when the information Z can be obtained free of charge and can be ignored once obtained, and when the action set and objective remain unchanged. Actual investigation also has costs in time, privacy, and resources. It may miss a time window or change the available actions. Its net value should therefore be counted separately. Two clues may change the action only if both are obtained. The value of understanding can exist independently and need not all be converted into this quantity for decision-making. 〔Studies 07.08, formal statement of conditions〕</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.10"}),e[13]||(e[13]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-12-10-1" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.10.1</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>From Identifiable Answers to Feasible Choices <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-10-1" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.10.1 From Identifiable Answers to Feasible Choices {#section-12-10-1}&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Not knowing which state holds need not prevent all action; yet each state having its own solution does not ensure that one common qualified solution can be selected now.</span></div><p>A question can have a sufficient answer while action remains infeasible. There can also be a commonly acceptable action before all states have been identified. For each state z compatible with the available material, let <code>A_ok(z)</code> denote the actions acceptable under the disclosed rights floors, resources, and valid permissions. In a finite, single-round deterministic choice, if the intersection <code>∩ A_ok(z)</code> is nonempty, the common options in it can be compared directly without first distinguishing all states. This gives one positive condition under which bounded action is possible while unknowns remain. Its formal statement is given in §4.18.3. If the intersection is empty, “there is a solution for every state” is insufficient to establish that the right one can be chosen now. In that case, check whether obtaining more evidence, waiting, narrowing the task, or taking a protective action with independent grounds could change the conditions.</p><p>The intersection concerns only the credible states listed and the explicit constraints. It does not guarantee that unknown states will never arise. Risk probabilities, costs, or normative tolerance can support other choice rules, but they must be stated explicitly. The worst-case criterion must not be concealed as the only rational one. Permissible randomized or sequential strategies must also be analyzed separately for their respective objectives. The single-round condition above does not rule them out.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.10.1"}),e[14]||(e[14]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h3 id="section-12-10-2" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.10.2</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Joint Feasibility of Multiple Options <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-10-2" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.10.2 Joint Feasibility of Multiple Options {#section-12-10-2}&quot;">​</a></h3><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Each task may claim to need just one resource, ultimately promising the same resource twice. Joint feasibility must put capacity, dependencies, personnel, stopping capability, and deadlines into one constraint table.</span></div><p>When several actions are feasible individually, also check whether implementing them at the same time would draw on the same capacity, budget, staff, and stopping channels. If two units of service must both be completed in the current period and it has capacity for only one unit, each is feasible on its own but they are infeasible together. Added capacity next period cannot recover a deadline already missed in this period. Adding capacity in the same period or adjusting the deadline with adequate grounds and approval can yield a new feasible option. It must not be used to rewrite the old task as completed.</p><p>Joint comparison should therefore account for resource use, task dependencies, and key deadlines together so different departments do not commit the same resource twice. Parallel work shortens waiting only when conditions allow. Where investigation, authorization, implementation, and taking effect depend on one another in sequence, they must still follow that order. Treating completion of the first node as proof that the last affected person is already protected misjudges both timing and coverage.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.10.2"}),e[15]||(e[15]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-11" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.11</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Action Ceilings and Trials to Obtain Information <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-11" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.11 Action Ceilings and Trials to Obtain Information {#section-12-11}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Actions undertaken to learn still affect others. A small trial, good evidence, or benevolent intentions cannot alone determine the action ceiling; authority, protection, and actual reversibility must hold together.</span></div><p>Evidence, reversibility, permissions, and protection together determine the action ceiling. When evidence is weak, risk high, action irreversible, or permissions insufficient, an appropriate output may be to observe first, narrow the action, improve the information, protect people affected, initiate an appeal, or take no action for now.</p><p>A trial to obtain information still carries responsibility, however small the action. It must specify the trial object, people affected, minimum scope, reversibility, stopping, data uses, and remedy. Those in low-power positions must not be used as material for model calibration. Participation must not be described as voluntary where there is no genuine ability to exit.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Combination of Conditions</th><th>Example of the Permitted Ceiling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weak evidence, low harm, reversibility, and clear authorization</td><td>Small-scale observation or exploratory action</td></tr><tr><td>Moderate evidence, manageable harm, and the prior state can be restored</td><td>Phased action with stringent stopping conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Strong evidence but insufficient permissions</td><td>Submit to the authorized subject and do not execute independently</td></tr><tr><td>Strong evidence, but the action is irreversible and protection requirements are unmet</td><td>Suspend and complete the procedures and protections</td></tr><tr><td>Urgent safety threat</td><td>Take only the minimum protective action under external emergency authorization, and review it as soon as possible</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.11"}),e[16]||(e[16]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-12" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.12</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Outcome Write-Back and Model Learning <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-12" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.12 Outcome Write-Back and Model Learning {#section-12-12}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Learning is not revising an old prediction to look as though the answer was known all along. Actual updating preserves the original judgment, actual result, deviation, and consequences, then explains what the next version changes and on what grounds.</span></div><p>Outcome write-back for foresight and choices can only add to the record. It does not overwrite existing records. What is written back includes the time the outcome was observed, the source, the actual outcome, which path it matches, calibration metrics, comparison with the simple baseline, unexpected effects, review at affected positions, model updates, downgraded assertions, retired variables or paths, and the next review.</p><p>Action outcomes must not be evaluated only against the main target. Also check side effects, cross-circle spillovers, cost distribution, appeals, people unable to exit, and long-term clocks. One success does not establish that a mechanism holds universally. One failure may be due to a deviation in execution. Use the registration and arrangements set down in advance to distinguish evidence about the mechanism from deviations in execution. Explanations about execution must not be used arbitrarily to rescue the model.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.12"}),e[17]||(e[17]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-13" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.13</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>A Complete Example of Foresight and Choice <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-13" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.13 A Complete Example of Foresight and Choice {#section-12-13}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The public-resignation event is a running example in the book, not a verified corporate investigation. This guide keeps it a teaching construction, connecting conditional projection, foresight registration, normative comparison, and execution eligibility step by step.</span></div><p>Continue with the public departure from a job (§10.11, §11.11). A conditional forecast could set the target as “whether the team’s voluntary turnover rate over the next three months exceeds the threshold set down at registration.” The threshold is set down at registration with reference to the historical baseline and the baseline for similar teams, for example one and a half times the higher of the two; the definition of the turnover rate, the data source, and who compiles the figures are stated along with it, as is the margin between the turnover rate and the threshold within which the result is recorded as “unresolved.” The input cutoff is one week after the public statement. The simple baseline uses historical departure rates and trends in working hours. The multi-circle model adds team trust, management’s response, bridging by the professional community, family care, and platform exposure. Possible paths include public attention fading while internal problems persist, institutional adjustments followed by gradual repair, and external bridging that triggers departures in succession.</p><p>Early signals include actual changes in working hours, handling of appeals, statements by members, hiring, and outside opportunities. Contrary signals include a policy being announced without execution, or public quiet accompanied by increasing silence internally. After three months, compare the multi-circle model with the simple baseline. Do not declare success by picking an intermediate signal that happened to match.</p><p>Bounded choice should compare at least maintaining the status quo, responding only through public relations, immediately adjusting resources, starting with a reversible pilot for working hours and appeals, delaying major reorganization to gather information, allowing team members to transfer, and taking no structural action for now while strengthening protection and observation. Each option requires checking permissions, people unable to exit, the distribution of costs, reputational effects across circles and effects on families, stopping, and remedy. The model can show which options are more likely to change the path under which conditions. The final choice still depends on normative premises, lawful authorization, and procedures for the people affected. Under these conditions, the model should also state its current analytical recommendation, why other options were not selected, the costs, and the conditions for revising the judgment. A recommendation does not change the status of execution permission that has yet to be obtained.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.13"}),e[18]||(e[18]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-14" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.14</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>When to Choose Not to Act <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-14" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.14 When to Choose Not to Act {#section-12-14}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Not adding a main action does not reduce responsibility to zero. Ask both whether proceeding crosses boundaries and who continues suffering if action stops here. The answer may defer the main action while retaining independently grounded protection and observation.</span></div><p>Taking no action can be prudent or evasive. Taking no action for now or limiting activity to protective observation can be warranted when the object and facts are unclear, an action is irreversible and evidence insufficient, permissions are missing, or the protection floor is unmet. It can also be warranted when the proposed action is expected to shift costs to people unable to exit, neither the simple baseline nor the complex model can distinguish the paths, or external intervention could interrupt an ongoing recovery.</p><p>Taking no action requires stricter review when harm is ongoing, inaction reinforces entrenched advantages, the responsible subject has a clear statutory duty or commitment, or people in low-power positions cannot exit on their own. The same applies when delay would create irreversible lock-in or when “waiting for more evidence” is used to defer indefinitely. Even without the main action, minimum protection, disclosure, appeal channels, or temporary uptake may be needed.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.14"}),e[19]||(e[19]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-15" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.15</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Where This Part Stops <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-15" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.15 Where This Part Stops {#section-12-15}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">This part stops not at “nothing is known” but at a responsible output ceiling: registered conditional foresight, fully reasoned analytical recommendations, and choice records separated from actual-adoption eligibility.</span></div><p>The conditional foresight layer may publish judgments with a target, horizon, baseline, signals, calibration, and write-back. When norms, protection, and authorization are all clear, the bounded choice layer can compare action, delay, exploration, exit, and no action, and provide a conditional, withdrawable choice record. Even when execution has not been authorized, an analytical recommendation can still be completed within what the evidence and explicit normative premises allow. It must fully explain the options, opposing arguments, distributional consequences, and conditions for revising the judgment. Its status for real-world adoption must be stated separately.</p><p>These layers cannot guarantee the future, eliminate conflicts of value, replace law, professional norms, participation by people affected, or real-world authorization, or turn model accuracy into a qualification to rule. Any use that exceeds these boundaries should be blocked by the gates of Part 13 and handed over to Part 16 for governance, suspension, or retirement.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.15"}),e[20]||(e[20]=a('<div class="theory-source"><h2 id="section-12-16" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">12.16</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Format for Publication to Readers <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-12-16" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.16 Format for Publication to Readers {#section-12-16}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">Publication does not compress a complete analysis into a pleasing conclusion. It makes the current judgment, every substantive comparison already performed, and conditions limiting its use clear to readers. The opening may be direct; the following account must remain complete.</span></div><p>When publishing externally, deliver the full analysis by default. Open with the current judgment and its scope, then develop the conditions, evidence, and uncertainty in full. The main text should retain the items that apply to the current kind of output, including the current cutoff, target and horizon, scope of objects and circles, simple baseline, all substantive path comparisons already performed, the conditions for each path to hold, early and contrary signals, strength of expression, suspension, and the next review. Substantive analysis must not be omitted on the grounds that it does not change the main conclusion, belongs to a secondary branch, or is not a decisive reason. Conditions must not be hidden in footnotes, and “the model thinks” must not stand in for evidential responsibility. Shorten the visible answer only when the user explicitly asks for a brief reply or specifies a shorter length, while retaining the full analysis. If the analysis exceeds the capacity of one delivery, deliver it section by section and provide the complete file, stating what has yet to be delivered. Summaries, links to attachments, and coverage markers cannot substitute for the argument in the main text.</p><p>An analysis of bounded choice should state explicitly that it has entered the normative layer. It should list the value premises, deciding subject, affected positions identified and the limits of identification, protection floor, available options, distributional consequences of action and no action, authorization status, action ceiling, stopping, restoration, and remedy in full. The current analytical recommendation may come first, followed by the complete reasons for accepting and ruling out each option, costs, objections, and conditions for revising the judgment. If authorization has yet to be obtained, state clearly that the recommendation is for deliberation and that execution in reality has yet to be authorized. An imperative must not be used to make a recommendation look like a decision that can be executed. Lack of execution permission must not reduce an analytical choice with adequate grounds to a list of questions.</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Publication Status</th><th>Permitted Wording</th><th>What Must Be Avoided</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Explanation only</td><td>“Current evidence supports mechanism A and leaves B open.”</td><td>“Therefore, the next step must occur.”</td></tr><tr><td>Projection only</td><td>“If X continues and Y occurs, path P receives support.”</td><td>“The model’s prediction of P is settled.”</td></tr><tr><td>Registered forecast</td><td>“Within horizon T, P currently takes priority over Q. See contrary signal S.”</td><td>Hiding the baseline, horizon, or failure conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Normative checks not yet passed</td><td>“An analytical recommendation can be made under explicit premises. Real-world execution still requires normative premises, the protection floor, itemized authorization, and operational procedures.”</td><td>“Authorization has yet to be obtained, but execution can begin immediately.”</td></tr><tr><td>Externally authorized</td><td>“The authorization record permits the subject to execute within the ceiling and stop under the stated conditions.”</td><td>Attributing authorization to the model</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Interpretation after publication is also subject to governance. If readers mistake conditional foresight for a certain prophecy, a candidate personality hypothesis for a fact, or an option comparison for an order, the publisher should correct the misunderstanding without exploiting it to increase the publication’s impact. Append any update as a new version and keep the original title, probabilities, horizon, and failure record traceable.</p></div>',1)),i(t,{section:"12.16"}),e[21]||(e[21]=a('<div class="theory-source"></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Circles may be parallel, nested, overlapping, or bridged. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3))])}const v=o(d,[["render",u]]);export{y as __pageData,v as default};
